        <p>CAUTION.—BEWARE OF PIRACY AND IMITA- TIONS. CauTrox.—Vice-Chanoellor Sir W. PAGE WOOD stated that Br. J. Comas BROWNE was, undoubtedly, the inventor of " OHLORODYNB " and, that the story of the defendant FREEMAN. was deliberately untrue, which he regretted to Bay had been sworn to.—See Times, 13th July, 1864. Sold in bottles at Is. Id., 25. 93., and 45. 6d. None is genuine without the words 'DII. J. COLLIS BROWNIS'S CHLOBOOYLN "on the Government Stamp. Overwhelm- ing Medi'3,l testimony accompanies each bottle. SOLE MANUFACTURERS T. DAVENPORT, 33, Great Russell-street, Bloomsbury. Lonion</p>
        <p>_ From Dr. B. J. BOULTor AND Co., Horncastle. We have made pretty extensive use of Chlorodyne inour practice lately and look upon it as an excellent direct, Seda- tive and Anti•spasmodio. It seems to allay pain and irrita- tioa in whatever organ, and from whatever cause. It induces a feeling cf comfort and quietude not obtainable by any other remedy, and it seems to possess this great advantage over I all other sedatives ; that it leaves no unpleasant after effects. " Earl Russell communicated to the College of Physicians that he received a despatch from her Majesty's Consul at manina to the effect that Cholera had been raging fearfully, and that the ONLY remedy of any service was OHLotiO- DYNli."—See Lancet, Ist December, 1864,</p>
