        <head>'PLE A SANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>'PLE A SANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Gas sometimes escapes, but the consumer never does. The elocutionist teaches us to speak; but, un- fortunatey, not when to speak. The penalties of fame are not sufficiently severe to deter many aspirants. "Do you take exercise after your bath in the morning?" "Yes. I generally step on the soap as I get out." Tut AWAKENING or Lovit.—Mabel "When did you begin to care for him?" May: "I think it was when Jennie Heartbreak set her cap for him ? " A WINTER PARADOX.—Jack : "Miss Stateleigh was skating last night." Tom: "Was she as cold as ever ? " Jack: "Well, not quite so cold as she Was last summer." If a man is as old as he feels, a woman should be as old as she thinks she looks. The best conversationalist is the one who can listen the longest without betraying his annoyance. A REMEDY.—Jones : Brown is always complain- ing about the cares and responsibilities his money has brought him." Smith "Why doesn't he try speculation?" MATTER FOR CONGRATI7LATION.—HeIen : "Anna is so happy, now that she is to marry the man of her choice." " Bessie : "Yes—even though he was not her first choice."</p>
        <p>A new York newspaper, in speaking of the criminal classes, says the really dangerous charac- ters are the "burglars, murderers, and scorching bicyclers." "DON'T look so glum, Pilkerton ; use cheery words--they cost-nothing." "Cost nothing? If I speak ten cheery words to my wife she asks me for some money." EVEN the triumphant prophet who is able to say "I told you so" meets a great many exasperating people who can't remember that he did. SHE (at the play)—" There's a blunder! Six months are supposed to elapse between the first and second act." He—" Well ?" She—" They have the same cook." NEWLYWED (proudly)—"l always make it a point to tell my wife everything that happens." Old Sport—" Pooh! That's nothing. I tell my wife lots of things that never happen at all." HE—" It's hard to be rejected all round. I spoke to your father first, and he swore I could never marry you." She—" Then why didn't you say so! 'I am beginning to love you already." lIENPEcH—" I got one of the most peaceful homes you ever saw now. I've hit on a great scheme.' Dumley—" What is it?" Henpeck—" I make my wife so mad she won't speak to me." FOR IssiANcE.—Mrs. Homebird "It's dread-</p>
        <p>ful to be disappointed in love." Mr. Homebird: "There is something a great deal worse than that." "What, for instance?" "To be disappointed in marriage." AT the North Pole tvenings are six months long. This makes it possible for a couple of chess players to finish a game before sunrise next</p>
        <p>morning. SUB-EDITOR: "Here is an article favouring a simplified spelling of the English language." Editor: "Who wrote it?" "A man named Smythe." MORE IMPORTANT. —Mr. Mimms : "George, are you sure you locked up the house, carefully?" Mimms : "By Jove, I mn't remember about the front door." Mrs. Mimms : "Never mind the front door. How about the coal cellar?" GENTLEMAN (to house agent) : "The great dia. advantage is that the house is so damp.' House . . . _ .</p>
        <p>Agent :1' Disadvantage, sir? Advant'age, I call it. In case of fire it wouldn't be so likely to burn."</p>
        <p>MR. GUSIILEY : "I dweamed lawst night—aw-- that you and I were mawwied—aw—Miss Amy." Miss Snubbins : "You call that a dream, do you?" Mr. Gushly : "Yaws, of eawse." Miss Snubbins : "If I had dreamed that I should call it a night- mare." BUTCHER: "What can I sell you to-day?" Servant Girl: "I've come for some roast beef and let there be plenty of bones." Butcher: "Be plenty of bones! How's that?" Servant: "You see, the money I gets for the bones is my parkisit." TEACHER : "How many zones are there ?" Boy: "Six." "No, there are only five." "Yes, there are six." "Name them." "The torrid zone, the northern and southern temperate, the northern and southern frigid—" "That's five; what is the other zone ? " " 0-zone !"</p>
        <p>A FELLow, boasting in company of his family, declared even his own father died in an exalted situation. Some of the company looking incredu- lous, another observed: "I can bear testimony to the gentleman's veracity, as my father was sheriff for the county when his was hanged for horse- stealing." Jisxs: "I am always embarrassed when I want to say the word vase. I don't know whether to say " vaze, vace, vahz, or vawse.'" Blinks: "You might take a hint from our servant Mary. She simply speaks of all ornaments as them there.'"</p>
        <p>Fr seems that a lawyer is something of a car- penter. He can file a bill, split a hair, chop logic, dovetail an argument, make an entry, get up a case, frame an indictment, empanel a jury, put them in a box, bore a court, chisel a client, and other like things. "Wan knows not, and knows not that he knows not, is a fool—shun him," says a well-known Arabian proverb. "Who knows not, and knows that he knows not, is humble—teach him. Who knows, bat knows not that he knows, is asleep— wake him. Who knows, and knows that he knows, is wise—follow him.</p>
        <p>KNOWS WHAT IT is.—" Oh, Henry," exclaimed his wife, as she threw her arms rapturously about his neck, "I do love you so ! Don't forget to leave me twenty youndi when you go to town this morning, will you, dear?" "And this," muttered Henry, softly disengaging himself from her fond embrace, "this is what you might call being hard pressed for money." PRIEST: "Look here, Patsy Shea, if you're a Sensible man, you'll never let a drop of whiskey pass your lips again. Sure, 'Lis your worst enemy." _Patsy: " Arrah, now, your rivirince, and haven't you tould us from the pulpit that we wor to love our enemies?" Priest : " True for you, Patsy, but I never told you that you were to swallow them."</p>
        <p>NICEFELLOW (to adored one's little brother): "There! You did that errand very nicely. Here's a penny for you. Little brother; "Oh, ma! Mr. Nicefellow gave me a penny." Ma: Well, my .dear, you should say—" Little brother: "Yes, I know I should say Thank you,' but I was so s'prised I forgot. You said he hadn't got a penny in the world.' A SOURCE OF WEALTEL—City Man: I should not think that undertaking .would be a very profit- able business in a village as small as this one?" Professor Tombs (the Ruralville underaker): "Well. -it doesn't amount to so very much in winter, but in summeri make a pretty good thing out of the mistakes of city people who can't tell the difference hetween mushrooms and toadstools."</p>
        <p>" Foa 10 years," said the new boarder at a boarding-house, "my habits were as regular as clock-work. I rose on the stroke of 6; half an hour later I eat down to breakfast; at 7 I was at work; dined at 12, ate sapper at 6, and I was in bed at 9.20 ; ate only healthy food, and hadn't a sick day in all that time. " Dear me," said the deacon, in sympathetic tones, "and what were you iin for?"</p>
        <p>THE Bo Y (applying for a situation): "You want an office boy, sir ? " The Man: "I do. I want a boy who neither plays football in the winter nor rides a bicycle in the summer." The Boy later a deep thank): "Say, mister, why don't yer commit suicide?" The Man: " lou impertinent young scoundrel ! What do you mean?" The Boy: "'Cos there ain't none o' them sort o' boys in this world,"</p>
        <p>" BY all the gods of ancient Greece, I tell thee my " Julius illosar spoke harshly. "I don't see why not," said Julius Caesar. "Pompey lets his wife and she rides the bicycle in them." . eof Mrs. Pompey. Cxsar's wife ilsticweanortthtoem m, must 10e-shove suspicion." " I wish you'd married the Latin preceptress," said Mrs. •Cresar, hunting for her handkerchief. "X ea.n't even take a pep. perniint lozenge without, your raising your eye- brows." .And Cxsar flung.out of the house, clap. ping his laurel on his brow in a rage, slammed the front door, and scorched oat of sight cl4wn the Apian Way.</p>
      </div>
