      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>7 LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>7 LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>My DRAB There is not the slightest doubt, that the formal lines of hair waving are not considered very good style. The hair is waved in order to lighten it, so that by a touch of the hand or the use of combs it can be made to stand out at the side or at the back. Many of the beauties of the day still keep to the somewhat bushy fringe, and only a few part the hair down the centre. Turned-back fringes are the order of the day, with a few soft curls on the forehead elongating into a deep point in front. The arrangement of this pointed curl in front needs some little skill, but any number of pretty women in London are adopting this style. For the even-</p>
        <p>ing it is mily hair is dressed low in England at present, but this the style of the future, and many of the smart women who herald the modes have been wearing it low in the neck as though in a net, and ere long jewelled nets will be coming back again, but at present one or two upstanding loops support the diamond orna- ments or light aigrettes, which are universally worn. In the country houses people dress the hair in high knots for the evening, and reverse them, and bring them down low for the day beneath the hats. A simple instruction to those who wish to dress their own hair is to gather the tresses up on the crown, and tie; then comb the front hair back, and secure it as it should go with invisible hairpins and an invisible net.</p>
        <p>Most of the new gowns show a tendency towards paniers indicated by trimming of a flat nature, and mostly accompanied by boleros of the same descrip- tion as the paniers. As often as not they are of guipure or gimp, lace, or some filmy substance, lined with silk, satin, or velvet, and often elaborately embroidered in pearls, crystals, and cut stones. These little graceful additions are also made to adapt themselves to many gowns, especially in thin materials, heavily embroidered, and a great feature on most of them are elaborate buttons. The loose bodices worn under them are, as a rule, lace over a colour. The boleros are more substantial than the lace capes, which transform any bodice and admit of varying chiffon linings, the colours over which they are worn making all the difference.</p>
        <p>A costume for early autumn wear is of reddish mauve cashmere, trimmed cream Bengaline, covered with fine tinted lace. A border of inser- tion lace is edged with narrow gimp, or Übe ribbon can be used in the same fashion, and the sash is of cream Bilk ribbon.</p>
        <p>Figured woollens, pretty as they were with silky designs or shot effects, do not seem like returning to favour this autumn, and plain serge cloth, or the faced costume cloth which has been used for coat costumes for some time now, betrays no sign of waning popularity. The mirror-like surface is no longer sought on this cloth, as, however good in dye and quality, it acquired spots too readily. and even the art of the chemical cleaner has tailed to restore its pristine beauty. There is already a note of autumnal tints both in gowns and millinery, and velvet is replacing lace and chiffon for trimming purposes. Cloth is really seen at its best when relieved with a darker contrasting velvet, and this is now used, cub directly on the bias for very Larrow frills, or laid fiat for lengthway stripes, these sometimes con. tinning round the skirt, and simulating the heading of a shaped flounce, or suggesting a tablier skirt. Now that sleeves are worn (says The Lady) so very small and close, the single-breasted, tight. fitting coat has a very characteristic style, and is eminently becoming to the fairly good figure. The tailor makes this type of coat fit like a glove, and the turn of the shoulder and the waist are "shrunk out" with the iron, and width is usually added to the bust by a little padding under the arms. The sleeves are made with only a soupcon of fulness at the top, and this is held out by a little roll of wadding, carefully disposed so as to retain its shape, however pressed. A charm- ing little coat is made in this tailor style, and very tightly fitted. There are only four pieces in the back and two fronts, and the lining should be cub exactly the same as the cloth, and made up in four parts, filling these in at the shoulder and underarm seams. The braid is the quarter-inch tubular kind, which is easy to put on, and can be pressed nice and flat from the outside if a folded cloth is placed between the iron and the braid. The back of the basque fits closely, but has na pleats. The braid is arranged down the curved back seams in the same fashion as shown on the fronts. The small lapel is cut in one with the coat, and the collar is made separately, and joins on to it. Particular attention must be paid to the pressing, and if very carefully cut one and three•quarter yards of fifty-four inch plain cloth will be sufficient. Your loving</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
