      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 9, 1898</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 9, 1898</p>
        <p>Despite her years, the Queen's voice hos lost none of the freshness and charm of bygone days, and her hear- ing continues absolutely unimpaired. 'roe same how- ever cannot be said of Her Majesty's sight, which has failed considerably of late. The Sovereign can only mar age to [(ad and write with difficulty, and in a large rsom she is frequently forced to 1130 opera glasses, t 3 see distant things. Several very charming new photographs have latsly been taken of the Queen. Some represent Her Majertv alone, others—the Queen surrounded by members of her family,and then there are some delightful groups,in which are included members of this household, and numerous pet dogs and ponies. Her Majesty's habit of driving at almost full speed, makes a successful snap-shot very nearly an impossi- bility. Now and again though the aspiring photoerapher meets with success, as was the case at Cimiez last spring. Strolling in the woods one day, this amateur heard tho noise of horses approaching. He hastened to an opening, saw the Queen's carriage, and prepared to take it. The exposures resulted in a capital reproduc- tion of Her Majesty, and little Princess Ena of Batten- berg, who was driving with the sovereign. Speaking of Royal pet dogs,when the Queen first came to the Throne, the King Charles, and the Blenheim held foremost rank. In those days, the lox-terrier was releg- ated to the companionship of the miner, the navvy. and ether men of like estate. The wonderful manner in which this most friendly and delightful dog has reached his apotheosis, must astonish himself. In fact he is some- times just a wee bit spoiled by his prosperity. It is still the smaller descriptions of dogs, in which fashionable women are most concerned. But probably none would to mere surprised than some of these indi- viduals, if they were informed that in thls particular they are as much in leading strings as their dog can be, and that the wires are pulled by persons who are interes- ted in creating an artificial value for certain breeds. Every ROW and then a new variety is introduced, and as soon as the animals have been put on the market, so to speak, and introduced by newspaper paragraph, and at all the foremost dog shows, the fun begins. The prices of these novelties are very high, and money flows into the pockets of those whose plans have been successful. In this way the Schipperke, a most uninteresting animal, found himself introduced into boudoirs, providsd with a luxurious basket-bed, silk cushions, and the softest of blankets, and fed every day with every dainty that could appeal to the canine palate. The Schiprerke is by no means of an affectionate disposition, and is therefore uncapable of making the only return possible for all this love and devotion. Cupboard love is about all it can manage, except in instances so rare as to be quite exceptional. It is row said that a slight limp will assuredly be the result of he Prince of Wales's acciSent. His Royal High- ness is foilg aware of this unwelcome fact, but has met the trouble in his usual happy, uncomplaining way. As to whether lirrping will again become a fashienable fad, time will show. When the Princses of Wales was affiie- ted with lameness in one of her knees some twesty-five ysars ago, Society women considered it the proper thi.4g to limp too. Needless to say, the effect was otten ludi- crous in the extreme, and the Princess's extreme dis- p'essure at this mode of procedure can starcely tes mar- . yelled it. The question as to how the little son of Prince and Princess Adolphus of Teck is to be styled.is again reedy. log attention. As every one knows, the Princess is a daughter of the Duke or Westminster, and, according t) German law Prince Adolphus himself, being the descen- dant of two morganatic alliances, has no right to the title of prince. With regard to our law, this only pro- vides for the style of precedence of the descendants of a reigning sovereign down to grandchildren. If the matter goes by pure law, ' Prince George of Teck "ashe is called, will to merely " Mister," and then considerable difficulty will arise in fixing his exact surname. The Quesn,however, has recogned the Prince as a Prines,and his wife as a Princess, and she may do the same to their small son. _</p>
        <p>---Gio:g-1-;na, Lady Dudley, who has been plunged into moorning by the detth of her mother, Lady Louisa Mon. crieffe,belongs to a family n- tad for their hesuty. She has teen a great leader of society, and possmed at one 'time almost the finest collection of jewels in England— a parure of tabphires and diamonds being exceptionally remarkable. It is said that they cost something like £60,000. Some of my readers will probtbly rem-mbar the excitement which was caused by the robbery of Lady Dudley's jewels at Paddington station some Tears ago. Lady Dudley is tall, very handtome,and ha a re- markably tine carriage. Few days pass now, without the tidings of further climbing accidents that kayo proved fatal. Among the latest, come two from the Eugadine, which has not hithetto httalued any gruesome celebrity in this line If this fatal season leaves no otker lesson, it will surely teach the ab'olute necessity of trained,ancl expert guides in any dangerous ascent. It is never safe to dispense with a guide, and the more man knows of mountains, the more one appreciates the fact. A good guide knows them as no summer visitor, no matter how experienced he or the may be, ma' canotad it is to be regretted that so many have come to grief, b_cause they would not realise it.</p>
        <p>_ The alpenstock it seems. Is no longer patronised by fashionable women. A cane, with a thick, or butt end, stout, yet light, is deem:d the correct thing for moan- tain excursions, and country rambles. The Empress Eugenie introduced the alpenstock into France,by bring- ing to the Tuileries the one she used during her ascen- sions in the Alps, in 1860, thereby setting the fashion for its adoption by the French grand monde. For elderly women there are now no distinct modes. For day wear the coat and skirt costume does for all alike,in the evening chaperons and maidens appear side by side in white satin gowns,and the large all covering mantles for day wear have disappeared entirely. A dainty cape is the only outer covering the elderly woman now pat- ronises, with the exception of the long opera clock,built as fussily as a debutantes, Pv,vided though, all this is not carried too far, the move is distinctly, one in the right direction. That a woman because she has left youth and even miedle age behind, should he forced to go about exhibiting fashions in vogue somewhere about the days of Noah, is, to say the least, unfair. In bygone days, a woman on the shady side of forty was restrictel to the dowdiest colours available, and never was she permitted to get within a decade or so of the fashions of the moment. But, happily, things are different now, and the middle-aged and elderly woman may enjoy the varying modes, and take a pride in her appearance, two items which would have been deemed deplorable in her, when our grandmothers were you, g. The most original treatment of the red, white, and blue idea, I have yet Fenn, I came across at a leading, and well-I:warn modiste's the other day. This took the shape oi3 dark blue grenadine, embroidered with white spots, relieved by delicious touches of deep crimson, which was introduced in the way of a gathered yoke, and a sash with floating ends. The gown was about to be despatched to a lovely young matron, -whose beauty it promised well to enhance. Delicate women and children may sometimes derive great benefit from iron or steel baths. The iron is said to brace and stimulate the nerves of the skin. The , sulphate of iron which can he obtained from any chemist should be used in the proportion of a quarter of an ounce to two gallons of water, tepid or cold, as a morning sponge bit h. After using the bath the body t timid be well rubbed with a rotr.rh towel,and the bather sh^,uld dress quickly, and then lake some exercise. For those who suffer from chronic rheumatism, a mild alkaline bath spay be used, composed of two ounces of carbonate of soda, mixed in three gallons of tepid water. This bath is very cleansing and has a softening influence on the skin. Pereions with tender skins, who suffer from itetating rashes, especially during warm weather will often End relief in the rtarch bath. Put into a couple"( pailfuls of tep'd water, two ounces of powdered starch, which has been well mixed with boiling water. The Ekin should afterwards be dried with a soft towel, and while the irritltion con' inuee, warm, light clothing should be worn, and all excess in eating and drisking be avoided. DAMSON TRIFLE.—Stew one pould of dameons with half a pound of lump sugar, until the fruit is soft. Place in a glass dish, make a custard with the yolks of three eggs and a pint of milk, and pour over. Arrange some Savoy biscuits edgeways on the custard, whip the whites of the eggs to which add a teaspoonful of powdered sugar, to a stiff froth, pile on the top, sprinkle with chopped almonds, and serve. "MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>The heat in New York during the last few days has been teriffic, the street tnermornecers registering 107 in the shade. Snakes are, unusually numerous in Surrey this year. A few days ago one over three feet in length was killed on allot- ments at Chessington. Mr. John Storey. of Durham, recently Bold a St. Bernard deg, aged eighteen month. to Mr. C. M. Pearson, of North Kilworth, near Rugby, for £l9O. A pier of ahe Ottawa and New York Railway Bridge over the St. Lawrence river fell on Tuesday, carrying down two spans. Thirty workmen were killed and 11 injured. The body of an elderly man was found on Sunday after- noon in a field about two miles from Ruthin, shot throne: the head The case is supposed to be one of suicide. It is announced that the volunteer long-service medals will In future be manulactured at the Mint, and that they are to I be fastened to the tunic in the same way as any other medal A serious fire broke out at Messrs. J. B. White and Co.'s, Limited, Swanseombe Cement Works. onSunday night. Two I mills were destroyed, and extensive damage was done to the surroundings. I The death occurred at Leamington. on Sunday, of Lady Louisa Moncreiffe, daughter of Lord Kinnoull, and widow of the late Sir Thomas Manor[elle, of Perthshire. She was 73 , years of age. The full text of the will of the late Mr. W, R. Gladstone is published. The gross value of the personal estate is returned at £59,505, and the net value at £51.732. The will is in Mr. Gladstone's own handwriting. In a nest In Highgate Cemetery three strong young cuckoos are being reared oy a pair of hedge sparrows. Curiously enough the voice of the cuckoo has not been heard in the cemetery this season. During Friday night the jeweller's shop in Market-street, Blackpool, occupied by Mr. Payne, was broken into, and a quantity of jewellery was stolen. The thieves obtained entrance by making a hole in the roof. The Blackpool police received en Tuesday night confirma- tory news of the arrest of Hope Parkinson, the bank clerk who is charged with absconding with £1.00.) a fortnight ago. Hole in the custody of the Vienna police. A dPstructive fire broke out early on Monday morning in Long-lane, Bermondsey, London. Several large warehotses we e gutted. The area involved was some 60 yards by 21 The damage will amount to over £50.000.</p>
        <p>The Dublin Corporation on Monday elected James Egan to the oflics of City Sword Bearer. ligan was convicted of treason-felony fifteen years ago and sentenced to twenty years' penal servitude. He was released some time ago. The Montreal express, on the Delaware and Hudson Rail- way, dashed into a tralley car near Coho sat seven o'clock n Monday evening and was wiecked. Richteen persons were killed, and ten more cannot suryive their injuries.</p>
        <p>, At the Stsckport Borough Police Court on Tuesday John Edward Kelly was committed for trial at the Chester Assizes. charged with the manslaughter of David Bancreft by kicking him.in the course of an altercation at a Stockport beerhouse. Three persons who are slid to ht.° failed to conform to the ceremonial at St. Margaret. Church. Antielci. in respect to standing. sittinr. kneeling. &amp;c., were on Sundsv arrested. and their names were taken, presumably with a view to prosecution. -•</p>
        <p>General Sir William Hope, 'Bart., of Craig Pall, some time commander of the forces in North Britain, died on Monday at his residence, Pinkie House. Museltiiirg. aged 79. General Rope served with distinction in the Crimea and in the In- dian Mutiny.</p>
        <p>At an inquest held on Monday concerning the death of Mr Percy Bailey, who dieil from injuries sustained in a bicycle accident near (Ake Vyrnwy, it was stated that the real was exceedingly &amp;interns, and the jury suggested that warning notices should be put up. In the early hours of eirettirday morning a disastrous fire broke one, at the Workmen's Home in Albert-road, Southamp- ton, whereby one of the occupants a man named Dleben lost his life and several others had narrow escapes. The damage is estimated at about £lOOO. The City Coroner en Monday held an inquest on the body of James Bastin. 53, carter, late of Chapel-street. Oldham- road, who was struck by a locomotive whilst working on the railway at Miles Plattinz on Saturday and killed. The jury returned a ver Mot of arnidental death. The Prince of Wales is progressing very satisfactorily to. wards recovery. On Saturday. at Cowes. he was allowed to stand on both legs, for a short time. He walked very carefully for a short distance to exercise the injured limb, and found the change very beneficial. According toe' Remp's Mercantile Gazette," the number of fallnres in Fngland and Wales razetted during the week ending September 3rd, was 72. The number hi the corresponding week of last year was 77. showing a decrease of 5, b lug a net increase in 1898. to data. of 174. On Tuesday afternoon an explosion Id gas occurred in the Grime Pit, Rotherham. Fred Httley and Tom Booth, miners and John Henry Snowball and Fred Parkin. ' trimmers)," all of Gremberough, were severely burned. They managed to reach the shaft, and were removed to hospital. The enited States Consul at Dawson City, in a letter dated August 4. reports that many unfor'un•te prospectors who are rumble to get away are stared in the face by imminent destitution and ruffering. The current reeorts as to the output of gold, he says, exaggerate the emeunt fivefold. A great fire broke oat at half-past eight, on Friday night in the works of MM. Gartaide.and Co chintz manufacturers. at Malaunay near Rouen. The damage is estimated at 2,000,000 franca. It is covered by insurance No one was Injured. Six hundred men have been thrown out of employment. On Saturday the supply of water be the East London Waterwerks Company was further redncee from six hours to four per day. Public indignation ev,s so great that it wail feared the turneocks would be interfered with in the die- ' cheree nf their duty, and policemen had to be paced on point duty to prevent any disturbance 1 News reached Beamnaris on Saturday morning that Hr. Perev Bailey. the third son of Sir William Dailey. of ealford, 'who has a residence at Beenmeris, whilst riding his bicreie from Bangor to Lake Vyrnwv met with an accident. After remaining uneonscious for about an hour, the unfortunate young gentleman succumbed to his injuries. A Faversham correspopdent alluding to the explosion on Friday at the Faversham Cotten Powder Conapanv's states that there is a possibility of par ially saving the sight of the woman Beod'e, and one eye of the girl Rickard. but the woman Martin and Seeger are totally blind, and the con- , ditlon of all four is described as precarious. A foreign seaman had a narrow escape on the Mersey on Saturday afternoon. While making his way in a boat from his yeesel lying in mid-river at the Garet= Dock. he was Carried away from his (worse by the strength of the tide and waves. The boat was being dashed with c mslderable violence against Cresseington Park wall, when the man's cries brought assistance, and he was rescued by means of a rope. Nathaniel Dawsen. aged fourteen, son of Mr. William Daw- son, Otley, succumbed on Friday to erysipelas arising from the sting of a wasp, which was inflicted about nine days ago. He with a boy named Phillips, was returning from Ilkey on Tuesday week, when the latter discovered a waspa nest. Dawson was severeiy stueg abut the right eye, and died as stated. _ _</p>
        <p>At Sevastopol preparations are belny made for a grand review of the whole Russian Black Sea fleet, which is to take Wane next Week, iss presence of the Emperor and Empresa. The Destiny squadron, under the command of Vica-Admiral Alexelew, will be reinforced by two ironclad!. one first-class cruiser, one torpedo cruiser, two gunboats, and some other small craft. _</p>
        <p>On Saturday night, the police broke open the door of a cot- tage at Dinhlass. Knighton. where they found an old rn an named Thomas Edwards aged 80 years, in a kneeling posi- tion, beside the bed. He was quite dead. A sum of money— said to be £3oo—was found beneath the bed clothes. It is stated that the deceased had br4asted that he could live on olio shilling a week. About 11.33 on Saturday morning, whilst a party of work- men were engaged in repairing the permanent way at Far- rlngdon-street Railway Station. London, one of the men. failing to hear the signal to stani clear oF an arn.roaching train, ‘eas knocked down and instantly killed, his body be- ing cut in half. The accident took place in the sight of a large number of passengers. A despatch from the city of Mexico states that a train of mules, laden with silver bullion, was attacked by brigands near Zan tteeas on Fr;day. The men in charge made but a feeble reslstanee, and were soon overpowered. The brigands got clear away with th ir valuable booty, but the soldiers are now In pursuit of them, and it Is hoped that the entire gang of them will be captured. A serious case of highway robbery and attempted murder is reported to have occurred at Silvertown An electrician named Forbes VFnite. age 22. was attacked by three men and robbed. about eleven o'clock at night. On his attempting to follow they shot at him, the bullet entering his right thigh. lie crawled to a friends house, and fainted from loss of blood. There Is no trace of the culprits. At Newport. Isle of Wight. on Saturday. Maurice Hol- brook was charged on his own confession with the murder of a boy named Peres, Rayter, In Parkhurst Barracks field. The accused, when he gave himself up, stated that he was very weak, having had nothing to eat but a pennyworth of buns for three days, Re did not, he said, know what made him commit the crime but this. He was committed for trial.</p>
        <p>The Birmingham pollee made a startling discovery on Tues- day. They went to a house in Dudley-road to arrest William Woodhead . on a charge of stealing coffin furniture, A certain °deur induced the Police to make a ea-eful search. when the holies of four children were found It Is sapposed that Woodhead had been entrusted with the bodies, and had neglected to bury them, lie was brought up at the Pollee Court Bud remanded. The young Queen of Holland was on Tuesday publicly in- vested in the Nieuwe Kerk at Amsterdam amid great rejoic- ings. Prior to taking the oath the Queen delivered a speech expressing her hanolness at being .• able to govern the Netherlands people," On returning to the Palace Her Majesty was recolved with tremendous acclamation. "Never in the memory of living Dutchman," man has the calm temperament of the says Renter's correspondent. "given place in such degree to unrestrained enthusiasm and excitement." A shocking fatality occurred In Nelson Railway Station late on !-aturday night. Bryan Smith, aged 47, wa% seen by R man named Fred Johnatone. of Burnley, riding on the footboard of a carriage. The train was then going at a good speed. and Johnstone watched to see how deceased would get off. --e rni"e'l the colleolor shortly afteesYards, and, hearing a gtoaning noise, he saw the deceased lying between the rails and the platform. lie died during the journey to the hospital. Both arms and several ribs of deceased were fractured, and he had also sustained Internal injuries. He leaves a widow and one child, Three persons, who belonged to fishing party have lost their lives at Monasterevan, Co Kildare, threugh the Capita' . ce ing of their craft. The persons are Sergeant Young. who had charge of the local police station ; his °°l, aged eight and a poli pensioner, ; named James Noonan. They were fishing on the Barrow on Saturday evening in a flat-bottomed boat, prop,lled by a paddle when through an awkward stroke on the part of the sergeant the craft was overturned. Two of the party managed to swim ashore. but the other three, who could not swim, were drowned. Mr- Smelt. coroner, held an inquest In Manchester on Monday, on the body of Robert Clarke. 17. the son of a labourer who II”es In Taylor-street, Openshaw. On Saturday afternoon the deceased was standing near some hobby horses which were in motion on the Onenshaw Wakes Ground. Michael Burke (who was, in the opinion of the jury. under the infinenee of drink at the time) in attemptiag to get off lone of the horses fell upon the boy, who was picked up in an uneonscions condition. He died in the Ancoats Hospital on Sunday. A verdict of " Accidental death was returned.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held on Saturday at Gweedore, near Letterkenny, Ireland touching the death of Connell Boyle, farmer and cattle dealer. The evidence indicated that Boyle was attacked end brutally beaten to death, and the empty condition and blooistained lining of an inside money ponket of the decease i's coat showed that the mi-rderer's object was robbery. There were six terrible wounds on the head, arty one of which was sufficient to cause death. The jury re- turned a verdict of wilful murder azatnst, some person or persons unknown, The police are hoperul ot arrestind the murderer, as they have a valuable clue in their possession,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>(C%Vrig ht)</head>
        <p>(C%Vrig ht)</p>
        <p>A WARD OF THE KING.</p>
        <p>By K.ATHARINE S. INIACQI7OID, Author of "Maisie Derrick," "The Story of Lois,* "Patty," "Louisa,' "At the Red Glove," "Miss Eyon of Eyoncourt," dr,c.</p>
        <p>CEIAPTER XXI. CHARLES DE BOURBON.</p>
        <p>Jeanne had asked to see the hostess, but she learnt that Madame Tarbes was too busy to wait on her. After the evening meal she bade Mathurine brush out her long, shining hair. The girl could not see well to do this, for the chamber was lit only by a single sconce fixed to the wall, in which two tallow candles were burning. Mathurine seemed unusually slow and awkward this evening, and Jeanne said so. "Madame will pardon me," the tirewoman blundered out; "I am frightened; this place is so lonely' and the hostess looks angered. Madame heard the Count and Gaspard ride away to the village, and they have not returned. ladeed, indeed, madame, we are strangely lonesome;' there is not even a cottage near us, and not a man in the place."</p>
        <p>"Where is Anne? Is she also frightened ? " "Madame, Anne is worse than I am; she sits below in the eating-room, weeping for very terror. She says we shall be robbed and murdered before morning; when the sour-faced hostess saw her tears she rated her, and bade her not be a fool." "The hostess is right, my girl," Jeanne said kindly. "Tears are often foolishly shed; they ahould not be wasted, but kept for our own short- mmings. Yet, it is lawful to weep for the loss of those we love."</p>
        <p>_ Mathurine looked significant; she wished her mistress would question her about Anne, for she believed the girl was weeping for lack of Gaspard's company; but the young Countess was not in- quisitive, and she was now possessed by an idea which she saw a way of carrying into action. "Knot up my hair and replace my coif. I will not disrobe till I have spoken with the hostess ;she must be at leisure by now." When this was done Jeanne threw aside the coverings which Mathurine had spread over her mistress's shoulders while she combed out the tong auburn tresses. "Bid Madame Tarbes come up and speak with you can go and sit with Anne. Two arecheerful company than one," she ended,m more meantime, smiling, for Mathurine looked miserable. Jeanne sighed at the truth of her own words. How seldom she had been alone in her short life; and yet she supposed that wives were often left alone when their lords joined in the wars which seemed to be for ever taking place. She had known few women, and, with the exception of Gillonne and her mother, she had a slight contempt for her sex. Women seemed to her either vain or weak. If her husband had lived he would, she supposed, have been her wise friend and adviser, but she would have preferred s friend who was not her husband; and then a deeper sigh came at the remembrance of Rolland's desertion. "But I must not think of myself; I have to save my lord of Bourbon." This would be the third interview she had had with hostesses, and the girl determined that t should not be wasted in mere words. She believed that to-night opportunity was given her, and to-night she would deliver her warning. The hostess came in. The tall, bony woman looked more like a man; there was distrust in her eyes as she stared at the lady, without, any of the timid shrinking which Jeanne's last hostess had displayed, "Madame," the girl said firmly, "there is no time to lose. My woman Mathurine is, I believe, safe with her fellow in the eating-room?" "Yes, madame." The woman looked cold and suspicious. Well, then, listen. You have a friend of mine here in the house, whom it behoves me to see without delay. I have a message to give him from Therese Vougeon." The woman's hard face became still more rigid; then she said between her set teeth: "Pardon me, madame, but by what right do you use my daughter's name about what does not concern you or yours?" Jeanne smiled at the uncourteous tone.</p>
        <p>"Your daughter sought me, and asked my help i for a friend n trouble, in hiding, she said. She did not know that I too loved him, and that the Duke was my father's dear friend." The tall woman pressed her hands together in her agony of doubt. "I cannot believe, madame must pardon me, but it seems as though she were trying to put words in my mouth—words that may doom not me only, but others, to prison and tb death. Madame is, is she not, the wife of the Count of Laval ?"</p>
        <p>_ "lam the Count of Laval's widow, but no wife. Who has betrayed me to you?" she asked sternly. The woman shook her head.</p>
        <p>"A warning came to me; it matters not whence I had it; it said that the Count of Laval "—she had lowered her voice to a whisper—"is hunting the country for the sake of earning the price of blood, and that his Countess and her women travel in his company. I have taken you in to- night for the sake of Therese, and because your face bade me trust; but I have had no peace since, and I pray you to depart with the first streaks of morning light." "No, no, no; on the contrary, you will now wait on your Royal guest, and crave his leave to admit ins to his presence. You will say his little cousin Jeanne.liergrist craves to see him."</p>
        <p>The woman's face changed at once. "Mom Dieu, madame," she said,. "time would have been spared had you said that name sooner!" She quitted the chamber as she spoke, and pass- ing up the uneven staircase, she opened the door at the top of it. She crossed the small chamber into which it opened, bare but for one straw pallet in the corner near the door. She drew back a curtain on the wall, and tapped at a shuttered grating behind it. The shutter beyond the grating was then unbarred and removed; a voice said: "Who goes there ?" and the woman answered: "The D.ukedOm. Then behind the grating appeared the grave, though pleasant, face of the angler who had spoken with Laval beside the tarn near Le Pay. "Well, Madame Tarbes, what news do you bring us--,or is it an alarm ? "</p>
        <p>.1 Madame Tarbes delivered Jeanne's message, and the face disappeared from the grating. t` A deep, strong voice said: "Yes, I will see her, for her father's sake; his daughter could not betray me."</p>
        <p>Then came some murmured words. When they ended, the angler reappeared at the grating. "This lady arrived with a troop of soldiers, did she not? "</p>
        <p>"Yes, monsieur; but they have gone further to seek a lodging. I would not have admitted the lady, but she said she had a token regarding his Highness from my Therese." "Well, then, my lord will see her; bring her to the door, and I will open it." Jeanne felt strangely excited as she followed Madame Tarbes up the foul-smelling staircase; she felt shy too, for she had been a little child when she last saw the Duke de Bourbon. But her natural simplicity came to her help. She remembered her promise to Therese Vougeon ; she remembered, too, the old motto carved on the stone chimney-front of her father's chamber at Longuedec—a motto which he told her was highly honoured in England, that strange islan&amp; beyond the sea. Jeanne did not believe its inhabitants could be, as Gillonne said they were, barbarous, if they professed such senti- ments as those of the motto with which she encouraged herself as she followed her guide. g Fais ce gee dois, advienne que pourra.' It is niv duty, and I will do it," she told herself. 'Madame Tarbes' knock was answered by the unlocking and opening of the door. "May it please you to enter, madame," the angler said ; and the hostess whispered that she would go down and keep the lady's women from taking note of their mistress's absence from her chamber.</p>
        <p>C.IIAPTER XXIL A WISII FULFILLED.</p>
        <p>Jeanne stood bewildered for an instant, while the door was closed and locked behind her; then raising her eyes, she saw the tall figure of the Duke, who had risen and was coming forward. She made a deep and reverential curtsey. It seemed to her that as he was a Prince of the Blood Royal she ought to kneel to him. But Bourbon soon ended her doubts; bending forward, he took her hands in his, and kissed her on either cheek. "Welcome, my fair kinswoman! The sight of you is a real refreshment to a weary traveller whose misfortunes have banished hina from the sight cf lovely faces." Betore the blushing girl could speak be had</p>
        <p>laced her fn a chair, and presented to her his friend. The sight of the Duke, a long-cherished wish dating from early childhood, had excited Jeanne,' and made her for a moment forget the great peril that menaced him; but the angler's matter-of-fact appearance and his words recalled her scattered wits. "Madame," he said curtly, "my lord is im- patient to know to what cause he is indebted for this visit ? " The Duke frowned; his extreme courtesy towards women made him think his friend's words abrupt. Jeanne's eyes sparkled with eagerness and affection as she gazed at the noble fate, on which the lines of remorse and carking care were already stamped. "My lord "—her sweet, 'timid voice added to, the fascination of her appearance—"may I ask if you think to sleep to-night in this hostel ? " "Yes, dear child; we had so planned," the Duke answered. Jeanne looked from his grand face, with its ivory- tinted skin and gleaming dark eyes, now fixed on her as if to read her thoughts, and then she glanced at the quiet, more commonplace counten- ance beside him. The quiet man looked far mom on the alert than Bourbon did; her question had brought keen anxiety into the eyes of De Pon,- perant. "Madame has a special reason for asking question. May I know what it is ?" he said. The Constable looked away : he had been about to question Jeanne on the subject of her mother's death; his friend's interruption appeared to him intrusive and uncalled for. "I have the gravest reason, monsieur," Jeanne said in a low voice to De Pomperant. "It touches the safety of his Highness; he is in danger while he stays in this house." "Your devotion, sweet child, makes you over- anxious," Bourbon interrupted. "The mistress of the inn is my foster-mother; she would as soon betray her own child." Jeanne felt alarmed at her, own daring. "Pardon me, your Highness," she said quickly; "the danger I warn you of comes not from within but from without; it comes from the Count of Laval, who brought me hither, and from his soldiers." The Constable looked at her with surprise. Foy de Prince 1 He is your husband, is he not? And yet I mind me that your message was from Jeanne Kergrist, daughter of iny old friend the most loyal heart that ever beat in France or elsewhere. You, dear child, must explain this riddle: how is it that I find Your father's daughter, if she is not a wife, travelling in the company of the Chevalier de Retz, or Count of Laval? Zrom de .Dieu! I know the man d and his deeds." Jeanne was greatly troubled, What could her affair, signify, she asked herself, when every moment was needed to put distance between the Duke his pursuers? But those brilliant eyes fixed so searchingly on hers compelled an answer. A happy inspiration came to her. She remem- bered Therese Vougeon. "My lord," she said with dignity—for she felt that the Duke judged her in ignorance—"l am the widow of the late Count of Laval, a good and noble man; at his death he bade his cousin and successor take me with him to Amboise, for I am a ward of the King." The Duke's frown alarmed her, but she went on: "Hence it is that I travel in his company. After we quitted Laval, news came that the King had gone south, and we followed him. It was thus that I came to Moulins, and spoke with TM/Ilse Vougeon at her inn, Le Cur Loyal. Till then I had trusted my guide, but she bade me beware of him, and be- sought me if I by chance met with your Highness, to give you instant warning against him. She said "—Jeanne looked anxiously from one face to the other—"that he was tracking you to give you up to the King." De Pomperant's hand grasped his sword hilt. "How came you, madame, to remain with the Count when you had received such a warning ? " The Duke spoke almost indignantly. "If you knew him as others do, lady, you would know that such u woman as you are is not safe in his hands; and if you consent to wed him child, he means but to rob you, and to break your heart." His tone changed to earnest pleading. "Do not listen to him, I beseech you; avoid him as you would a pestilence." Jeanne smiled, but she looked grateful. "I thank my ford for his kind thought for me. I would gladly free myself, but how can I? I am alone, save for my two women. Surely, my lord, he dares not harm me, the widow of his kinsman, and the King's ward ? " Charles de Bourbon shrugged his shoulders and turned away at the name of the King. De Pomperant, however, was impatient; he thought that Jeanne's affairs had been too long under discussion. "Then you believe this man Laval to be close at hand, madame? We heard he had gone further." "I have no certain knowledge, monseigneur, but I have a strong suspicion that unless he meant mist. chief he would not have ridden off to seek other lodgings; I believe that he and some of -his followers will return here as soon as darkness hides their approach. They will soon be here." Then clasping her hands imploringly: "Pardon my bold- ness, my lord, but do not, I pray you, linger; your precious life may hang on moments; believe me; you are not safe in France, unless, indeed "—she paused; then timidly—" but that is not for me to say." "Nothing could come amiss from those pretty lips," the Duke said gallantly, and as he smiled at her; the girl understood Therese Vougeon'a enthu- "siasm ; she felt that she could die, if need were, to save the Duke from capture. "What do you say, De Pomperant ? Shall we stay or go?" Instinctively his hand sought his sword hilt. Jeanne fell on her knees before him. "My lord, my lord, there is still a way by which you may baffle the Count and his schemes. Go to the camp at Lyons, and give the lie to these foul calumnies that villains have spread abroad." The Duke started, but he bent forward and raised her. "Too late, too late, my child; yet I thank you for your loved zeal. The King and I are two; were I now to present myself before my Royal cousin, Foy de Prince! he would hand me over to the provost-marshal, or at least consign me to Loches. No, I will take my own way, and strike a blow for liberty, and, maybe, help to free the King from— from bad .advisers who obscure his better judg- ment." He turned to his friend, who had become very impatient. "'Tis urgent that we get into safer hiding," Dc PompcSrant said; "we cannot take our horses, my lord; the way is too exposed." "Maclaine," the Duke said to Jeanne, "your courage is admirable; but for your timely warning we should have slept here, and we should have been trapped, without any means of escape. Farewell, Jeanne; I may not always be an exiled fugitive, and then ask of me what you will." He raised her hand to his lips, and De Pomperant opened the door for her exit. Bourbon walked up and down the small chamber in moody silence. This need for hurried flight chafed his proud spirit.. He lookod hard at his com- panion to see a sign of yielding on his face, and if he had seen it the Constable might even then have given in and stayed where he was; but De Pomp&amp; rant was buckling on his sword and looking to the priming of the pistols that lay on the table. Even as Charles de Bourbon gave that yearning look at his friend, there rose before his mental sight the fair, voluptuous face of Louise d'Angou- leme, and with her came the memory of her repeated efforts to blight his happiness. She had thwarted his early passion for her daughter, the noble Princess Marguerite, and now she had un- blushingly asked him in -marriage, in order that she might have a just title to his fair provinces. "Accursed harlot!" he muttered; "I will not be taken alive to satisfy your hatred!" Meanwhile Jeanne stood on the staircase, hold- ing by the cord strained against the wall. She felt dizzy with excitement. Was it true that she had actually been in the presence of the greatest captain of his time? And he had kissed her and acknob- ledged the kinship between them! He had even thanked her for her warning! She stood thrtibbing all over, and then she began to wonder how the fugitives would make tha.t escapp; she was bound to do all she could to help them. i" She went swiftly down the uneven staircase to seek the hostess and to warn her of their depar- ture. She turned for this Isurpose to the kitchen, but from the other side of the dark passage at the foot of the stairs she heard voices in loud dispute. As she listened she felt turned to stone; in one of the voices she recognised Gaspard' s ! "Hold ryrir pence, beldame! I tell you we will search the ns% You cannot hinder us? Wait till the Count joins me; he will not be long in coming; he will teach you the power of the King's authority!" Jeanne leant against the kitchen door-post, pale and trembling. She could not feel sure that the</p>
      </div>
