        <p>THE WATER IN THE RkTRS.-TS tr A THICK INKY-LIKE FLUID?</p>
        <p>Councillor Dn. CANNELL, in moving the confirm tio-t of the minutes, said that he should like t call tip a%cen- tion of the Deputy Coairman of the IVat Cmam t:eo to the serims complaints which were heinz made about the colour of the water at he ba...114. Ile did not aLogether blame the committee, because from what he understood there was a certain amount of tannic acid from decom• posing leaves. At the same time he believed water cauld be praduced very much sup.yrior and of a better oolour, although there was nothing of a delet.rious natur • in it. The punlic condemned tie water, cud he aske I the Deputy Chairman of the committ.33 to sty whether it had been filtered.</p>
        <p>Councillor Sritw GREEN: Certainly. Councillor GRACE asked whether, taking into consider- ariaa the unsavoury look of the water supply. it would not be es well to arrange for a supply from Win- wick. lie ()IA 'seed to swimminErin tne batsa with the water in its prerent condition. It was very rep-llaut to attempt to diva into a thick inky-like flail ilk that which was supplied at the bathe and cilled wate-. Is might he ascribed to tannic acid from the leave+, ter, if the watsr was to he allowed to remaln in its preaent condition for two or throe months in the yeas, the re- venue from the baths would decrease initead of in Crease. _</p>
        <p>Councillor JOLLEY : It is the talk of the town. The MAYO 2 suggested that the two commistees should meet and discuss the matter. What bad been Faid that night was out of ord3r. Councillor Lss thought it would have been much bet- private notice of these coestions, or the tuosuhaalvne ftiivce3a. Councillor Grace witn his usnal flowery ltnguaze had compared the wetly to an inky fluid, but thAt was purely a mat :er of imagination, he- came; the colour of the wat3r was brown. The water was inevitable at this time of th] year. Jle • npieornf.eewtlhyichha wrmalse sins it was sinnply the colour of veget t- supported ths suggestion for a meeting of the two com- mittees. The water from Win wick was not suitable for c A inc rynything degree. would be better than the bathing c oune tuorrp Se AB R present state of affairs. The minute] Were then adopted. MARKETS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>It Wa.9 reported at a mee'lng of the above commlt that the total income from all sources far the past four weeks was £332 3s. 101 ; carreopendinz period last year, £364 13s. 6d ; increase, £l7 101. 41. These minutes were passed, oa the tnot•ou of Alder- man HUTCHINSON, SPCOIIIPd by Councillor Woods. GAS COMMITTEE ._ _ •</p>
        <p>THE IMPROVEMENTS A.T LONGFORD WORKS. Councillor HEATON, in moving the adoption of the minutes, aliudel to several of the tenders tor coal, and said there was an increase in price of 31. per ton of coal in the tenders accepted. The price of coal had beeo rising rapidly. Commenting upoa the resolution that the West's Gas Improvement Company's tender for stok- ing maehinery be adopted, he said their object in intre- ducing stoking machinery Was to reduce tho arduous work in the t tort house as far as possible. Therefore they had provided in the agreement with the West Gas ;Improvement Company to supply coal storage hoppers land lay out the works so that they could change to power-stoking machinery at a lab r date, if the increased production of gas justified the outlay. El andetoking a; at present carried on at Longford cost 2s. per toe of coal carbonized. 'rho manned stoking machine consisted of two coal breakers and engines combined, two elsvators for lifting the coal out of the store and deposi log the coal in.two fire I hoppers. In C3Slof a breakdown it was arranged to carry the coals b 7 a cross conveyor from either of the coal elevators to the two hoppers. The e were two movable hoppers which ran on tram lines in front of the ret -rts. Each of these charging machines was provided with a scoop which ran on a guide intolhe retort, and a turn of a handle opened the scoop and deposited the coal evenly oa the bolt ono of the reror-. The two drawing machines ecrisisted of a light frame on wheels carrying the rake for drawing the coke cut of the retort. The whole of the lifting and conveying of the ' coal was done by the steam engincs, except running the scoop fall of coal into the retort, which was done by hand The coke Was drawn from the retort by hand, the machine carrying the weight of the rake. The cost of working the manual stoking machine was about Is 6d per ton of coal carbonised. The inter •st, sinking fund, and cost of repairs did not exceed twopence per ton in any of the towns from whieh they had obtained information. The saving would not be a very large one, but it would enable them to carry on the etoking with- out calling upon the stokers to do the heavy work in the retort house, which was very trying, especially in the hot weather experienced lately. The new retorts were fixed at a higher level, and were longer than the old ones, consequently they were harder to charge, and they allowed the stokers 61 perahift for the extra work. He had every reason to believe that at an (arlv date they: would he able t) reduce the price of gas still further. (Elear, hear.) Councillor Joan EVANS wren led the adoption of toe minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW GREEN coiri he had read tlp: most in- teresting report is the newsnapers of the visit of the gas engineer to the Longf ord Gee WOYkylll I it occurred to him thot it would be a goo I thing if the committee. would invite the Council to inspect the Gas Works. (Elear, hear.) He thought they ought to congratulate their gas engineer upon the position he had obtained in the gas world. (Hear, hear.) The MAYOR : We shall be vory pleased to have a visit from the Council and then you con SO3 for your. selves what is being done. Cur cllor GRACE said that when the Gas Engineers visited the Widnes works the whole of the Council were invited. What had been done in Wairington was only a little bit of the dog in the manger policy. (Laughter.) A!derman Surroix : And the visitor?' should 140 enter- tained when they get to the Gas Works. (Laught ) Referring to the resolution respecting the payment of half the cost for laying the gas main in Borough-road,he said the Corporation paid the other half on the condi- tion that when the time came when the Gas Committee sold sufficinot gas to pay 8 per cent, on the cost of the outlay, they would refund the amount spent by the Corporation. Councillor GRACE : A very reasonable proposal. Councillor BURTON mid he was very pleased to hear that gas consumers might expect a further reduction in the price of gas. He would like to know whether the contractors for coal had given any guarantee as to the carbonising qua' ty of the coal ? Councillor FURNESS : What do the committee purpose spending on manual stoking machinery ? Councillor HELTON in reply said the coal was tested before the tenders were accepted. With regard to the cost of the stokers it would be something near £2,000. He saw no difficulty in the way of the Council being able to visit the works, and he would inform the Chair- man of the committee of the desire of the Council. The minutes were then paseei. THE DREDGING OF THE RIVER.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Rivers Committee hqld on the 29th August the Borough Surveyor was instructAd to report upon the condition Of the channel in the Mersey which the Manchester Ship Canal Comp Any are required to dredge and to keep dredged. Alderman ROBERTS moved the confirmation of the minutes, and said complaints had been made that the dredging was not what it ought to be, and the commit. t.e, therefore, wart rd the facts verified by the Borough Surveyor. Councillor HELTON seconded, and the minutes were confirmed. FIRE ENGINE COMMITTEE.—ASSISTING AT A</p>
