        <head>PRACTICE.</head>
        <p>PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the 22nd August it was resolved that the firemen be allowed a sum of 5. each for the purpose of visiting a neighbouring town to take part in a practice, and that the necessary arranp,emeres be left in the hands of the Chairman and Borough Surveyor. Alderman MITCHINSON moved the confirmation of the minutes, and alluded to the visit of the firemen to neigh- bouring towns. The money which would be paid to each fireman would, he contended, be well spent. Councillor SMETHURST seconded, and the motion was agreed ta. THE AT lENDANCE AT THE GYMNASIUM.</p>
        <p>- At a meeting of the Gymnasium Committee held on the 22nd Auzust it was reported that th. atten3Ance at the classes for the past month was 376. The total atten- dance during the past season were 43,716. On the motion of Councillor Jo rtx EVANS, Feeonded by Alderman SUTTON, the minutss wero nariied. THE LICENCE OF THE FEATHERS HOTEL. SHALL IT BE SOLD ?</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Comenittee held on the 29th Auau:t it was reeolvel tt.at Mr. F. G. Rini- dock be engaged to make monthly test' of ass, for a fee of £l2 12s per annum, and that in the opinien of this committee, it is dpairahln to di^plir of the licFnlA of the Feathers Hotel to no best advantage and interests of the ratepayers. The Mayon to Alde-man Roberts) : You sent me a letter. It is not usual to Told !otters sent under suoh circumstances. The Town Clerk advises me to that effect. The letter refers to one minute on the agenda paper. Alderman ROBE RTS acquiesced. The MAYOR moved the adoption of the minutes, and Alderman SurroN seconded. Allerman ROBERTS rimarkei that he had coma heel&lt; on purpcse that night, twenty-four hours sooner than he need otherwise have done, in order to have somet'iing to say about the resolution in reference to the licence of the Feathers hotel. In the first plane. he could not approve of the resolution as draftel, for the simple reason that it was proposed to exoress an opinioa , upon the disposal of tie licence without the Council being informed of what was considered to he to the hest ' advantage and to the interests of the ratepayers. The resolution was an improper one, and a Mtlel better msthol would have been to appoint a special committee</p>
