        <p>to consider the best means of deaang wien tae der nce, and that committee to report ti al) Co rice. As the resolution stoodat might mean anything. It might mean the opinion of whoever was ulthnatery aepniaral to deal with it, without the question necesearity cemin before the Council again. It they passed that reaolu iers, now it woad he necessary in order to it etTeet that mom 'one ehould be appointed to carry it out. He thought the Colonel ehoul 1 hive a voice in toe aelecti in of the perm o: pereons woo were to be appointed to carry it omit. tie coneidered it Was a ,gre at pity that the repret of the comm-tee who wen r. appointed by the General Purposes GUYlrtlittee wia not suomicrere at the list me a- ing of the Conseil and discu,.s 1 so that everyone might hare known and a diyis'on taken end the matter wattled. He quite believed that is was one of those subje rts which might cause a good deal of tumble and give fse to nertain feelings of "pp 'sheen, which might mate 't difficult to deal with it in a way satisfactory to all. Without expreasine any opinion as to tilt manner in which licenaes ha 1 boon dealt with by the Licensing authority—because that was rot a prorer place in which to express seal an opinion—there was an opinion ia toe t owe, he knew, that those lieinces might have been distributed perhaps in a manner more grew ally satisfactory. With that, however, they had nothine to do. Assuming that this license) was dispased oft, tee only likely perch iser—he diii not need to meg- tion names—it woul be on condition that a transfer Was obtaiued f or the liceaca to a particular district ab nit which they all knew perfectly well, He WVi one of thole oeoole who were no: it-any sense depmdent upon licences for his pTs mat comfort. Hi real'a el the fact that everyine did not think the soma way, aid that there was a largo number of people, who wer I quite as much Witted to respect as he, who did not a wee with him on the subject, but he ail not rhlok GI ore, could be two opinions on the point that to tak 3 a lieence to a district which was already well !supplied was a m s- take, and could only be followed by cors-rquenc gi ex- tremely unpleasant. Be was one of those people who thought—and he had had sufficient experience to know —that a large proportion of the licence holders were doing th-dr level best to carry on a bueine49, the diffi- culties of which no one outside could understand, well withia the units c.f the law. If that cttes of licensees was to be encouragad it was of the utmost imeortance that tenet should not le4 mai() to resort to imeroper means to make a living. One of the ways of bringing th it about was to put a number of licences in a particle hr d'swict where the requirements did not weer ant ia If this licence were sold to a particular firm frr trans- ference ti this part cater place, he said without h ,sita- tion that the district was amply suppled, and it would be impossible for au ble living. He would like however to mike l the licensees there to make a decent a higher platform. Tao), as a C ouncil awna ntioa tdsa alwaysper epxgeePalcpoticonsider ed in t merelyhe p financial considerations. Teat reservation of the 011 Academy. This was another qerstion and one, he ventured to say, whits:to was above the mere question of £s. d. As far as Bridge-street was concerned there could be no to opinions that a few less licensed heaves Would be an advantage. As to time transfer of them to othw districts he hold that they ought not to go in`o a now district without it Was the wish of the people residing the'.'. He theref ore thought that the host interacts of the ra'e- payers would be sire-el by allowing th 3 licence to lapse altoanther. He admittei that if they did so they would benefit the remaining licensed houses, but that was a far less evil than transferring the Heelers to a diFerict already ab indantly supplied. It woula be in the best int onsets of the town intellectually, socially and morally, to lot the licence lapse. He submitte t that in this case the proper and businesslike way would be to appoint a com- mittee who would report to the Council, and then the members could take the responsibility of accepting or rejecting he p ropsa 1. COUOCiIiOT BURTON sad there west no wish on the part of ratepayers to ins pr ova their conditioa be the sale of licences. They had already too maw in the town, and it Was imrovible to have a oublic-houso without mak- ing somebody drunk. A 3 the licence had been purchased with the money of tee ratepayers he hoped that the question as to its dispo ill wool Ihe dealt wit's be- the Council so that The rit ayers mialit know all waas re- gard to it. The resolution should be ref erred back for further consid ration. A'derman SUTTON said he quite aareed with Alderman Roberts that notaing seould be done with regard to the licence without time woole of the. Council being ma lc acquainted with it, bet he to ok it that t qii?ation would be dealt with by the General Parp 's es Committee, and itossinuoh as the eommittee ,V 1.4 (11:11p 'gel of the whole oF tee memeers of the Coo cOil, tee's, would all be aware of what was going to take place Ho die not therefore see the necessity f w a sel?ct committee to c siler the qiiestion, as the General Purposes Cemmit tee would consider it and it would be brought up for confirmation or otherwise before the Council at the next tneiring. He was not one of teose sentimental people who believed that one extra public-house in a district meant increased drunkennees. There was no doubt a good deal of drunkennnse existed in tee to we. Although he was opposed to too many licences beina granted, a.nd althmah he was in favour o! mane' lieences Virta 'tia7rl up in o— der that bongos of a better description mialat be erected, he was still of opinion that deiltikClllCS3 Was alt; in - creased by the erection of a good hose. If noople 7Tr'n required drink maid not get Plan one bows§ they would go to another house for it. They had sesedmi,e th it drunken-Asa in Warrington had very much diminished in the last few years. Theref ore he ,11 th'fdt they ouget to throw rued.aol depeiye the rateplee-s of the benefit of a licence by allow'ng it to lapse, !de eelei- dered the ratepavera should have the full benefit of it, and they should .reisno:e of it in a way that wou'd be melt conducive to the interests of thP tewe. He consi- dered that the proper ceurse woold be to allow the mat- ter to go before the General Purposes Committee, and whatever resolution was arrived at seoul ; be br ught before the Council f-r erinfirm stioa. Councillor F. R. Rormars said he did not know what the resolution meant. It was so worded that the most rabid teetotaler could hardly daaaree with it if be collo put his own interpretation upon it. It eppeared to him that everyone of them who objec.ed to an amplification of the facilities for drinking co-old vete hr the reerawien as it stood with a clear conscience. It was because thee- felt they were b'i'g led into a trap that teats, objected to veto. They wanted to knew what the resolution really meant. The proposer and seconder of the resolution had Rem? mea..,ing in their minds,and some object was placed before the committee,which led teem to pats the resolu- tion. Of that he had no doubt in his own mind.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : I shoold like to say that I was not pre- sent, as far as I remember, at this particular meeting„ hut the obje't the committee had in view was that the licence should not be allowed to lamse. Councillor F. R. ROBBRTS ! Why did they not say so? He thought it was desirable to dispa' 0 o' the licence in the best interests of the ratepayers, hut in their best interest the licence ought to be allowed t lap,e lie considered the moral and spiritual interests of nennle far higher than their financial intaresta. He very much doubted, in spite of the improper laughter of Councillor Lee, whether the disposal of the Homer, would mean any linanc'al addition to the interests of the rate- payers. An increase of the facilities for drinking increased the amount they had to pay in the shape of police salaries. poor rate&gt;. Scs,.. There was considerable difficulty in knowing what to do , In the matter. He thought that the committee ought to lay before them some more clear and pard:cit exnosi- tion of thrir intentions than the resolution embodied. They could not help feeling, that there wee snmathing more than met the eye. Aomething was being done with which the Council would not ha acquainted. It wa. all .ery well for Alderman Sutton to say that the General Patpatea Committee consisted of neer'y all the members nf the Cognril. Some of them.they might be the humbler members, felt that they were there to rep-esent their constituents, and they had no mandate f,om those con- stituents or their own consciences, to traffic in licences. They had a right to be taken into the confidence of the committee in this matter, and they insisted that every- thing should be done in an open and straightforward way. (Hear,bear.) Alderman Surrox : Quite right.</p>
        <p>Te IYOR Idon't think th.re is anything to the contrary, so far aa ths General Purposes Committee is enneereed.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor Funtrvss saw no reason why they Shnuld Objegt to the resolution, ag it committed the Council to nothing. The oninion was exnr -aged that the licence ought to be disposed of to the heat interests oc the town. Re s.hould be very reticent about allowinv the licen3e to lame, althouzh he should not like to see the number of licences increved. But the time had come when other licences would have to be zranred in the gring dis- tricts of the toem, and the present wag a case where they had an onpo-tunity to grant a licence where it was absolutely required. It wan a very Toren sum for them to throw away—something like £6OOO for two licences. He could not help but take rxnoption to the licenee it was nroplied co nut in Orford-lane. They had there four licensed place, indoor and outdoor, within some- thing like 3 minutes walk of the place it was prnposed to carry the licence to. Cum:6llor GRACE : Is this within the score of the minute P.</p>
        <p>councillor Fuaunss : I mutt Pay that I wou'd not he a n,rty to allovving a licence to lapse unless we get some- thing fr it. Councillor Snaw GREEN move as an qtrondmant that the whole of the mtfluC o the comm',tee he confirmed with the exception of the resolution relating to the Feltlyws Hotel -</p>
        <p>Councillnr SRILLCOCIC.Annider,. Cnunedlnr SIITToN sunplrted the amendment because it only in another way RlMport-01 the resolution. Councillor Siilaw GREEN : You will have to thrash it out in Commite. Councillor Worms said they were onlv juct carryiniz out the purport the Gereral Purposes Committee had in view. Councillor JAMES EVANS said they wanted to know rrrr of the details of this proposed disposal of a licence. Votinv for the amendment Wag nen proceeded with. Far it there voted Alderman Totem-Cs, Councillor Shaw Green, Plimpton, lihn Evans. Ellison, Duncan, Burton, J3,llles Evens. Furness. F. R. RThertii, Peatencke, Jolter. Woods, Shillcook, Wilkinson, Blackhurst, and John Roberts. It was therefore declared carried. The remainder of the Committee's minutes were then passed and the Council rose.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON B NK RITPTCY COURT,</p>
        <p>I" FRlDAY.—Before Mr. Re2istrer Rideway. THE FAILURE OF A WARRINGTON BUlLDER.—Pstrick Thomas M Cie, balder, 18, Clegge-street, Warrineton, appeare I for his first public eeaminas ion in bankruptcy. His gross liabi ides a-e expected to rank at £657 12s. 9d.. and the assets are estimated to produce £444 4s: 61., leaving a defiiency of £213 as. 33. In hi 3 s'atemeet to the Offieial Receiver the bankrupt alleged ss the causes of failure "Building my property too good, and p ittine ton much material into them." He also stated at the Official R-ceiver's office that he commenced business in 1888,with £6O capital; and wholly attributes his deficency to Las on rea iaioa of his assets. He only first became aware of his Insolvency when he failed to obtain a loan of £5OO about two or three weeks ago. He hod net coecraeted any debts with knowledee of losolv- ency; and had failed to keep complete bo As of acc mint, or perio Really to ascertain his true financial position. The securities held by creditors fully secured were mortga yes on 11 housea and on one shop at Orford,near Winwsck ; five houses an i one shop in Longf ordestreet. Warringt os ; seven houses and one shop in Earle and Clegge-streets,Warringtoo,and one house,yard and works in Geggestreet, Warriegton.—Mr. Potter (assistant offi- csal teceiver) said the hooks hal hem b idly kept. The cash book came to as cud about fur months prior to tee receiving order. The bankrupt hal received considerable sums of money after that date, and it was important that these should be traced. Toe trustee wanted to trace one in particular. Re would ask the Reeistrar to make an order that the bankrupt should writs up his cash-boie up to the time when he aeplied for the receiving Order.—Tae Debtor stated that his wife had attended to the books, and he was of opinion that they were complete when he filed them —The Re- gistrar made an order for the cash book to be entered up by the Th Oct steer —The further examination was ad- journed. RIVAL EARTHENWARE Deerenes.—With reference to the failure of Joseph Wilson, earthenware dealer, Knuts- ford-rosd, Warrington, Mr. Potter said this bankrupt's case was very much mixed up with Brook- field's, whose bankruptcy had previously been before the court. Brookeeld was subjected to a law, cross-examination, and he ad- mitted having sold goods at less than cost price. Wilson failed shortly after Brookfield and said the cause of his ieselvency was the undue competition of Albert Brookfield who had underso'd him. From the facts which came out at Mr. Brookfield's cross-examination he (Mr. Pottse) was inclined to believe that Wilson's state- ment was absolute'y true. Personally he felt great sym- pathy with Mr. Miens. He had since heard that Mr. Brookeeld was anxious to say that he had not sold under cost price and that Wilson's statement was unreasonable. —Mr. Fowler, who represented Mr. Brookfield, said his client had received certain goods from his relatives in the Potteries for whicn he had to gat the best price he could. When Mr. Brookfield received good earthenware he got the full price for it, and did not under-sell any- one.—Mr. Potter said he proposed to close Wilson's ex- amination at once. He believed the debtor had answered the questions straightforwardly and he believed his statement. He (Mr. Potter) was of opinion that Brook- field hal sold earthenware in a thoroughly reckless manner regardless of price, determined to dispose of the goods come in what might. It was natural that Mr. Wilson's business should suffer in consequence. His sympathies were with Mr. Wison and if Mr. Brookfield wanted to turn round now on what he had previously said he (Mr. Potter) would net attach much importance to it.—Albert Brookfield was then called, and it was decided that his adjourned examination should be proceeded with before closing the examination of Wilson—ln answer to ques tions by Mr. Potter as to the discount he stated at the last court was owing to him by Messrs. Brookfield Bros., he said that ia face of the invoice books of the firm which were now produced he must admit that all dis- counts due to him were deducted from the invoices sent with the goods, and therefore the sums debited to him were net.—Coetieuine his crose-examination Mr. PoVer said I understand that you eo-nplain of the statement made by the debtor Wilson that Your competition con tribeted to his downfall ? Witness: It was not alto- gether due to me fr tee simple reason that I only stood one market in competition with him.—He says that con- tributed to his failure?—Witness : I didn't sell the same class of goods.—Mr. Potter (to the Registrar) I believe Wilson and disregard what he his said.—By Mr. Fowler (to witness): Have you undersold him in any- teing P—Witness Yes,l have undersold him.—ln order to undersell him have you sold under cost price e—Wit- ness : Only rubbish.—Where did you get it from ? Witness : Brookfield Brothers.—Were you in the habit of selling good china or earthenware under cost price ? Witness : No net good china ; but the cracked china I sold at what it weuld tetlh.—The Registrar : He didn't qualify his statement as to selling under cost price when he was last examined in this Court. He had since signed the shorthand notes as being correct, and there was nothing in them of a qualifying nature.—Mr. Fowler : I don't like to ask him any more qaestions.—The Regis- trar then closed the examinations of Brookield and Wilson. A CONFECTIONER'S Beenennercv.—John Broadhurst, residin aeid eaerviag on business As a wholesale con- fectioner at 266, Keutsforl-r -sad, Warring.: on appeared for his first puelic examinetoe in bankruptcy. The liabilities amounted to £216 9s. BJ., and tee assets alter de luctine the usual charge wero estimated to prod ace £22 64111, leaving a deficiency of £194 2s 91. In his statement to the Offi eal R s Aver the bankrupt allevd as the causes of his failure" The rise in the price of flour, loss!of horee, bad trade, paying too large a sum fur tee busiriess, and competition.' Tire b aukrupt further stated to the Offilial Receiver that in October, 1897, althmels without capital, ha took over his late iather's businesa and agreed to pay hie father'e creditors, he hexing died insolvent. He was aware of his insolvency immediately after he commenced business, and ha 1 contracted all existing liabilities since that time.—Mr. Potter said the liabilities wer s small, and the debtor had giv,n very frank answers to tine questions. He had made a mistake in tektite up his father's b isiness and under the circumstances he propos .d to close the examination. —This was done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>RETIREMENT OF INSPECTOR COX.</head>
        <p>THE RETIREMENT OF INSPECTOR COX.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING PREiENTATIONS.</p>
        <p>0.1 Peiday arternoon t oere was an interesting function in the P 'cede Roo n at the Police St ation, Warringroe, when ex-Iruipector Cox was the re- cipient on babel{ of his wife a ail himself of numer- ous presents eubscribel for by his late cemredee. The proceedings were witnessed by all the available me aebere of the force. The presents were exhibited on a table in the ceutre of the room and were greatly admired. They included a handsome oarorneter with the following inscription engraved on a silver plate :—''Ptesented to Inspector George Cox, as a mark of respect and esteem, by ids coon- radeis of the Warrington Borough Police, on the occasion of his retirement after 26 years' service. August 26-h, 1893." There was al act a china tea seri- vice, a lady's and gentleman's umbrella, a geutle- man's silver mounted ebony walking sticks framed photographic group of the Chief Coastable and Insnectore end a go d breast pia. The Cum' CoNsTancE coo 1-cl the proceeiines by readiae, the mieutes of the Wee 1 Committee prom ing Sergeant Rowbottom to the position of Inspector and Constable Lewis to that of sergeant. Inspector &amp;emirs then asked the Chief Constable to make the prese itetiea to ex-luspector Cox on behalf of the police force. The OntEF CONSTABLE in doing so slid a stranger com- ing into that room at the pr, s-nt moment might be ner- d stied if he thought he (the C lief Constable) was ab out te coed Wan, extensive sale by aucion of Soma pre tv goods. (Laughter) fie was not, however, going to do that ; he was going to d o s meet ling more pleasant at though the function hod a note of sZcleesr in it as well as pleas ore. As I ,speotor Spiaks had just said they were losing from their midss, a vaiutd comrade—one cf the men who almost head d the list in length of service. After 27 years of good and faithful work Mr. Cox was pass'ne from their midst and would no more be num- bertiti. alio-lest teem as a naemb ir of the Warrington Borough Force. He was going out and taking a well- earned pension, and surely no police officer wha ever re- ceived a pension got it with such good-will as went with the auporannuation of Inspect or Cox. (Hoer, hear.) El 9 joined the Borough force in 1872, was a merit con- etabie in 1.878. a s irgeant in 1877, and an inspector in 1881 Ile found on lo )king through the books that the whole of the 'sheet which dralt with promotions and meritorious acts was filled 119, and if Mr. Cow had not retired a new leaf would have been necessary. (Laugh- ter.) But what did he find on the other side of the sheet which was for entries of doling uenci:s and reports and erime3 against police regulations ? There was a blank. and not a single report against him during the whole of the 27 years. (Applause.) A little while ago when Insp,ctor Cox and his elder colleague, Inspector Spinks, tendered Ulf ir resignation through him to the Watch Committee the committee by a unanimous vote went out of its way to record a resolution expressing its high appreciation of the services of both officore. A gentleman who was sitting at the table at the committee meeting said to hen (the Coil Constable) "You wit have few like COX when he his gone." He believed that if they heel some 1k; him in the force they had none better. (Hear, hear). Teat expression cf the Watch Committee was only an echo of What the general pubic felt in respect to Inspector Cox. On every hand he holed words of regret at Inspector Cot's departure. He was leaving them without one single enemy. Than was something for any men to say who hal beea 27 years in any town and a great thing for a police officer to be able to say beoause they were put in the way of making enemies in the d seharee of their dirties. . With regard to the manner of the d seharge of Inspe :tor Cox's dutits there coed ban) two opinioes. He had always done them like a man and done his best. When he had gone into a police court he h•id never made it warmer for a dofendent than necessary. If there wis anything to be said in favour of the piisener he always said it. He (the Chief Con- s-able) once heard a magistraie say " I would believe I C3x's word as sour as I would believe the oath of any I men." Tnat was a grand thing to tro s aid of any &amp;Eel.. If he were giving a new police officer advice as to how he must conduct hens 'lf in order tic get on ad to be well thought of wed become a vain d servant of the public he should say follow as near as you c-n in the fee stars of ex-Inseecter Cox. (Hear, hear ) He ceul I assure them that thee were n•t empty pereees in Me mouth, and he could goon indefinitely in a similar strain. lie should, however. now proceed with the busi- ness of the afternoon. The first present was from the pence force—a barometer which was to be the spacial property of ex•lnipector Cox—and a china tea service for his good help-naee-, Mrs. Cox. (Applause.) From the detective department he had to present a silver- mounted ebony walking-stick for ex-InsPector Cox, and a silver-mounted silk umbrella for Mrs. Cox. Fr9M the Police Athletic Clubmf which Mr. Cox was the treasur, r, he had to present a silver-mounted silk umbrella. Fro n the inspectors there was presented a framed photo- graphic group of himself and the Inapeetors. and from his cornrede. Inspector Spieles, he (tho Chief Constable) had to present a gold brevet pin. He hepur Mr. Cex would live long to enjoy these presents, and Galt the declining years of his life might he crowned with health, prosperity and peace. (Applause). Inspector SPIXXS endoroe.l what, had barn eaid by tin Chief Conetabes. He was sure teat the whole of the fore, along with himself felt the severance after work- ing together for so many years. Since the time when he toot( ex-Inspector Cox to be examined by tho PJliNe Surron they had never had an angry word between them or any mieunderstanding. It was a great Weasure fn him to be able to say So, and on the preoront occasion he could hardly find words to express himaeif. He deeply regretted parting with a man who for such a long stretch of Tears had been a pers mai frigid. In diffi- culties when he had wanted some one to confer with he I had always constiitsd Inspector Cox, and found him a ' true friend and helper. If there was any Work to b 3 done Inspector Cow would never look at the clock. , (Hear, hoar.) He was always there when he was I wanted. His assistance had been very valuable to him (Inspector Spinks) and they had worked shoulder to shoulder. With reference to Mrs. COI( she had alway 3 male her husband's home comfoit ibis and happy, an i he thought a good deal was due to her for Inspscror Cow's success as a police officer. He would call noon the force to give three cheers f ~r Inspector Cox before they said Farewell. 1 Three cheers were thee heartily given. euspector TAYLOR proposed &lt;3 vote of thanks to the Chief Constable for making tee presentation. Toe first ' face he'remember seeing on coming to Warrington was ' that of ex•lnspeetor Cox. A brtter pone() officer and a man and friend he had never known. (Applause.) ' Sergeant CHENEY said ex-Inspector Cox had always been raly and willing to give advice to any officer who I sought it. He was always honest arid straightforward in his actions and never overlooked the fact that he was a public servant. (Applause.) P.C. names% as the oldest police constab's, said he commenced duty on the force with es-Inspector Cox and t3ok hie turn with him night and day. He was sure the police constables would miss a good friend when Inspector Cow had gone. They wished him and Mrs. Cow long life and happiness in their retirement: He went i away with the goodwiti of the people of Warrington and all who knew him. (Applause). Ex-Inspector Cow. who met with a hearty reception on rising to acknowledge the gifts, thanked the mem- bers of the force on behalf of Mr3.Cox and himself, for the beautiful and valuable articles just presented to him by their worthy Chief Constable. They would always be a pleasure to them and remind them of the many happy years they had spent in Warrington. He very much re. gretted parting from old comrades who had boors associated with him and who had worked shoulder to shoulder with him in the varioue grades of office it had been his privilege to fill. He again thanke I them, Wnat would now say farewell to all of them. (Applause). • The interesting pm:ceilings terminated wits the sing- ing of "For he's a jelly good fellow," It will be seen in another column that the leraytor (Alderman J. H. Wright) has opened a subscription Ifund for an !. intended testimonial to Inspector 1 Spinks and Cox. I . 1</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week Ending 27th august 1898. Darin," the week the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington Registration District 50 35 The excess of births over deaths was 15. The Birth Rate was 41.4 per 1,000 of population. The death-rate from all causes was eq,„ii to 289 per 1,000, against 19.5 the average of the previbus 4 weeks, and against 23.5 the corresponding period of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 174 per I,ooo,against 2.8 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks, and against 13.4 the corresponding period of 1897. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 op. 19 5 8 3 35 The deaths included-1 from diphtheria 1 from measles, 1 from whooping cough, 18 from diarrhea, 2 from lung diseases, 1 from heart disease, 1 from in 'juries, 10 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week:—Scarlet fever, 3 eases, Diph the-ia 1 case, fever 0, enteric fever 1 case, Erysipelas 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital for week ending,. August 27th, 10. METEOROLOGY—The mean temperature was 601 Fah., and therefore 1.6 deg. lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 82F., was recorded on the 25th inst., and the minimum temperature 37F.. on the 22nd in3t. The rainfall measured '6B inch. The general direction was S.E. the reading of the anemometer denoting 85 miles. _ _</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORHAM,. Medical Officer of Health.</p>
