      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>V&amp;LOER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>V&amp;LOER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Own (the -v7ii-se-rira.-I;nrted that since the last meeting h had male the toll iwing valua-ions :—ln the townshtp of Warrington, Mr. B. Cenninghem, bottling stores and rabies, Owen-street, gross £5l 103.. rateable £44 ; Messrs. W. Silcock and Sons. King-street Wire Works, additions. gross £l2, rateable £lO ; Messrs. The Whitecross Company, Limited, Milner-street. The stables and additions reported by the oversoers, near Priestley•street, he f tund to be the re-roofing of the stable at tar Are, and the taking down of the roof over the galvanizing shed and its renewal. The new roof over the g tivinizing shed was of more value than that taken down, but it would effect the rental value s little as not to be worth notice. Me. James New- ton, Twiggary Farm and land, Manchester-road. Mr. James Newton, appealed against his asseesments. The assessment on the Twiggery Farm. gross £2O, rate- able £l7, should stand. The two other assessments should be struck out and a new assessment inserted of agricultural land 53 acres, gross £9O, rateable £B3. The Twiggery beds were ia the, occapetion of Colonel Black- -1 burne and should be aesesssrl to him as under : twintaary ' bads, 12 acres. gross £24, rateable £22 104. Mr. Ernest Sennett, Old Warps, Knutsford-road. Mr. Bennett ap- pealed against his assessments. The assessments put under Mr. Bennett's name were those which held good in the whole of the property which was in the eceapi.. tion of the late Mr. John E. Davies. The ts Old Warps " itself was still in the occupation of Mrs. Davies at a rental of £5O, which was about to be vaeated imme- diately. The cottase attached to the" Old Warps "and the farm buildings, together with 22 acres of land were in the occupation of Mr. Bennett, who had also the right to graze the remainder of the land, when not in use by the Corporation, and he paid a rent of £5O per annum, on annual tenasey, without restrictions. The assessments standing in Mr. Bennet:'S name should be struck out and new assessments entered as under: Mrs. John E. Davies" Old Warps," gross £5O, rateable £42 10e.; Mr. Ernest Beorett,cottage and buildings gross £l5, rateable £l3 ; Mr. Ernest Bennett, land 22 acres of grazing rights eroes £35. rateable £33. These assess- ments leave 29 acres 3 roods 29 parches unaccounted for except that Mr. Bennett will be assessed for certain rights over this land. He was of opinion that an assess- ment should be entered against the Corporation for such riahts as they would possess over these lands for the public ns under : Warrington Corporation 29 acres 3 roods, 29 perches, land ne tr Old Wams, Knuteforcl-road, gross £3o,reteable £2B 10 t.The entrance lodge was separ- ately assessed at gross £6 is. ,rateable £5 In the town- ship of Poulton with Fearnhead: Mr. Royston appealed against the essestment of chemical works gross £l5O, rateable £ll3 ; of Paddington House gross £5O, rateanle £45.He had valued the works and thought the assessment should he reduced as follows : Chemical Works, gross £llB, rateable £96. Paddington House, stables and gardens, gross, £45, rateable £3B. The lands adjacent to the works and also adjacent to the house occupied by Mr. Royston were seperately assessed and the cottage which had its tack-entrance in the works was also separ- ately assessed. The report was adopted. THE FIRE AT MESSRS. RICHMOND'S. Mr. Smith on behalf of Messrs. Richmond and Cam- party, Limitticl, applied for a reduction of assessment on the ground that there had been a file there and damay„ed the premises. The pnrton bum', down was assessed at £303 gross, end £316 10s rateable.—The Clerk told Mr. Smith that Mr. Owen, the valuer, would visit•the premises and the co /mane would have to pay the full rate up to the time of the fire, and allow- for a reduction afterwards. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>APIT " CHAT.</head>
        <p>APIT " CHAT.</p>
        <p>What the Corporation of London received and spent last year is at last mads public. The most interesting it ems rotate to the Diamond Jubilee, for celebrating which over £7,000 went out of the treasury. Remembering the gorgeous spectacle presented by the Lord Mayor on Jubilee Day, one learns without surprise that, his new purple robs and train cost £B4. It was cheap. The redecora- tion of his stage coach took £2B4,and was much less dumbfounding. Who would grudge the City Mar- shal £l2 183.6 d for Hessian boots and a cocked hat, £22 7s.for a new bearskin and other vanities,or £92 for riding on a horse? On furniture for the Man- sion House £2,000 was spent,in addition to several hundreds on the decoration of the building. Illu- minations cost the Corporation the modest sum of £l,4oo,besides £lB5 for that dismal failure, the illu- mination of the dome of St. Paul's. The City Fathers shine most in the entertainment department. They refreshed themselves and their friends to the tune of £2,217 at the Corporation ball, gave the guard of honour £34 in drinks,spent £BO on flowers, £249 on their trip to the naval review although the ship and the creature comforts on board were gratuitous, and £3Ol on an ad iress to the Queen and a trip to Windsor. After these items who can say that £13,673 is too much for the City's pension list, even with the addition of .0,043 for •almshouses and £4890 as charitable donations?</p>
        <p>In view of the fact that the tribesmen beyond the Indian frontier are invariably found in posses- sion of good serviceable rifles, the statement is of interest that the mutilated remains of discarded Martini-Henrys are eagerly bought up by Belgian manufacturers. In conversation with a gentleman in the trade the other day, I was aosnred that the bulk of these weapons can be made fit for use again. The War Office instruction is that before old rifles are allowed to fall into the hands of the marine-stare dealers they must be so treated as to be useless in the hands of an enemy, bat this rule seems to be carried out in a very perfunctory fashion. The woodwork of the stacks is destroyed by fire, and from this cause the more fusible por- tions of the metal suffer. But the action is seldom seriously damaged, and the :'nicking" of the barrels is so ineffective that 50 per cent of them can be straightened again and made almost as good as new. A ready market is obtained for the rehabilitated rifles at Teheran and other places in Persia.</p>
        <p>At the vary moment when the Admiralty ia try- ing to subsidise the mercantile marine with the view of inducing shipowners to train boys on board their vessels, who might afterwards be drafted into the navy, the Department has been seized with a strange scrupulmpness about accepting bays on board men of war who have been brought up in the . training ship Shaftesbury. These boys are under the charge of the In iustrial Schools Comrcittee of the London School Board, and none are admitted to the training ship who have been guilty of any seri- ous offence. Moraover,the lads are picked boya,bath as regards their health, their physical condition, and their conduct, and no boy can be admitted to the training ship without the approval of the magiitrate before wnom he was originally charged. The school Board has remonstrated with the Ad- miralty with a view to gettinz a reconsideraticin of the decision in the case of juvenile offenders. The Admiralty is surely drawing the line a little too hard and fast.</p>
        <p>The terrible oppression to which many employees in shops are subjected is receiving some check from the post action of the London County Council, and will meet:with a still further bar by the instruc- tions which the Council has just given to its in- spectors under the Shop Hours Act. These inspec- tors during the last year paid 365 fl visits to shops, and the number of cases in which they found the Act violated in some of its provisions amounted to more than a fourth of that number. It is gratifying to notice that these figues do not mean that one in four of the shops deliberately worked their young people beyond the 74 hours per week fixed by the Act as the limit of weekly employment, and the Public Control Committee of the Council reports that the number of cases in which young persons now work beyond the 74 hours is rare. The offences were of a minor character, such as not exhibiting notice cards and so on. As the C ouacil has resolved to enforce the Act with more vigour, it has issued instructions to its inspectors to that effect The number of 3652 investigntons in a wilderness of shops like London is a r:diculously small result.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of London Ills issuel a letter to Anglican Oaplains on the Continent pointing out that any marriages celebrated in England on banns published in an English chapel on the Continent is in law invalid, and reminding them that they have no authority to publish banns for the celebration of marriage in England. This circular has caused some little conr,ernetion amOnzet the chaplains, Some of whom complain that theirattention should have been called to the matter earlier. It is com- mon knowledge that marriazes have been cele- brated in England on banns published partly abroad and partly in England, The chaplains will of course at once act upon the Bishop's in- structions, but there is a dipodtion to question the strict accuracy of his view as to the invalidity of such marriages. Tae general opinion is that before a marriage can be declared void by reason of the undue publication of banns it must be shown that bo',h parties were cognicant of the irregularity.</p>
        <p>The Royal Hoeicultural Society is taking a' new departura, with the view of stimulating the culti- vation of apples and pears throughout the country. The assumption is that apples and pears are the most useful of all Enl.-eish.grown fruits ; but, con- sidering that some disticts are much less favour- ably situated than others, the object is to offer sub- stantial encoureg•ament to the growers in such dis- tricts. They cannot compete with districts to , which nature has been more bauntiful ; and the society, recognising this disheartening state of thing, has divided the country into ten districts, and is offering separate and independent prizes to the succesaful competitors in each district. The Society will t Ike the trouble of unpacking the fruit and of exhibiting it, and will pay part or whole of the fares of the four principal prizetakers of each diAtfct. All this should do comething to give the fruit industry an impulse.</p>
        <p>One side of Bing-street, Westminster, is all but dem-dished to make way for Government offing, and the other side will soon follow. It is a short diruzy str.,,et, chiefly known in these days for the police station where Lefroy was received immedi- ately after his canture. But it has an interesting history. Formerly it stret -riled from Charing Cross nearly to the Abbey, and the Merry Monarch con- templated rebuilding it, a design he abandoned like many others. ft was the scene of a Sacheverell riot. Oliver Cromwell paid rates for a house next to the gateway of Blue Boar's Tlead Yard, but the dwelling was swept away long ago. The yard it- self has still a brief span of life.</p>
        <p>Go99ip is full of speculation here as to the hon. ours that await Sir Herbert Kitchoner and his officers for their respective shares in the reconquest of the Soudan. A. peerage is the least that can be given to Sir Horbert, and the opinion of clubland has already selected a title for him. Viscount Kit- chensr of Khartoum he is to he by common consent. The title is alliterative and appropriate It is more difficult to suggest adequate rewards for men like Generals Hunt-q. and Rundle, Brigadier general Macdonald, Maxwell, and Lewis, or Colonel Broad - wood, who have for years devoted themselves to the service of Egyp!-, taking their share of such chances as fvquent fighting might give them at the sacrifice of vgimental promotion. Hunter, one of the best of fighting men and a capable leader, as he has proved in every crisis, merits all the honour that may be given to him. Nobody has dope more uous than he to make disciplined soldiers of the impet- friend and colleague Rundle has always taken a RYPtiaris and more sluggish Soudsnese. His full share of every campaign on the fr.intier of Egypt; and there has been very little fighting in- deed during the last ten years against the dervishes in which the tough, cool-headed, courageous Mac- donald and Lewis and Maxwell have not taken part. o having To Colonel Broadwoorl belongs the credit of having made the Ezyptian cavalry an excellent force for reconnoitring purpo and f ces, improved even upon the standard to which these squadrons had been raised by their old commander, Burn Murdoch Promotion and honours for officers in command of British troops will come in the natural course of things.</p>
        <p>It is taken for gran'ed here that the papers which Mr. Gladstone has bequeathed to his family will be entrusted to Mr.. John Morley or Mr. George Rus- sell for biographical purposes. Covering as they do practically the whole of the nineteenth century, co far as the eventful history of this country is con- earned, the dOCutzenta are far more valuable than</p>
        <p>even those which the Kaiser would like to have sup- pressed after Bismarek's death, and certainly those lelt by Lord Beaconsfield, and which Lord Rowton still refuses to publish. It is a remarkable fact that the latter has never vouchsafed any explanation of his decision to keep Lord Beason:4eld's capers a ' profound secret. Can it be due to Court influence? It is well-known that the Queen conferred the most marked favour upon Lord Beaconsfield, and there is a belief that Lord Rowton deems any publication of his memoirs inadvisable within the limit of her Majesty's lifetime. No such influence prevails in the case of Mr. Gladstone, and I learn that the com- mission to prepare the papers will be given soon by the executors, the Rev. St-Then Gladstone and Mr, Herbert Gladstone, probably to Mr. Russell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FATALITIES OF THE BITHING SEASON.</head>
        <p>FATALITIES OF THE BITHING SEASON.</p>
        <p>A RESCUER RESCUED.</p>
        <p>Tax list of fatalWea while bathing, during the past summer, has stiown n 0 diminutiln upon previous years, in spite of the many instances in which reicues have been effected. A noteworthy case i that of a young man named J. H. Laugharne, of 3, Msrlin's-terrace, Haverfordwest, wh3 has played the principal part on two occasions of rescue frosa death. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Lanharne wag walking by a Welsh stream one afternoon when he heard a scream and saw a lad struggl- ing in the water. Be leaped over a wall, injuring his back in so doing, and though unable to swim, plunged into the river, and eventually rescued the boy, when sinking for the last time. This brave deed was never recrgnisal, but it is not the only occasion on which Leugharne's life has been in peril. As he stet Ld to a Pembroke Herald reporter : " I was taken ill in 1889, when employed at Cardiff Post Office,and getting weaker every day, I was at last unable to work. A medical man, after treating me for eighteen menths,seid my easel was one of incipient con. surnption and spinal Curvature. He then seat me to Brompton hospit il,where I was treated for seven weeks. I then came hems, and »lona this time rescued the drowning boy. After that my back was very bad,and I returned to the hospital for another thirteen weeks. After leaving, I was ones more taken seriously ileani so for the third time I was treated at the hospital for another three months. " Although I received benefit at the time, after I again ' came home my blood seemed to becetno plisoned, and I 'broke out' all over. The doctor said I had skin disease. Then I had a gathering in the left foot, and the doctor said it was due to a diseased bone. I suffered such irritation that I could not sleep at nights. Tnen the crisis was reached, and I felt like a dying man. But the rescue came ; one day a pamphlet was handed in at the cher telling of marvellous rescues that had been effected by Dr. William's Pink Pills fr Pala People, and my mother advised me to try the Pills. I ob.ained six boxes. and when I had takee the thri b ix, I could feel myself gaining strength and hope every day. I afterwards took the remaining three bleats, and now I honestly feel as healthy as any man could he : the irritation has passed away, and the wound on my feet hes hea'ed• For years I could sleep but little at night, but now I enjoy sound refreshing slumber. I was also a martyr to rheumaism for t welve years; now it has disappeared. Mr. Laueharne's expedeere s another instance of the remediable wiy .Dr. Williams' Pink Pine cure.when hos- pitals and doctors have treated unsuccessfay : nese Pills have cured many thousand/ of cas-s of anaemia, rheumatism,chronic erysipelas, bronchitis, and ecz tine. They are also a splendid nerve and spinal tonic,and thus cure paralysie, locomotor atavy, neuralgia, St. Vitus' dance,and nervous headache. They are ott Linable of all chemists,and frnm Dr. Williams' Mediln3 Company. 46, Holb3rn-viadect, Lend3n, at 2s. 91. a box, or s'rc for 13s. 91. but are genuine only ear full name, Dr. Wil- liams' iink Pills for Pale People. Worth Less imitations sold under misleading titles should be avoided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MIN CUTS HIS THROAT AT</head>
        <p>A MIN CUTS HIS THROAT AT</p>
        <p>WA RRINGTON.</p>
        <p>INQUEST-STRONG REMARKS BY THE CORONER.</p>
        <p>On Friday at the Police Court, Mr. S. Brizhouse, coroner, held an inque3t on the daath of Thomas Brown, a married mtn,who committed suicile on Tuesday even. ing by mattinT his thrcal with a razor. The deceased, we may adcldaq:l only recently been discharged from the Rainhill Asylum. The mother of the dectived, who was the first vetoers callod, exhibited great distress of mind She said the Awl= authori ie hid sent a letter to ht san's wife aFk'ng her to came and bring him Ont. Her d tughzer in-law th n forwardi'd the Mtn- t) her (witues3) and went and brourzht him tome. When witnocs saw him on Monday night the thought he was no bettcr. The Coroner then read the letter tothe jary,which was a printed document. The letter orai to this effeet, "We be to i 'Corn, Mrs. Brown that ThDIIA9 Brown is recovered, and has received his dischargo from the asylum. We should be glad if Mrs. Brnwa would Come for him and bring la completa out of clothes."</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night las't, the witness continued, her younger son took him fir a walk shoat 6 o'clock, and she witness said to him "Tommy how do you feet ?" and he said "I feel very bad mother." She then advis d him to lie down. At this point, the Coroner cousid Ted it I host to dispense with any further evidence from the witness as her mind was wandering frm the point at issue. He advievi that she he led outside the court room as she would continue talking and sobbing. The next witeesa called was Peter Meakin, 41, Bank. Street, who explained that th deeessed was his step-son. In answer to a quotation fro 13 the coroner he said it was correct that the decsaaed went ti the Police Station suffering from delusions, and WA3 s flf.; to the Asylum in the ordinsry way. A letter from the Asylum came to the deceased's wife, who lived in servici out of town. He (witness) saw hirn on Monday wh,m he came back, and said as soon as he st 97 him " Teat man is not as swell as when he went in." On Tws lay about 8 o'clock deceare a's brother went opatairs to Fee to the gas iii deceased's bedroom. He waded through something on the floor and concluded hiebrother had beenvomiting. He went downstairs and informed his mother. She found deceased had cat his throat with a razor. The deceased was cn the Timr ii) his night clothes. Sergeant Dean then arrived, and they found the razor n a pool of blood. Ia witness's opinion ho was not fit to be diecbareed. The Coroner : I think under the cireumstances, the proper course is to adjourn the inquest. It is scandalous that he should be discearged on th a Monday as cured, and on the Tuesday this should happen. Wet must look after people whatever their p:sition in life may be. Toe last witness in answer to the Coroner said ha and his people were very much dissatisfied. By a Juror: The deceased bad not threatened to do anything to himself, but before he went in the asylum :everyone knew he was cl a suicidal tendency. The Corsner : We will not put the relatives to any annoyance. They will get the burial order for Wednese day. (Addressing the witness) : We will give you the burial order and will adjourn the inquiry. We shall want an explanation, and I will see that a medical man COMPS down to give us an explanation if he can, how it came about that your step-son was discharged.l am speaking for the county authorities, when I say they will want it as well. (To the Jury) : 1 see the relatives will be mere ntisfied if the inquiry is adjourned. The asylum is there for the convenience of these people, and ' it is for all such people to be guarded. The inquire was adjourned until 10 o'clo.ck in the morning of Friday, the 16th September.</p>
