        <p>W.aut we need twelve million tons of 11_ itrate of soda annually. Can we get it? Sir Witr.a.vm CROOKES replies : "A preliminary cal- culation shows that there need be no fear on that sore ; Niagara alone is capable of supplying the !equired electric energy, without much lessening its tuighty flow. The future can take care of it- The artificial production of nitrate is clearly tvithin view, and by its aid the land devoted to lleat can be brought up to thirty bushels per acre, Standard." When the demand overtakes supply atll further ahead, the old problem will return 3 mentions the food-productiveness of the ssuana, which is one hundred and thirty-three that of wheat, as showing what the tropics 'Y tic, for the ternporate zones. It will probably suggested that as nations become more civilised ue Wheat consumption may decline, as it ob- ti?usly does as families become better off. Sub- etuutes, chemical and otherwise, may come into The problem, in fact, will probably hay e solved itself before 1931. Science, in fine, has no it to alarm us about the far future if it cannot ',estremedies and compensations.</p>
        <p>THE desperate valour of the The Fate of Dervishes has not availed. Maim sm. Mandism has been overthrown. centre of its power and the Khalifa himself a been overwhelmed. It was the last of the ?ghting faiths of the world. The MAHDI believed 14a resurrected and conquering Mohammedanism. l'rena the desert he was to sweep down to the sea, alld then to unknown regions. He did not live to lee his dream fulfilled. He conquered the Soudan, ' heestablished himself at Khartoum, he defeated Ilicks Pasha, he built up Omdurman, he had ~erdon beheaded,and he soon afterwards perished !himself of the excesses in which he indulged, le'aving his mantle and his authority to ABDULLiII, the halif a, who is now a fugitive, and blay become a captive. There is no ch ince ter the re-est iblishment of Mandism. It was a</p>
        <p>religion ruinous to the Cantral Soudan, because it Meant the maintenance of hordes of Dervishes at the expense of the male inhabitants of ear3ry place 14 importance on the Nile, who were taken from tile fields to be made into soldiers, and it also ktsssalt the constant raiding for grain, plunder, and tnt'llnishment of the tribes who were weary of a a leader which gave them no rest. The b-,4ll(lists have exhausted the country for some o'recls of miles above and below Khartoum, octlginally. the place was the great centre the 'Middle African slave-trade. Long of slaves tramped to it from regions about 47171t1 below the great lakes, rested there jghile, and then want for ward to Berber, Some on eairo, and ethers on to Suakin. It was an krab city, and the beginning of Mandisru was an ta,b revolt against the measures taken to sup' 13Nss the slave-trade. OSMIN DIGNA was a slave r4aider before he beeamo a leader in the cause of Til3 KaILIFA. cannot hope, even if he .t,ades capture, to set up his standard again in any 0 b 'ler place. Hie presence will mean that he has Be4beaten, and beaten by soldiers of Moliana- -11 Ise, as well as of Christian faith. Is may try set up some semblance of authority in a llicte place. But we hope it may be possible tO 0 take him prisoner and to convey him to alro. The terrorism he has exercised over 6he region which acknowledged his sway has given Placa to the liveliest satisfaction. Plunderings, exactions, a heavy blood-tax, the iron heel of clopression, the bitter fury of fanaticism, have Passed a way. The rule of Egypt always meant justice Itt Khartoum, even when it was severe. It will 11(31v be associated with the complete overthrow of °Ystern which has made the place hateful for ttearly twenty years. Mandism is a wonderful Induet of the time, in a land where so many toarvels have appeared, At one time it seemed in- bl'incible. The railway has been the means of vanquishing% for it has made advance easy, sure, it'd irresistible. Ma.lulism may now be included 41110ngst the extinct faiths. It began its career //I.th the sword and the spear. It has been killed by rifles, Maxim guns, and artillery.</p>
        <p>rt• St. Helens is a tow: which apparently suffers rattle in the way of moral debasement by the trv.ing of children under the age of 13, with in- iricating liquor. Twelve months ago the licens- lg uomMittee of the town passed a resolution )jPr'ecating the practice, and appealing to the v.lcl:licans to discontinue it. According to Chief Btable Wood's statement, the magisterial re- was not an order—has been attended to tb"11 a sedulousness which clearly indicates that the publicans have even more consideration for tO Juvenile purchasers of liquor in their danger, ti;4ll for the disfavour with the bench into which th9Y would fall by encouraging the serving of :sren. Only three cases have been reported the year, which, out of a population of r. (Int 90,000 is an infinitesimal percentage. There Fla nO better way of fostering intemperance than by !serving young children,and the general discontin- &amp;ice of the practice,whichwe believe is gradually ,nuorrung actual and real, will go a long way to- Wards lessening the country's yearly drink bill, meliorating poverty, and raising the moral tone ^Of the future generation.</p>
        <p>There are very good grounds for believing that those who style the Ship Canal a "white I;.lophant" seriously libel the great undertaking. is true enough that the shareholders derive no nividend from the Company, and may therefore be Pardoned for any bitterness they may feel against ,the Ship Canal as a delusive road to fortune, but 'tovertheless traffic on the waterway is rapidly Swelling in volume, earnings are increasing and ex- Pouditure decreasing. With such a state of fifirs existing there is ample reason for the optim- t.tlo tone of Alderman Southern's speech at the gkanchester City Council meeting. At the Present time,he stated, every berth was occupied, 4,11 d the question of increasing the capacity of the Mrt had to be considered. Here then we have a te,2inplete refutation of the croakings uttered by ttio people who prophesied a scarcity of traffic on ego waters of the Canal. By enterprise and • 4ergY the number of vessels entering, and clear- g from Manchester is already so large as to t4,.11.5e the directors to think over the possibilities increased accommodation, and a deepening of th. channel for vessels of greater tonnage. To ...Yout any such scheme as the one mooted would involve a large additional expenditure, and the. increased trade done by the Canal is of ricient dimensions to persuade the Manchester , Payers into viewing such an outlay with com- Noney, then there should be no doubt that the e„l'P Canal has at last turned the corner and iered the road leading on to success. It will be foeng ere the end to that road is reached, but there d) all the same, more reason for hopefulness than tesPendency, and a lot of people can fare sump- on hope when there is nothing more gnhstancial at hand. Tho Ash.</p>
        <p>MI</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>under-Lyne magistrates have been .to barter a license with a brewer, but their brethren at Warrington, they refused offered. Messrs. Gartside and Company _ .red to give the Corporation a piece of lue £l,OOO, and situated in a congested return for the privilege of getting stronger .a rising locality by converting the Birch into a fully-licensed house. Though the if struck would have enabled the Corpora-</p>
        <p>preparo</p>
        <p>ion to treble the width of a narrow street, the Licensing Committee, at whose head was the Mayor, refused the application. In Warrington a similar "rising district " has had a new public- house thrust upon it to relieve a locality where alehouses are the most plentiful of any kind of property. The bargain is not such an atrocious one as that suggested at Ashton-under-Lyne, be- cause the number of licenses is reduced, but all the same the brewers are being allowed to retain the belief that wherever there is a "rising dis- trict" there they may plant a public-house, not because the inhabitants are pining for it, but be- cause the magistrates can see their way to a more or less advantageous deal.</p>
        <p>There were perhaps more gas engineers in War- riagton on the 27th ult than at any previous period in its history. This was in consequence of the visit of the Manchester District Institution of Gas Engineers who had been invited by the Warring- ton Corporation Gas Committee to visit the Long- ford works. The Institution seeks by the mutual assistance of its members to disseminate know- ledge as to the best means of manufacturing gas and supplying it to the consumer at the lowest cost. Opinions are freely exchanged between the members as to their successes and -failures in ex- periments which have been tried,and thus the In- stitution serves a most useful purpose. The im- provements which have been carried out at Long- ford were favourably commented upon by the visitors, and even to one uninitiated in the art of gas manufacture it is easy to see why Longford possesses advantages over Mersey-street. The saving in the cost of carting coat alone must amount to some thousands of pounds per annum. At Mersey-street every ton of coal has tolae lifted out of the railway waggon and carted to the works, whereas at Longford, the waggons of coal are shunted on an elevated railway inside the works, and by releasing the trap-doors the coal slides out of the trucks into the coal store. When the improvements contemplated have been com- pletely carried out, the coal will be tipped into conveyances which will supply the retorts, and the coal having served its purpose, will be dropped into waggons again, in coke-form, without having been handled. Those who are disposed to grumble at the price of gas will perhaps be more than a little surprised to learn that fifty years ego Warrington gas consumers paid 15s. per thousand cubic feet. Substantial reductions have been made since then, and in November next a further ; reduction of 3d. per thousand cubic feet is to be Imade, but still we are some distance from obtain- big gas gas at so cheap a rate as our neighbours in Widnes.</p>
        <p>If the tale told by the " Newcastle Leader" be true,then Lord Salisbury has done much to render inappropriate the simile in which the late Prince Bismarck described him. The statement published in the journal mentioned is to the effect that it was the spirited and deterlained action of England, through the Prime Minister, which alone prevented the intervention of European powers in the struggle between America and Spain, with the object of humbling American ambition. Lord Salisbury is credited with promptly vetoing the base proposal, said to have emanated from France, by a threat to "join forces with the Ammican Government and declare war upon France and such supporters as should come to her assistance." And we are finally told that the French Government "beat a hasty and undignified retreat." The British lion " rampant' is a novel spectable in these days, and it is hard to imagine the humiliating submission of France and her allies, seeing that both have fearlessly twisted the lion's tail on many recent occasions. The Prime Minister as the vehicle of such a mandate from the British throne, assumes a role which fits him but strangely, though we should be only too glad to see it becomingly and per- manently maintained.</p>
        <p>The policeman's lot, we have been told in Gilbertian phrase,is not a happy one. Still it has some compensating advantages and we are dis- posed to think that at least two local police officers will be inclined to agree with us. Two presenta- tions have been made to men who have faithfully served their Queen and country in the police force. The first of these interesting events took place at Daresbury Courthouse on Tuesday when P.C.Robinson,who has been promoted to an- other division, was the recipient of a purse of gold from the inhabitants of Moore. Such demonstrations of the indebtedness of the inhabitants to the village policeman are very rare, though perhaps they are all the more welcome for that reason. They will at least give the officer concerned an incentive to continue his loyal and devoted service in his new sphere of labour. The presentation which took place yesterday in the Parade Room at Warring- ton was of an altogether different character. After 26 years faithful service Inspector Cox is about to doff his uniform, and quit the scene of his long labours. His comrades of the Borough force in order to mark their respect and esteem for him, presented him with several handsome gifts. On his entering the shades of retirement, we join with the many in wishing Inspector Cox much happiness in the enjoyment of his well- earned rest and pension.</p>
