      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MR. FRANK H. CROSSLEY.</head>
        <p>MR. FRANK H. CROSSLEY.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to record the fact that our towns- 1 man Mr. Frank H. Crossley, the talented conductor , of the Warrington Musical Society,has received Ihe additional appointment of conductor to the Liver- pool Musical Society. The Society has already commenced preparations for the forthcoming con- cert season. The " Elijah "is to be the first work performed,and the first rehearsal for this was held lon Thursday in the Temperance Hall, Hardman-st. Mr. Crossley goes to Liverpool with excellent cre- dentials, both as a conductor and violinist. A re- ference to Mr. Crossley's career appears in the 1897 edition of the " British Musical Biography." Born in May 1864, Mr. Crossley studied the violin and pianoforte under private masters, and in 1880 went to Natal, South Africa,where he was appointed con- ductor of the Cathedral choir and of the Pieter- maritzburg Philharmonic Society. In 1887 he went to Berlin, and there studied the violin under Emile Sauret, and composition. with Wilhelm Tappert. Three years later he obtained the appointment of conductor of the Warrington Musical Society, a position which he still retains, as well as a similar one at Newton-le-Willows. Mr. Crossley has com- posed several vocal and instrumental works, in- cluding a cantata "Adele" for soli, chorus, and orchestra; a string quartet, a melody in D for violoncello and piano, and a number of songs. Under his able direction the Society should make great headway during the coming season. The , Society will also have the advantage of the experi- ence of Mr. Charles Collins, the well-known organist of Mossley-hill Church,who has just been appoiuted a member of the committee, and who is actively in- teresting himself in its work. The " Elijah " it is proposed to perform early in November, and the ' committee are strenuously endeavouring to secure the services of the veteran Charles Santley for the occasion.</p>
        <p>PATTISON AND PERKINS, 17 SANKE Y Sr., WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>OPBN TO-DAY,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, ISEPrEMBER I.oTll,</p>
