        <p>[BY A ConausPoNnENr4 The success of the Runcorn team at Wigan, is gratifying beyond expreisioa ; and is a matter Lir much congratulation, inascuuco as it shows that " volunteers are far better than p..essed men." Then, again, that 15 men (all local) should go forth backed up by only half-heartt.d support—tor almost everyone considered teat they would be beaten— and secure such a brilliant victory is an a:laievve- ment of§which few towns can boast.</p>
        <p>The man to whom belongs the credit of the vic- tory is the time-nouoared veteraz S. Hmghton, who played a vigorous, hard and useful defensive game. fie played as ,f all Runcorn were looking upon him and pnved what he always is, and may we hops always be. a credit t 3 the " wea, ets of the green.' Tnere was nit a man on the field that showed better form than " Sammy." two dropped goals w-rs both clevar performan-es and demonstrate that player's resource behind the pack.</p>
        <p>The rest of the team all played remarkably well, and deserve every credit for their gallant defence of Runcora's honours. True tae Wigan te was handicapped by having Walkthali (the centre tree- quarter) injured early ,n the game. But then,tima- corn, were Ilan licapped in being without several good raeu—who are now suffering foora wilv, might be called tr.e " hump," and have an idea that they cannot be dispensed with.</p>
        <p>Now, a new aspect is put upon affiirs, and proba- bly,provided that the committee of mairagemlut go in for " volunteers" and *stick to them, the " strikers" will be induced to coma to teeir Surely if Houghton is man enoigh to play tor hi ONVII cub, the other " smaller fry " should put their ,houlder to the whcel, especiiiiy during a crisis of this kind.</p>
        <p>On Saturd iy, the senior fifteen cd Runcorn, hive a rast from competition filo ,ball, and would do weli to spend the time in ettiu a litte mir 3 practice for the data they tack,e Salford at Runco,m on the 17th. Salford °pule 1 the sea ou in flue style, beat- ing Tyldesley by 13 points. Oa the whole,however, the Runcorn fifteen have nothing much to fear if they show the same spirit a 8 at Wigan.</p>
        <p>As the profeisioual element has now been admit- ted into tile Northern Unim,l submit the followitu deftaition of a pro''es-donal for tan bsnefit of t,lose of mi reldsrs wh may nave Illy doubts as to wow., a" Peatessional "ii the meanith., or rather intention of the Northern Union is :—(1) Ile is a per-ou who is pad: any sum above that whin he has „wally spent in travelling expensss, (2) and in addition he ' must bsin r ,gular emplopsent in socas trade, art, profession, or venture, ottier than, billiard marker, waiter in licensed premises. or any emplJyaieut, connected with any social club,</p>
        <p>It i vident that the:Northern Urlion is desirous that it members snail be true sportsmen,auci whil4t a positton as w,ll aaldleociwaiantgelythreeemmp to tohcecillif fyorsluoc,th.time, etc., they at the same time consider that no one should be in- duced tot tke up wnat is now genuine sport, as a means of livelihood.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SCIIOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of , this Board was held on Tuesday eve iiag. There Wr 3 present : Mr. James Pritohard (in the chair), the RiV. C tnon Wood, aev. a. B Gore, Messrs, E. Imison, C. A. Timmins, and J. W. Lightburn (cleik). TILE INSPECTOR'S REPORTS. The following reports of Ger Jetajoscy's Inspector were laid before tbe board : Brunswick and Granville S:r.oet, Boys Schoo; : The boys' school closes a bog career with a gooiS examination. English is the least satisfactory es imication. Girls' scaool 'Die girls are in good order, and do fairly wall In the elementary subjects. English goo I. Objet lessons and domestic economy fair onlv.—lafaits school : The infants school is doing a deal bA:er. Re id.ng is still fair only, and arithmetic is rather weak in the first class. The other elementary work is good. Obit lessons arc less successful.—Mill Brow: The -sohool hag recovered the lost ground, and now does vary well.—Victoria R oad : Boys' School: the ' boys are in eery goo I order, stud d very good work.— Gills schso" : Toe ails school is coi.ducted with much &amp;lei: en. Victoria Ra I Eireoing cc Boys' school: fats school coatioues to main!ain well its excellent charactsr.—Girls' school : Toils school continues to do remarkably good steady work; th 3 lessons given in "Science of common things" am particularly pleasing.</p>
        <p>REDUCTION OF STAFF. Ia consequence of the transolr of chair in from Gran- viile•md SMo )1 0 Victnis-road Sctiool tag tff the firmer scho,)l had t 3 be re laced. anl it we„s with regret that Min Whakes' services were displased with. IRSURANCR.</p>
        <p>It was resolvad b inareass •he insurance on the Victoria-rod Schools from £2,000 to £5,000, and ou the fittings from £350 to £l.OOO. A stasnswascort. The resignation of Ms i Eft so 1, of the Victoria-road Schools, Was acoepte 1. Tht Bo rd revises the fetchers salatias in accordacce sof h the scale.</p>
