      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RIINrjORN AND VirIDNES.</head>
        <p>RIINrjORN AND VirIDNES.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT NIIRSINO. There have been 2 cases on the books from Jaly 25th to August 21st, viz.—Nine method, twelve surgical,,lnd one typhoid. Of these two are convalescent, leaving 20 on the books. Twenty-s3ven patten:s have reosived occasional visits ; two of these have died during the month. Numbsr visitqy lid to olsss, 180; number of</p>
        <p>occasional visits 103; t )tal, 286. RUNCORN COTTAGE EIOSPITELL FUND. On Sunday afternoon the Widnes Subscription Prize Band gave their annual sacr.A. con3erton Runcora-hill in aid of the Runcorn C3ttage Hospital Fund. Ideal weather prevailed, and tae hill orea,nt imusaally animated appearance. The band gave an excellent per- formance, and a a-,0 I response was in de to the appeal , on behalf of the fund.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NATIONAL SCHOOL. The current issue of the Parish Magazine " contains the following :—"Tae success of the National 53M901 in all three depir;ments is m )st gratifying tot is managers and shoald cc so to the parishioners, firm repoit of Her Majesty's Insp ctor, lately receivcd,is rcally good. The grants,including that for the specific subject, are in all cases the highest obtainable, and the Head Maser and Head Mistress 38, together with their o tpahle assistant, are to be much congratu'ated." ACCIDENT TO A RUNCORN POLICE CONSTABLE. On Satu;day ait.-rnoon P.C. Wilson, who has been tnnsferred from the Macclesfield Division to Runcorn, was engaged unloading t me furnitare in Pe -St e 3t,when !a mangle tell off the harry aid knocked him down, in- ' Meting a deep cut on the head behind the I4t ea-. Wil- son was unconscious for some time, bat uader the t:.e it- ment of Dr. McDougall he soon recovered, and is now progressing favoaratiy. The wonder is that ttie olfizer escaped wi.hout broken hones.</p>
        <p>FIEIE AT RUNCORN%</p>
        <p>About 7-30 on Monday evening a lire was disnovered in a bedroom at the house of Mrs. Price, Halt m-road. Runcorn. An alarm was raised, and th .fie brigade were summoned. In the mrantime a number of neigh- bours and friends rendered timely assist ‘nce, and by tile time the brigade reached the Ap It the id imq had been extingtiisne.d. It appt ars that the Are originated through the gas in one of the bedrooms igniting the curtains. the sounding of the alarm attrac,,,, d a large crowd of people to the house and considerab a excitement pue- veiled until the public were assured that all danger nad been averted. Among those who were quickly on the scene were Inspector Hicks, Serg,Amt Gui and P.C. Gregory. SWIMAIINO CHAMPIONSHIP OF WIDNE3. The swirnmi,g champimship of Widnes W,15 contested for last Saturday attern.ion. The distance which WAti about half-a-a-Me, was from a mirk in the Slip Canal wall to a malt ok.mDsite the R iyal Brit mina Elute'. Mr. Fair Turner started, and Councillor Garrard offiiated as judge. The competitors wer ,—Messrs. E. J. Richards, W. Matthew, P. Weave, J. Mckenzie, E. Dickson, and R. Tonkies. The race, which proved itself a fine struggle, eventually resulted in Peter Glaave winning .by three yards, the same distance being le?t ween R. Tontines, sec(rid, and E. Dickinson, third. The first prize whcn was a hands Me gold medal, the see nal and third being silver with gold centres, sunplied by Messrs. E and Co., Sankey-street, WArriagtoa, ware subscribA for by the tradesmen of Widurs.</p>
        <p>THE ALLSOED THEFT OF Becox. At the Runcorn Police Coact, on Friday, before Mr. Walter Timmimi, Frederick Coen, a wet-kilo lari coaral- ter, was charged with stealing 2 b 3. of bacon from tho shop of Edwin Gregory, Provision dealer, Bridge-street, on the Ist S.qat mbar. From the evidence of the pro- secutor it apoeared that on Toursday morning the prisoner waikad into his shop and picked up a piece of bacon from the counter,about 21bs weight,awl was about to walk out with it when Mr. Gregory stopped Min at the boat Joan The prisoner ordered him to clear our, and said he would" squash "a dozan talon like him. Prisoner eventually got a Kay with the bacm, and was apprehended near tire Police S:ation by Sergeant Co9per. —Superintendant Okell said the prisoner had been drink ing for a cons.darable time, and it was a question whether he was responbie for his actions. He asked that he should ba remanded for a week in order that the pion dlctor might have him under observation.— Prisoner was accordingly remanded for a week. ST. MICHAEL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN. -</p>
        <p>The Vicar writes as follows in the current number of • the " Parish Magazine ":—" We lost a friend last month who had made himself much likd and respect sil amongst no '• I moan Mr. Moyle. It is not ton much to say that St. Michael's congregation,their schools,cl asses, and associations will miss him greatly—porhaps " con- scieet "is the word wh'ch may describe his work amongst U 9. We all felt that he would always do, wed could be ted,ted to do,the best he could without sparing tranself,atad guide mn le sve their mark wherever they go. We all trust that at Horsham he will find oppor- tunity for his own and his people's advancement in all good and holy things. For the present, and perhaps for some time, St. Michael's will be.so to speak, in commis- sion. No one will be in charge bit myself. Mr. Oliver is kind enough to be responsible for the district, and certain other matters, but for any settleinent,oth=r than a temporary one, it will not lee wise to look just yet." THEFT BY A DITTON DOMESTIC SERVANT. On Monday, at Widnes Police Court, before Alderman W. W. Gossage and S. Sadler, Edith Platt, 21, domestic servant, was charged with having stolen a ring and a tablecloth, valued at 255., the property of Mrs. Green, Ditchfield-road, Ditton. It appeared from the evidence that the prisoner, who had been apprehended at Gres- ington, where she was in another situation, by Police • constable Thornton, was employed by Mrs. Green, for five weeks. She left on Sunday. August 20th, and after she had gone the ring, tablecloth, and o her articles were missed. Information was given to the police, who found the ring pledged at Mr. G. I. Neil's,, and that the tablecloth had been sold to another woman. Prisoner, in tears, expressed her contriti in, and promised not to steal again. She was remanded for inquiries to be made as to other articles found in her possession. Yesterday she was sentenced to two months' hard labour. RUNCORN HEATS RECREATION GROUND. On Wednesday evening, in the Council Chamber of Runcorn Town Hall, a meeting of persons interested in, Runcorn Heath and Runcorn aid, was hell for the pur- pose of appoini ing a v Auer under the provisional order issued by the Board of Apiculture. There were present the Rev. Canon Wood, in r,spect of g'eee land, Messrs. W. Fisher, representing Cawley. trustees and himself, Arthur Salk:ill, representing various land owners, F. A. Lake, on behalf of the 'Deb so Council, W. Handlay, db- trict councillor, and James Wilding (surveyor).—On the proposition of Mr. Lake. seconded by Mr. Fisher, Canon Wood was elected chairman. The Chairman moved, Mr. Fidler seconded and Mr. Salkeld supported, that Mr. Wilding be appointed valuer under the Enclosure Act,' of the strip of common land which has beet given to the Marquis of Cholmondeley in exchange for him relinquish- ing his rights in Rune orn Heath. Tins motion was car- ried unanimously, and the Chairman baying signed the statutory records the meeting concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN COTTAGE HOSPITAL FUND</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COTTAGE HOSPITAL FUND</p>
        <p>SWIMMING GALA.</p>
        <p>A further effort was made to increase the funds of the Runcorn Cottage Hospital Fund on Wednesday evening when a swimming gala was held in the Big Pool, by per- mission of the Ship Canal Company. The affair had been organised by" Professor "H. Finney, described as the Champion Midget Swimmer and high diver of the world.who is the brother of the celebrated James Finney. Fully 3,000 persons surrounded the pool, and while the interesting pro3eedings were in progress they were iris portuned by collectors for contributions to the Cottage Hospital Fund. The Runcorn Volunteer Band,undr r the leadership of Bandmaster Outhwaite,p!ayed eclectic): 8 of music and Mr. C. Harris's serenaders gave admirable vocal entertainments at intervals. The first event was a swimming, handicap for youths under 16 yeare,and re suited : let, N. Wilkinson ; 2nd, W. Weston ; 3rd, F. Fishwick. In the sixty yards race the winner was H. Farmer, 2nd, A. Hull ; 3rd, J. Weston. Hull C3l-119 in first but was disquali- fied for an infringement which he rectified and then suc3eeded in coming in second. Fer the 440 yards race B. W. Moreweol, Widnes, won the first prize and his brother T. W. Morewood, tbe second. These were the only competitors.J.Faulkner succeeded in win- ning the first prize for walking the greasy pole, A. Ellis capturing the second prize. The latter was more suc- cessful in the duck hunt, in which he was awarded firs prize, H. Bentham coming in second. An interes'i.ng item in the proceedings was the exhibition of scientific and ornamental swimming by Professor Finney. The list of feats included undressing under water, illustra- tions of various kinds of strokes, swimming without using arms, swimming like a porpoise, the water wheel, spinning like a top, marching on the water, rnotionlees floating, &amp;c. Re also gave an imitation of how .his brother succeeded in performing his great feat of swim- ming from Blackpool to St. Annes, a few days ago. Unfortunately darkness came on before the proceedings w. re half finished but this had been provided for by erecting lucigen lamps at certain prominent points and they well illuminated the pool. Prizes were offered to the collectors and these were won as follows : —Ladies : let, Miss Flaepell ;2nd, Mrs. Outhwaite and Miss Millinger a tie. Gentlemen : Ist. C. Minis ; 2nd, w. H. Malings. Proe,ramme sellers : Ist, Miss Oath- waite ; 2nd, Harry Sewett. A prize was also awarded Miss A. Alice. Guessing competi.ion : Ist, L. Price ; 2nd, T. Truss. The proceedings were efficiently carried out by the following officials :—Timek-eper, Mr. G. I Whinyates; judges, Messrs. J. corns and T. C. Williams; handicapper and starter, Professor H. Finney.</p>
