      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>LECTURE ON MEADESLSOEIN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>LECTURE ON MEADESLSOEIN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CROIR ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>-•- St. Paul's Weeleyan Church, Runcorn, he'd their choir choir, wnich fully annivereary Sanday,witen tee Rev. EL H. mnedti on its efforts. t 13 B. a., of Woolton,preaclied special sermons. Appropriate was the anth- in, 0r meritedn musicaling it tisleirlevs oweeoi, car teihareale nipdbr,eirrti ted,i)pwb,,y7dittutil "Ascribe tint) tie Lord, including a quartette by th i,ses Jones and Crosby sn I Messrs. Pilgrim and Povaii. iaely appreciated by the congregation. At the evening Tch,Eis Burrows, and Harrison • ser'vice the tnusic included the trio " Lifatntial easeiv:sl; iPjia'her:),rbeey'iMvisdseasdTm,lyr:Critroesba7a7dt'Sienkin ; touble mlCAseferd), wtte a -1.:1 .11133, and "S.vell tile guar e;te, "'Dieu wilt keep hun," (Williams), by Ms .faylor and Parry. Borrows, Miss, s M. and J. Cr shy and Siinkin, and daN eaj ete„t anthem,reb m, y. , Miss a,), he, Wit and h Mc. b ymmo s s with Theee efforts were of high met it, aud. it would be iuvidi- ous to single anyone out for CUStilleClo,Cl. The o:gan was skilfully manipulated by Mr. W. H. lime. On Monday evening the Rev. H. H. McCullagh de- livered a leci.ure in toe church on "Mendelssohu : his lad and works." Prior to the lecture there was a short organ recital by Mr. Birkett Musgrove, A.R.G.0., the resided, and there was a good attend- selections being t aken from Mendelasolni's works. Mr. The Lecturer said there had been great musicians who George Bellamy p had been at any rate celebrated for intellectual power, and there had been those who were. musicians and rat, thing more. They hed.been something of a kind of me- dium for the transmission of music to their fellow crea- tures. Bet Mendelssohe was vary much more than that. It was true he was not a literary eriaa,but he might have become a literary man, as was evidanced by the beauti- ful lines which he had left behind him. His letters formed the most deliget'ul reading, and if there were any young people present who were interested in music, he could advise them that they could not do better, if they wished tor something light, bright, and interesting in musical literature, than to read these letters of Mendeissolen. He was not an ar- tist,but he might have become an artist. He bad illus- trated those letters by some beautifal pictures of bright hand painting, and from the beginning of his career to the very last weeks of his life he was interested in art as a painter. Some of his greatest friends were artists. He was not a politician, but he (the lecturer) believed he might have .become a politician. He was very much interested in politics,and as a fore,gn- er he was interested in English pontics. He was in- tereeted IEI the Catholic Emancipation Movement. He also greatly, enjoyed hearing O'Connell pleat, his case before we bar of the English House of Commons. With regard to his character, now beautiful it was, how pure, how affectionate, how domestic, how amiable. People delighted in him for hi s own sake, and net only for the sake of his music. He was sought not for the sake of his inscrurnent, or for his playing, but for the sake of his iii the year 1809. Three very distinguished poets own pereenality and character. He %sae born at ham- burg were born in that year. 'rue great Tennyson was born in that year, and what he might call the great poet- statesman Gladstone, and the great poet Mendelssehn. As a little boy of seven years of. age, Mendolesohn weat to Paris—in 1816—for a short visit. Ia 1820 he coin- meocel seriously to compose. In the yeer 1829 he commenced his travels in England and on toe Continerat, which were part of his education. These travels continued till the year 1831. In the year 1835 he settled in Leipzig. Two years later he married, being 28 years of age. In the year 1846 the " Elijah " was produced, and in 1847 he died, at the early age of 38 years. The lecturer weht on to describe the composer's c tracer at some leiagth,end related many interesting ineidents of his life. At inter- vals excerpts from Mendelseohn'e works were given by the Misses McCullagh daughters of the lecturer, whose skilful interpretation of the works of MendelesOhn platrifort -) " The Rivulet," Ml,s McCullagh ; evoked the warmest applause. These items included— violoncello and pianoforte, " Romance Sans dS;au''r:otleffsoCirr" Miss Mary ly.McCullago and Miss McCullagh; trio in C Minor for pianoforte, violin and violoncello,the Misses mccob i agh; Concerto n E Minor, for victim, Miss Isabel McCullagh; organ sonata No. 4 Mr. BirleA: Mus- grove; cheruses "flow lovely are the messengers " ("St. Paul"), and" Be not afraid" (" Elijah "), by the choir. Tee choir's best efforts was " How lovely are the messengers," and Mr. Beckett Musgrove on the organ was heard to the best advantage in the organ sonata. The duties of conductor were ably undertaken by Mr. B.Cresby. A collection was taken in aid of the choir funds,and subsequently a very hear:4, vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer, tee performers, and the Ch lir- manfor traeir services. The meeting terrniaat id with the Doxology and Benediction.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING DISCOVERY AT DYMM. Information has been given to the police at Lymm that the body of a woman has been found in the Bridgewater Canal at CornAl y Bridge. The body was discovered on Friday.aitsrnoon, and was in a fearfully Mangled condition, evidently caused by the screw of a steamer. Though it was evident that the body had not been in the water many hours the left leg identified arm were missing. The re- mains have been as those of Ann thirst Battersby,a single weman,formerly living at Orrish Meres,Harborough-road,Aehtou-on-Mersey, She had been in a weak state of health, and on Tuesday last was missed from her home. Information was given to the police and dragging operations were carried out in the canal. The deceased was between 45 and 50 years of age. Nothing had been seen of her from Tuesday night until her mutilated body was found in the canal at Lymm, and how she got into the water is a mystery. The body had no doubt been injured by passing steamers and dragged down the canal for some distance. On Monday evening a leg of the unfortunate woman was ._found in the anal at Norton, near itunc.iru.</p>
        <p>The Danish Government hag decided to accept the Czar's invitation to a Peace Coalerence,which it is under- stood the Rus4ian Government desire should be held at the cud of the present or the beginning of neat year. •</p>
