      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>TEIE ItAJLWA.Y FITA.LITY AT WIDNES:</head>
        <p>TEIE ItAJLWA.Y FITA.LITY AT WIDNES:</p>
        <p>INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Le,t Fr day actern ocia an inquiry into the circunn. stances attending the death of John Davey, upholsterer, 167, Islington, Liverpool, was held by Mr. S. Brighouse at the Widuee Ac d eat Hospital.—Mr. H ore appeared on bele ill of the relatives of the deceased, and Mr. dime- son represtentei the Great Central Railway Company.— Jan e Devey, wife of the deceased, said he was 49 years of are. Tnere was nothing that would cease him to take his hi ,as he hide comforeabla home and pleaty of busi- ness.—WilliamNewnes,l6,Forshaw-st.,Warrington.stated that hi WAS in the employ of he Cheshire Lines Railway. Re was wo:king at Rouge Green stetim. ane the driver lof a coal train was coming dawn in the direction of Liverpool. He told them that a man had been knocked down on the other side of the bridge, Witness went to the place t 3 render first aid, being a member of the am- bulance corps. The deceased was lying clear of all lines,about 159 yards on the opposite side of the bridge from the station. W.tness fancied the deceased had been knocked in that p.s.tion.The engine might possibly have caught his ri4ht thigh and knocked him over on the siee. Dece teed was living when witness got there. Hie riels leg was bleeding, and witness eedeavoured to staunch it, obtaining neckties and scarves for bandages. Wit- ness then got a cabin door, and conveyed the deceased to the" Volunteer." He WAS subsequently tak ea to the 'hospital, witness accompanying Senpson, in I reply to the Ceroner, said he had inquired from the drivers of trains, but 'hay did not know anything about I the affair. The last trim that passed over the r oad was ' the Midland Express from Stockport to Liv:rpoel at 1.30, which went at the rate o' BO m les per hour. This engine was carefully examined, but noleing could be found. The driver and fireman were alio closely cries- timed, but they said they never saw him —Mr. Bore said the deceased was an abnormdlly broad man, and perhaps if he was on the narrow line be might have been eaueht by an engine.—Miss Paee,a 1311,59 at the hospiree deposed to the decees.d beiag a irnitted into the iastitu- tiou at ten minutes pest two on Taesd ay afterneon. He died at five minutes past three the sane afternoon. He was conscious until within five minutes of the time or his death, but he made no st itement. His injuries consisted of c =pollee fr melee; to the upper part of the right leg and the left arm. His right leg was reduced to a pulp. there were also injuries ' to his chest, and a sli flit sat p w .nod. the Cor 'leer said Newnes, the last witness, had prepared a report abed t the affair. It stated that the de :tor' asked the nurse if it was a Cisc of suicide, and the de- ceased said " No, no, no." Wen tee nurse asked the deceased what he was doing on the railway he replied that he was doing something for hicasel : —Miss Page said that the latter part was correct, but regarding tee doctor's question, the deceased did not say it was not a case of suicide in her presente.—Newaes was t lee re• called, and he stated that the doctor ask,d the question, but witness would not say that he asked the nurse.— Replying to Mr. Hors, Miss Page said.the deceased was conscious. Be did not make any pious ejaculations be• fore he died.—P.C. Stubbs stated teat he had made eareful inquiries in the neighbourhood about the accie dent. Be hsard that a man had seen the deceased killed but upon witness calling upon him he denied having seen anything.—The Coroner, reviewing the else, re- marked that the only thing that was proved wis that he had evidently been injured by some passing train but ' under wltet circumstances there was not the slightest evidence to show. It was most remarkable he would admit, as the widow said the deceased was in good cle- ment inceS, that he had a happy home, and no trouble or sorrow, either domestic er pecuniary. Toe only evi. deece was that the deceased was killed on the line. They ' must judge for tkeinsclves,as he was only there to direct them on the points of law. The jury returned an open verdict.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>-- – THE RECENT DROWNING FATALITIES,</head>
        <p>-- - THE RECENT DROWNING FATALITIES,</p>
        <p>REFERENCE AT RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH. During his sermon at Runcorn Parisl Church, on Sun- day morning, the Rev. W. d. Stables (seoior curate) re- ferred t o the untimely end of the boys Williamson and , Owen, who were drewned und or circumstances which, by this time, are familiar to our readers. He said al- though their sympathy was equally shared by the par- ents and friends of both lade, yet, naturally, their thoughts were more closely identified wen the unfortun- ate a :cident which happened in the vicinity of that church. It seemed to him that as yester Jay. just as he finished service in the church, be heard a scream and a shout—a scream, he believed, of the boy who survived— and on running dawn as quickly as he could to the water's edge. there was tolthing to be :teen but the calm still Water. He believed, and surely it :mist be a com- fort to the friends to know it, that every hing possible for mortal man to do was done, but it was useless. On Thursday last the remains of both lads were committed to the grave—the bodies, but not the souls. They must never forget that it was only the body that was buried. He (the preacher) had had some experience of ' lads, and though,of course,it was especially sad to see a funeral of a young land, yet to him there was always a sort of greater hope or py in taking this funeral of a lad than that of an elder parson. Many good elder p .rsons departed this life, hu.La the caste or children they c ou'd not help thinking, they knew it in a great part to be true, that while there was in them a love of play, and all that was frolicsome and merry, they did not readily forget the religion they were taught at their m other's knee or in school. Yes, they liked, as they stood by the grave side, to think of these children as gone to Him who said "duffer the little children to come usto me, and forbid them not," ant 53 they left them both to the tender mercy of God. The ne nub ore of the Church Lads' Brigad% to which the deceased boys Were attached, were present at tse , service, under the direction of Lieutenant Smith and Sergeant Hill.</p>
        <p>A -DRUNKEN BRAWL AT 11017G11T05 GREEN. At the Occasional Court, at Warrington on Tees- nay, Edward Walsh, a farm labourer, was charged with inflicting serious bodily harm on John Hooley on Sunday night. Sergeant Brindle said he appre- hended the prisoner at one o'clock that morning in the Paddy-house on the farm on which he is employed at Houghton Green. He was taken before Hooley at the infirmary, and was there identified as his assailant. Hooley, who is suffering from a frac- tured jaw and cut lip, is detained in the infirmary. —Prisoner was remanded until Friday. DARNSBURY Scnoor. BOARD. On Thursday last week the monthly meeting of this Bo3rd was held in Daresbury S&amp;ools. There were present the Rev. H. T. Clarke (chairman), MO P. Tickle (vice-chairman), Captain Roger, and Messrs. Broom, Philips, Cooper, and C. E. Linaker (clerk). A further letter from Miss Fairclough, mistress of the Preston Brook School, in connection with her re,ignation was read. A letter from Miss Green, of Warrington, applying for the situation was also read. Captain licriger moved, and Mr Tickle seconded,that subject to the approval of the Education Department Miss Green be appointed mistress of Hatton School at a salary of £45 per annum, with an increase in twelve months of £5 it her services were satisfactory, in v4ich case Mrs. Wells would be transferred to Preston Brook School. The motion was carried. The report of the above school showed that useful and good work was being done. The school had obtained the highest grant. Captain Rodger stated that all the teachers who got the full grant ought to be complimented by the Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>NOTES ON. GIMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON. GIMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CYCLING. There have been a 3 litany rumours abut the 1899 pattern of Dunlop tyre as if it had been a State secret, out at last, to refute the error and satisfy curiosity, authentic particulars are now being revealedin appear- ance the roadster tyre is seen at a glance to be corru- gated, as before, with the Dunlop-Welch non-slipping device,but the pattern has been reproduced on a much smaller scale, tne undulations cansiethag of many more but much shorter waves, in a narrower strip covering the head only of the cover, and at each edge of this cen- tral strip are longtitudinal flutings, such as are already familiar on the Palmer and other tyras. Another feature ncticeable tl the eye at once is the increased depth of the rim and its reduc:d width, which combine to make the tyre apparently stand away from the rim more than was formerly the case.</p>
        <p>But the greatest change is one that is nat visible, yet will make itself felt at once when oae has to detach and re-attach the cover. The single staut wire concealed in each cdge of the cover of old is now replaced by a single thin wir which encircles the rim thrice. There are not three wires, but the effective strength of three wire; with only one jain, Cage her with a great increase of flexibility, as the three portions of wire at any one point readily move upon one another when manipulated, thus I rendering attachment aud detachmant a simple and painless operation that needs no tool and CIUSB9 no dam- age to o 3e's knuckles or one's temper. The threefold wire lies with its several lengths side by side bind-like, when the tyre is inflated &amp;ad the ecli;es of the rim are now made of elliptieal instead of ercular section, in order to afford the better bearing surface for this broad- ened edge of the cover.</p>
        <p>For racing tyres the fabric only is used, with a tread of rubber so thin as to be scarcely perceptible, and a road-racing tyre is made with a tread ot sufficient wear- ing substance for Belt work for a short time, while the walls of the tyre are left uncovered save by toe tbion,sc possible skin of rubber, scarcely thicker than a coat of varnish, and designed solely to prote3t the fabric from wet and not to sustain any wear.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. Once name does to Abell reign sapretne among the sports in the land, and I. for one,, am not inclia,cl to regret the fact atall. Crick t has grown quite scale, especially with the exmhampicms corning so many heavy croppers, and making their supporters feel highty disgusted. and a Itcle aanamel to confess that they had either a birth qu all ficat ion or any otht:m remote con- nection with the county p alatine. But bygones should be bygows, and the one topic now is football, and in Warrington, Rugby football. Fora month or more en- thusiasts have been discussing the doings and possible dee js of the men being licked into shape in the hands of trainer Redo iffe. Rumours have gine the rounds about strange facas being seen on the field, but I fear the owners of these faces must have been visitants from another vrorld—they have certainly not mtt wiairsad ire , this. The Warrington players are still all local lads,and much credit to .'eaa it tley can win matches, to-day's especially. •</p>
        <p>There is soma likelihoed of this season being an alto- gether exceptional °tie in Warrington: It starts with an unprecedented bang and splutter. To hive Swinton and Oldham cavorting about the Wild3rspeol ground on successive Saturdays-3mi those Saturdays the fist two on which football may be p'ayed,is an event which has rais3d football enthusiam to boiling point. We all felt coccumaily anxious for the first tussle with its attend- ant excitement ; but the prospect of seeing the chem. pioas,and runners up to °eminence with was really over- whelming. Saturdar was a fine day too. After noon ' the sun's refulgent rays penetrated the thick cloud of smoke and apprehension which overhung Warrington, and the intending spectat era— hese of them who had no sympathy with the players—rejeiced greatly thereat. There were some woo even foil, a pleasant thrill when they once again a w the o/d flag floating in the breeze, with the mystic letters W. F. (No C.) thereon. The abience of that C. lent an additional charm. I myself ' had watched it gradually vanish into thin air during last se isentand teok a sort of proprietary interest in it. There is not the least probabiiity that a hatef al, brand re w streamer will be hoisted up. Oh no! we know the Football Club too well te imagine that. The present rag will flutter aloft there until there's nothing left to flutter, and even theta its wraith will be heisted up with the same regularity, every Saturday.</p>
        <p>--- About 7,000 p soplo wended thor way to. Wiliertpool, and on arrival at the enclosure sorted theuoselves out to the 'vet of their ability. S.ane went one way and some anolhsr The jog :ging with tie stinds, prices, etc., has been rather -o .f using. The aristocrats •(hon mem- bers and offisials) have men traasfarred to the ori;inal " sixpsnoy " si le, the plutocrats sent bask to the old " threepenny "—Ili" " fourpenny " side, eni the press left in the lurch. As far as I know, the Warring- ton Club is the only one in the Northern Union,—ar any other Union— to relezate the memb.rs of the Press to the cheap side of the enclosure</p>
        <p>The borne ;trt in tlreir attra3tive prinir ese and blue jerseys, received a hearty welcoming cheer on being .el into the ft •11 by Vie ever-green Smack " Bate. A reception no lees cordial greeted Valentine and his merry men—who by the way were regretting the absence of one Rees, of Swansea, this player, as aptly remarks, having gone home to change his collar, or something of the sort. Bevan filled the vacancy, so that with the exception of Barber, still absent from the VVarrington rinks, two strong and repre entative teuns faced each other. Swinton undoub'elly had weight in their f tvour,but as events showed, it will take more than that to en stele them to lewer the Warrington colours at WildersPool.</p>
        <p>--- From the kick off by Taylor, will faced the sun and wind, it was seen that the visitors were not going to have their own way, notwithstanding the fact that one or two of the home players were novices in first-class , football. Boardman (forward) J. Holcroft (left wing) I made their debut. The constant practice of the War- ringtoniths seemed to have remo red all the stiffness usually apparent after a season's rest, and the activity and energy they introduced into the game consi terably upset the Lions. "Smack" and his brother had nearly all the chances bahind the scrimmaged, and right wall did they use them. Beyond an occasional burst,Swinton was never dangerous during the first quarter. Valentine, in particular was wed' watched, and the visitors back play get little chance of showing its effectiven-sa.Before fifteen minutes had elapsed Holcraft put in a well- judged centre, and the home forwatds t Arnig the ball LIP, the Swinton line was reached in " no time " and R. Bate scored the fi,st try of the season for Warring- on, amidst great cheering. Holcraft did his beat at the goal-kick from a difficult angle, but was unsuccessful. Faris reverse had the effect of rousing the Lions to greater exertions. Fine play by Badger should have re- sulted in something tangib'e on two octasions,but Bevan knocked on at the critical mnment. Combining better in the forward rank, Swinton obtained better opportunities,and eventually a swaiging boot of passes all along the line resulted in Bevan romping over. Bos- cow and Moores both had a try at holding him, bat it was no use, the speedy little Welshman was destined to score. Valentine's kick at goal met with the same fate as Holeroft's, and half-time arrived with honours even.</p>
        <p>The home crowd considered themselves entit'el to some satisfaction at this state of affairs. Warrington's performance against the wind and sun was decidedly enc =aging. When the gams was resumed, anticipa- tions appeared likely ta be roalisad, lor the Swinton line was endangered immediately. Naylor got over with the ball, but was recalled for some irrNu'arity, and the spectators reserved their cheer for the next occasion. The next item of note was a scarred skull for Messer, which neceihitated quite ten minutes stoppage. Lasteid of this being a misfortune for Swinton it was the reverse, for it gave their heavy forwards a breather which they plainly needed. From this point the Lions hal as much of it as the wire-p alters, and the game to the close was and ave tnheascopmosblaleti.ta No rt tfhur3tfiheelrd credited took Place, with a point each. But for this lengthy stoppage I think it is certain that the Swinton defence would have collapsed ber.ore the onslaught of their harder,and lighter fonnen.</p>
        <p>The players on the home side, individually and collec- tively acquitted themselves remarkab'y well. There was not a mistake made the whole of the a'ternoon,and all displayed a dash which spoke well for their training. The three-quirter line as an attacking instrument was, as it has been for a couple of seaons, rather weak, though the quartette defended superbly. Holcroft made a good beginning. It is hardly nnessary to accord praise to the two Bates, who were well-nigh perfect, but am ing a nee set of forwards it was possible to pick out Naylor, Taylor, Boardman, and Falrourst as the most prominent. Boscow was as safe at full-back as ever in lois life, though he kicked none too strongly.</p>
        <p>The team as a whlle, struck me as big a capable combination, rather smarter than laqt year, but still de- ficient in speed among the three-quarters. Some new blood is clearly required here.</p>
        <p>S*raton appeared less formidab'e than hat year. The three.quarter line was good at times, but the pass. in machinery was unmistakably rusty from disuse. t; At half-back they are well represented, the forwards are a fine lot, and Goodman does yeoman service behind, but there was an absence of last senon's brilliance. Valentine, himself, played only a moderate game, and was never particularly dangerous. CI C LI S T S USE PILLAR BRAND. Lubricating and Burning Oils and Cycle Enamel, Buainellette.and Brunswick Black. From all Dealers. AGittcrs : McLICAN AND CO., 13, SSIL-STREET, LIVERPOOL.</p>
