        <p>THE RISING IN CREfE. BRUISE TROOPS ENGAGED. ONE HUNDRED KELLED AND WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>CANDIA, WEDNESDAY. The bombardment of H'r tklei on was re.maited this morning. The st:e ts of the town present a terrible sight. There are numbers of deal bod;e4 in mo3t of the thoroughfares. M. lialckaironos, the British Vice-Consul, has been murdered by the rioters. rhl Consulate was fired and he perished in ihe fl tmcs. The fighting in the streets has been serious, and the cu nber of British killed and wounded is stated to be nearly 103. A consi- derable portion of the town is destroyed by the fire. The Camperdowa took pert in the bombardment to-day. A large force of seamen and marines was landed from the British men-of-war this afternoon. They were attacked bv the Muisulenen rites and a serious en- gagement commenced. rue ruilrish troops took part. It is rumoured that the Turks have passed the cordon. CANEA, THURSDAY. The firing of the British warships at Candia vestirday was most precise, and did an .norm Is atnount of dam- age. The Mussulman quarer of the town was almost entirely destriyed, and idafo.t lamely several Christians were killed by the shells.</p>
        <p>THE HEAT WIVE IN LONDON-1 RECORD.</p>
        <p>In London at noon Fest vdav the tmperature was 91 degrees in the shade. and 125 degrees in the sun. Tins is the rederd for the year,the uottest day previously being Au ;nit 22, when the thema miter marked 35 degrees in tip shade. THE SIRDI.It'S VICTORY. OFFICIAL ESTIMA rE OF DERVISH LOSSES. 11,000 KILLED AND 16,0)0 WOUNDED. The latest news from the frJat is contained in a des- patch Irm tan Sirdar datsd Mouday,F,,,iving an estimate of the Dervish losses. 'ice number of killed is placed at over 11,000, and the injured at 16.000. One hundred camels sshiM the Khania id arranged to mnunt him for his nigh:, have been captured. It has tran,pired that the day bsfire the great natio the native levies, corn- m,n led by Majnr Smart-Wortle7, engaged a thousand Dervishrss, killing a third of the number. Over five hun.ired krabs, mounted on camels, were des;satch d after the Khalifa, He is reo:-Ttel to be moving with much speed that some of Li 3 wives have been dropped on toe road. THE WIGA.N CASE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Robinson was committed for tral on the charge of having procur3d certain noxious drugs for an unlawful purpose. ANOTHER RAILWAY ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>A. railway. earrl32e ran off the line at Exeter yester- day. Sareral passengers were shaken but not seriously iojured.</p>
        <p>ELECTRICITY WORKS DESTROYED BY FIRE.</p>
