        <head>WEATHER FORE'A.ST,</head>
        <p>WEATHER FORE'A.ST,</p>
        <p>Tne weather report for the North West of England up to noon to-day is as follows :—Southerly and south- westerly winds, light ; changeable ; some showers</p>
        <p>- thunder locally'. - A DERBY TRAGEDY. A woman named Emily Elizi RowbAtotn died at Alentown,nesr Darby, yesterday, from the effects of ars illegal operation. A married woman named Ruth Jane Talbot, of Derby, was remanded by the county magis. traces on Wednesday, charged with committing the offence, and the charge will now be altered to one of murder. Toe inquest on the body was formally opened last evening. THE FERGITNA REVOLT.</p>
        <p>TASHICRND, _THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>By an act of clemency the Czar has reduced from a million to three hundred thousani roubles the contribu- tion to be imposed upon the population of the Ferguna territory as a penalty af the recent revolt in that pre- vince. AfTEUPT TO WRECK AN EXPRESS. A DIABOLICAL SCHEME. The Press Association's Northampton correspondent telegraphs:—A dastardly attampt to wreck the Birming- ham express was made near Northampton on Wednesday night. An iron railway ch lir and fish plates were fastened to a rail on woich the train passed, but fortun- ately the speed at which the train was going causcd the obstruction to be forced out of the way and the train kept the rails. Bad it been otherwise, a terrible disaster must have resulted. Tae police have the ma:ter in hand bet they are very reticent, THE DUICEI QUEEN,</p>
        <p>AMSTERDAM, THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>This morning Queen Wilhelmina, accomp'amed by the Queen-mothertopeneu two great arc exbibitions,organieed in honour of the coronation.</p>
        <p>SWIMMING ACROSS TIIE CEfANNELt</p>
        <p>Frank Holmes started at 9.40 yesterday morning to swim across the Channel from Dover. There was a very hot sun and the temperature of the water was very high. When about five miles off the English coast, which is about quarter of the way across channel. Holmes was doing woaderlully well ia the water,and as fresh as when he plunged into the sea a little before ten in the morning. Up to the present he has shown every indicatioa of being a very powerful swimmer, and he has just the physique for this stupendous undertaking. He has also in his Lavour the extremely fine weather. Holmes has kept to the breast stroke ever since he started, maintaining one regular movement. A Central News telegram says :—Elolmes made steady progress for five miles, and wts then going strong and well. He went along some distance with closed eyes, and affirmed he had been asleep for twenty minutes. ile expressed nimself confident of performing his task.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED SIICIDE THROUGH A LOVER'S QUARREL. At St. Helens Police Court on Wednesday, Mary Rooney, 20 years of age, of 67, Bold-streot, was charged with attempting to commit suicide by jumping into the canal on the previous night. She was rescued, and on being arrested by Constable Wilkes she said she had been keeping company with a young man whom she had not seen for a fortnight until Tut slay night. They had a few words,and she told her be would not see him again for a fortnight. She suspected that he had another girl, I mad she jumped into the canal to drown herself. She was tired of living, and her parents 114 refused to allow her to marry him. The girl, the Cuief Constable added, was now sorry for what she had done. Sue was dis- charged with a caution.</p>
