        <head>SYMBOL OF STRENGTH.</head>
        <p>THE SYMBOL OF STRENGTH.</p>
        <p>Britannia is the symbol of strength at home and abroad, and whether upon the ocean wave or in the serried rants of her brave sons in Egypt, India,or China the fame of our country is always identified with power of will and strength of nerve—the highest state of cour- age that makes heroes. Science is the handmaid that helps ts maintain this standard of supremacy, and the great gifts of nature are concentrated for the use of men in Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, a tonic to keep every faculty at its brightest and its best. Mighty is the power of the spade and those who wield it are the pioneers of our greatness. The fearless miner burrowing in the earth and the strong navvy removing mountains are the very sinews of the nation. These men must always keep their strength at full stretch. Lsbiur of the severest is their lot and full health is necessary in accomplishing their daily tasks, and if they are attacked with Indiges- tion, Liver Disorders, or Pains in the Back, they know they can find relief by taking Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. The hard working toilers, generally, of the United Kingdom, should remember the old saying, "Prevention is better than cure," and that just as it is nece,sary to call in a medical man, when brain and body are overtaxed, so s it desirable to do all that is possible to keep the system thordughly up to the mark, for every rush of Dompetition and extra labour. You have a regular doctor, have you a regular preservative of health to save you from the doctor and the doctor's bills ? If not, try Owilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Great Tonic Pre- ventative against Serious Illness. The distressing worries of a family household lead to the greatest suffer- ing—the nervous system being specially strained in the turmoil, and Neuralgia, Nervousness, and Debility, with their attendant miseries are the most frequent torments that women in particular have to endure, Low spirits and Depression often render housewives quite unfit for their onerous duties and to prevent this condition Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is strongly recom- mended. Don't delay if you feel out of sorts, but get Gwilym Evans' Quieine Bitters,a purely vegetable tonic, which contains the strengthening qualities of Sarsa- sparilia, Saffron, Burdock, Gentian, Lavender, and andelion, as well as Quinine. The exact remedy you want for Palpitation, Sleeplessness, Biliousness, or Rheu- matism. It not only gives you freedom from pain but strength, and zest for your work, that makes it a pleasure. This perfection of medicinal preparations is advocated as a first aid towards quickening commercial instincts for the counting-house or the market-place, it e a Tonic that is sure to be wanted. This world- renowned remedy is sold in Bottles at 2s. 9d. and 4s. 63. each. Beware ef Imitations. See that you get" Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters " with the name " Gwilym Evans' on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. This is important. Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters Manufactnring Com- pany, Limited. Lianellv, South Wales.</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A MOTHER'S NEGLECT.-CENSIIRED BY THE MAYOII..</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Friday before the Mayor (in the chair) and other magistrates, Samuel Parker and Elizabeth Parker, 33 restock-A , were summoned by Inspeclor McAteer for neglecting their child on the 2tith August and other days during the past six months to the danger of its hcalth.—Both de- fendants pleaded not guiltv.—lnspector McAteer said in consequence of a report sent to his office he went to the defendants' house in company with Constables Wool and Smith. There was no one in then but the woman came in later. After a time the child came in. Its face bad been recently washed. The child said her Aunt Polly had washed her face, and put the clothes on her. They asked the woman to undress the child, and she did pct. The child was in a dirty condition. The woman said she neglected her home through running her neighbours er- rands and for beer. Witness then went into a back bedroom where an old woman named Warburton was lying. The defendants had taken the woman out of the workhouse. Defendants had been under his observation fir the last six months and during that time he hail celled upon the woman often but it Isai had no effect. Her husband worked but he believed the wife spent the money in drink. P.C. Woods corroboratni the Inspector's statement. The male defendant said be only got from 18s. to 193. a week, as he seldom got a full week in ; and he gave her 14s. or 153. met of it ; but he denicol that the child was neglected.—Mrs. Johnson said the child ha i plenty of food, and more than jt could eat. —Mrs. Leigh cross- examined by the Inspector, said it was true that she had said to him on a previous occasion that the wife was of drunken habits and the home was neglected.— The Chairman of the bench said they would dismiss the male defendant, but he was morally responsible for the child and the horse; hut as for the female defendant they would think very little of sending her to prison, as according to the evidence Riven by her own witnesses she was much to blame. They would adjourn the case for month and If there was not a decided improvement in the house, she would have to go to gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>MINOR CASES. WARRINGTON_BOROTTo IT POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. WARRINGTON_BOROTTo IT POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>pjunay.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Cannelllor Smet- hurst, Alderman Roberts. and Mr. S. Wright. rr Pore AND SCRIBE." —Daniel Webb and Arthnr Stringer, test from Wolverhampton were charged with begging in Wilson . Patten...street. on Thursday night —Defendants pleaded guilty.—Detective Inspector Monaghan said he was In company with Detective Forster. "bout twenty minutes to seven. The defendants were calling from house to bonne showing a begging letter of which they had duplicates. He took them to the police station, and money was found upon them. The men said there was a great deg of distress in Welverhampt, n —The Chief Constable said one of the men was an ex-insurance agent and the other a petraan out of work. The letter was written in verse ; Webb was the poet, and Stringer the scribe. They had a list o! towns on paper which they either Intended to visit or had already visited, Defendants were committed to prison for 14 days hard labour each. ASSAULTING HER BENEI'ACTRR3S.—Mary ManniOn Was charged withbeing drunk and disorderly and with assaulting Alice Harman in Old-road on Thursday night —Defendant pleaded not guilty to both chargee.—Alice Harman, the wife of Joseph Harman, stonemason. 11, Old-road. Latchford, said she had taken the woman in her house out of pity, haring found her sleeping out some weeks ago. On the night in question the defendant Came in drank, celled her names and asseulteri her, taking hold of her by the head and throat. She screamed and a gentleman and a policeman rescued brr. When Mannion got outside thr house she knocked two women idown.—Thomas Smith, road foreman. corroborated.—Ser- geant Smith said he had to eject her from the bruise. Her language was not fit to hear. —P.C. Holekiss corroborated.— The Chief Constable said it was her 13th apnearance.—The bench sent her to prison for one month with hard labour. DRUNK IN DIAL-STREET:4OI Th nnas Higham, militia- man, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Dial' street, at 20 minutes past 12 o'clock on Friday morning.— The officer sail he asked him to go to the Barr acks quietly and saw him on his way, but the defendant came back. He made use of very bad language.—The Chief Constable said the Provost Sergeant gave the man a very indifferent char. enter —The magistrates fined him 10s. and costs or 14 days' hard labour. Als APPRENTICESIADENTURES.—This was a Claim by Messrs. B. and S. Chsmiley of Bridge-st.. fo Ils. for loss ot time by their apprentice Harry Massey. 158 Brighten-rd., Seaconabe. Mr-I' E.Roberts who appeared for the defendant, said they ad- mitted the claim, but he wished to ask their worships for an adjournment as the boy had been exceedingly unwell. He had been trying to get Messrs. Chandley to release the boy from his indentures. He hid absented himself because he been compelled for the sake of his health, and he (Mr. Moberts) was In hopes of coming to some arrangement with Messrs. Chandley —Mr Chandley said he did net object to an adjournment of tie CBl3e but at the same time be denied what had been said by Mr. Roberts. The fact was the father and mother of the boy -wished to leave the town and take him with them. The Mother had been to him to explam that an I to see if arrangements could not be male to release Harry from his Indentures. to that time the boy had not lost any time from ill-health. The boy was sent out of town to do some -work and the latter part of the week he stayed at home ill. It was simply an excuse to get a release. They (Messrs. Chandley) had had to send someone in his place to do the 'work. An adjournment was granted for 14 days. APPLICATION FOR SEPARATIoN.—This was an appileation for a separation order and maintenance by Elizabeth Hitchers 33, Bostoek-street from her husband James Ilitchen, 58, Cartwright-,treet.—Mr. A. Browne who appeared for the de- fendant, admitted the desertion. bet he allayed the rniscon- - duct of the wife in support of the case. The man got 19e, per week and six weeks of the year 22s Sfi. working for Messrs. Walker and Son, and he was about £l4 In debt part sof which had been run on by his wife without mewl The wife -made 105 a week herself.—Questioned by Mr. Roberts the wife said she was a fustian cutter, but there was not a bit of work to be had at present ; and she was net willing to part with the child. She could not maintain herself.—The Mayor; I don't know what my brother magistrates think.but I think It is very sad to listen to such cases beween young people.— An order was made out on defendant to pay 7s a week and the wife to have the custody of the oh 1-1 THE AD:OURNED CASE OF ALLEGED NEGLECT. —lnspector 31CAteeT, RiS.P.C.C., said in the ease against Mary Burn Harrison. which had been adjourned for a month, he was very pleased to say he had visited the defendant's house on different occasions, and there had been an Improvement. He now wished to withdraw the charge on condition that she looked after her children and home better than she had dem) In the past. —The bench said they were very pleased to agree to the charge being withdrawn. Andotrasan CASE ,F ASSXVLY —This was a case in which margsret Carter had been charged with beating Sarah Collins on the lbth August and which hail been adjourned for le days.--The defendaut now pleaded not guilty.—Sarah Collins said on the day in question about 4 30 she heard a disturbance and found her little girl crying on going oet. The child said Mrs. Carter had been beating her. She first ao asked her (the child) "what she was staring t' and then She (witness) asafteid:vm. Drdosl scraew Mrs.yotirm Carter other any dy money?,' afar? Seale words the defendant assaulted her.—Tho Bench considered the case proved and bound defendant Over to 15oelp the peace for six months and pay the costs or in default days. PILFERING BY BOYg —Sohn Johnron .'•Catberine-street, „d</p>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE 7410- ) LIVER PILLS. Szil3A Fill. BEAUTIFUL TEETH ; "0,1 k fro an will use daily On the With .1 price. watt a feW 4t 4136 41 1, , in a SOZODONT, r, the pleasantest dentce in the world- Cleanses the teeth and sPaoss Care between them as nothing else Sallow will. Bound and PearlY White Siek Be, teeth, rosy lips, and fragrant and cure them so as ~,, breath ensured. graved. Chemists, ls. INe: . 1.41. k for SOZODONT. ,2. s fid.</p>
