        <p>Harry Howard 92. Catherine-stre.t, were summoned for wilfully breaking a fence in Folly-lane. the property of John Warburton 65. Bewsey-road on the 28th August—John War- burton said the boys broke the fence about 4.30 on the day In question and to ,k a tut nip out of the field. They had done about ss. worth of damage: John Wright (U) corroborated and the bench fined the boys Is. each for the damage done and to pay the cost of the summons._ . •</p>
        <p>AT LEll.l;in NEGLECT 07 HOME .va) CHILDREN. —Reuben and I Mary Jane Worrall.9. Morris Court, were charged with neg- lecting their children John. James, Joseph, and Maul on the 24th August, and during the past six months.—Defendants pleaded not guilty.—lnspeotor McAteer said the children's ages ranged between nine and one year respectively. He went on the day in question with P.C. Monxs to the house,and two of the children were in at the time. The boy Joseph was in a most filthy condition and his flesh was showing through his trousers. The other child was dressed In a few filthy clothes. The eyes of th 3 child were com- pletely filled with matter, and the lent] was in a filthy condition. The only furniture in the room was a box and a small table; no fire; as for cocking materials, there was only one sauceptn and a cup. Upstairs the room wcs in a most filthy condition, In the corner where two beys slept was a mattress and a dirty tick on the top. The bed occupied by the husband and wife was also in a bad condition. The smell of the room was abominable. He went on the 26th and 27th and there was no improvement. On Thursday night he went to the house and it was in the same condition. Last Saturday when he went the husband was drunk on the door step —P.O. Monks corroborated.—The defendant, Mary Jane Worrall, tid they were poor but she did not wilfully neglect the children. The male defendant said he had been out of work for some time, and they had been obliged to sell all their furniture. The Mayor Paid the inspector had done quite right in bringing the case bef3re the court. They had deci- ded to adjourn the case for a month, and if the report was better the defendants would hear no move about It.</p>
        <p>SATURDLY.—Before Mr. Thomas Birchall (In the chair) and Messrs. R. T. Fair°lough and J.W. Hayward. A DESPER/TE Onaaacrra.—John Doyle was charged with being drunk and ref usin rto quit the premises of the Lower Angel. Buttermarket-street, on Friday night ; with assault- ing William Osborne at the same time and place, and with breaking a window in Horaemarket-street.—Prisoner pleaded guilty to beino drunk, but not to refusing to quit. The other charges he said he knew nothing about.—Mr.Shaw licensee of the Little Horse ghoe, said that prisoner came In about ten minutes past ninn. He said sorn.one had done him out of his lodgings. The prisoner was drunk and witness told him to go out several times. He would go out and come bscit again. The third time he came in he took a kick at the door and broke a pane of glass at ss. The prisoner was more like a madman thsn anything else. —William Osborne, manager of the Lower Angel, Buttermarket-street, said he came into his place about ten minutes past nine. He was drank and begged from the customnrs. Witness asked him N go oat. ' Prisoner asked him to corns out and fight. Witness blew his police whistle, and prisoner kicked him on the knee —P.O. I Chrimes said he heard the sound of the wnistle, and on going to the house found prisoner drunk and challenging the crowd to fight.—Questioned by the Chairman, the prisoner said he was a labourer and came in town on Wednesday from Bed- ford Leigh. and was to commence work on Monday at the White Lead Works.—The Chairman : You will have to go to gaol for a month with hard lisbour. We cannot have people of your description coming to the town, breaking arid smash- ing windows, and setting the public at defiance.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) Councillor Shaw Green, Councillor Smethurst. and Messrs. F. Monks, S. Wright. and Thomas Birehall. 1 A CHARGE WITHDRAWIY.—The Chief Constable said some of their worships would no doubt remember the case of Nor- man Matheson who was charged last week with having been found On the enclosed premises of Mersey Chambers, Mersey- street, for an unlawful purpese. The man had explained that he had gone in, in a state of drunkenness to shelter and had fallen asleep. He had got locked In and had used his best endeavours to let people outside know. He (the Chief Con- stable) might say there was much in the man's past that was very sad and regrettable, but nothing worse. He had held a very high position in the eduaatioual world, but had fallen through int3rnperanee. Fr-.):n first to last he (the Chief Constable) had not found anything in his previous CA eer that would au gest he Was a thief by any m3ans. He would therefore ask their worshina to withdraw the charge. The de'endant was ars ardingly discharged. • MlSCF.LLANEollB.—Llattlel Crosby, model lodging 11019 es Friars Green, WAS charg-d with being drunk and disorderly In Butter market-street on Sates -day night. —P.C.Turner said defendant had been ejected from the Lower Angel. ills language wall very Sad. The Chief Constable said it was de fendanre 83rd appearance and his terms of imprisonment went back as far as 1873.—The bench sent him to gaol for one month with hard labour.—Thomas Brown, labourer, 3. Vic- toria-street pleaded not guilty to the same charges—P.C. Smith said on Saturday night the prisoner was hitting a 1 woman in the face with a handkerchief in the Theatre Hopi!. 1 He followed him to the door and defendant began to use bad 'lam:nage. Witness advised him to go home.but he wont' not and he was obliged to take him into custoly.—The Chief , Constable said it was his sth appearance.—The Chairman: Perhaps you will make choice of better language in future You are fined its and costs or 14 days in default.—Thomas Leff. 75, Dal am-sane, for the same offence in Scotland-read' at 10-15 p.m. on S.turday night was fined Sc and costs.— Ellen Rainford. hawker, was charged with the same offence In Brides-street at 12.15 on Sunday morning. Evidence was given that defendant was lying on the footpath and when asked to move used hal language. The Chief Constable said one of the most respectable tradesmen in if ridge-street had to get up during the night to call for the police to remove the woman, as she was sitting on the footpath shouting the most abominable language. Defendant was committed to prison for one month with hard labour.—Bridget Sauce, 4, Buckley-street was lined Sc and costs for the same offence in Buckley-street on the 29th Anguet.—Thomas Brown,labourar, and Mary Brown. husband and wife, of Wright.streetSankey Bridges, were summoned fo:. being drunk and disorderly In Wright-street on the 29th August,—The male defendant did not appear. PT. Smith proved the ease. Their house he said vase veritable shambles; pictures were amathed, and gas pipes broken.--The Chief Constable said it was the wife's 7th appearance and the husband's 10th. The bench fined the female defendant 5s and cos a and Ora male lOs and casts or Seven and fourteen days resoentlyely.</p>
        <p>BREACH Or THE PeACE —James Conl'ffe hbourer. 15. Hook Crossley-street, Th nnas Howatrl, joiner, 7. Eldon street. Joseoh Birchall. tin p'ate worker. 53 Hidori-at•eet and Samuel Hadcroft, wire worker, Mill Yard, Fennel-street (all young men) were summoned for aomitutting a breach of the peace on the 18th August in SI/lon-street. P.C. Whittaker proved the case- The ilhief Constable said that enTier (Bdon-street.) was one of the worst pqrts of the t ,wn —The defendants were all bound over to keep the peace for PiX months, and pay Ls. each for the costa.—Henry Trante-. 83. Cartwright-street and Jerre' Briow, 13. Fox-s-reet. bets forgemen, for the same offence In Priestley-street, were bound over to keen the peace and pay the costs. MxaeaOv,eaocs.—Robert kirk, labourer. 19 Smith-el r.ot, was tined Is for having his chimney On firs on the 27th August, Thomas Holland, fruiterer, Croma-atrert. %VIA finest is. for having his dog at large on August 26th,</p>
        <p>TUESlalt.—Beore Mr. R. Garnet (in the chair) Datrax.—Joseph Thornas pleaded not guilty to a charge of being drunk in Knutsf,,rd-roact on Mmdav night —P.C. Cor- bett, said he received a comolaint abont. .3a and foun 1 the defendant in the Cheshire Che.se Inn, lie was using bad lAnz,,ge Ind lierqing Srmoney. Witness took him to the aPnodlicueieStiatititotiniy.—lasdngr i,l3agmenetnt e 4 ti tbneelnlistswhioepe ;iiirurnnekd out ot a public-house opposite. —The Chief Constable (to the magistrate): He doss not belong to Warringt ifl; and is one of the unemploy-d.—Defendant was fined 105 and cots or 14 days —4amnel Sias:pith woo charged with the same offence in Schno!.-3treet on the previous night.—lividmee was given that defendant was drunk without a coat on in School- street In answer to the magistrate the defendant said he was a fireman on the London and North. Western itaiiwv.— The Chief Constable said the defendant would lose his situ- ation and his whole career would be spoiled if he was con- victed. so he wonld suggest that on his promising to give up drinking the case should be withdrawn.—The defendant ex- plained to the magistrate in answer to a cin salon that be was teetotal no to 21 years o age and he was now 29. —The Chairman : Well if you will Edo the pledge with Mr. Taylor and keep it you will go on all right. Will you do so? The defendant : Yes and keep it.—The case was accordingly withdrawn.</p>
