      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSION.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. Philip Srwalcman (in the chair), and Mr. A.. R. Norman. _ _</p>
        <p>OBSCENE LANGUILGE.—Mary Ann Smith. a young Woman, was charged on a warrant with using obscene hnguage in Waterloo-road, on the 11th Auvust.—P.Cl. Fry proved the case.—Tbe prisoner said she hut been in a borne in Deans- gate. Manchester, and it was her intention to return to. the home —The Chairman said thev would adjourn the case for a month to see If the prisoner Carried out her Intention. If ehe went back to the home Superinten teat Okell would report to them, cod she Would hear nothing further about the Catle AN IRUSIIMIN'S BErIiffiRDINLEII ADVENTURE —ThONSins Burke was charged with being on end l used premises, belong- , lug to John Dyke, for an unlawful purpose on September 4th. —John Dyke stated that be wa , manager for the Ship Canal Comp my's boat building yard. known as the Sprinch. On Sunday afternoon, about four o'clock, he beard the dO4 bark- ing. and his wife went into the yard. From what shs told , him he went out and found the prisoner in the cellar kitchen yard. The ptisoner would have to come through the ytrd, over the iron bri ige. and get ove- wall 10 feet high to reach the yard lie believed that had It not been for the dot prisoner would have ransacked the home. The dog had bar red for half an bout, and they could not understand what was the matter. Prisoner said he bad walked from Liverpool, and did not know the "rule.." Kis Wei asking f Sit drink oft water. The prosecutor add that was no Mace to acme for a drink. He found the prisoner straddlelegged on the wall. He was bringing the prisoner to the Police Station and w walking same distance behind him foe fear he did anythnig to him when the prisoner got away. Witness went and informed the police.—Sergean,i Gill stated that after making inquiries he found the prisoner nen. the Cemetery. He charged him with being on enclosed premises He replled '• Are you going to charge me with stealing anything ?" Witness said he ha.-I told him the charge and p honer th,n sold'' I Was after a drink of water."—The prisoner said he was in a strange country, and did not know where he was. He was sinsiely asking for a drink of watar.—The Chairman asiced why he hid himself, and the prisoner said he did not like to be put in prison for asking for a drink of water,— The Chairman sal* he would be sent to gaol, for 14 days' hard labour.—Prisoner, Thank you. sir. (Lau2hter.) BRUMMAGEM'S CILAMPIoII " ftc TRGUBLE.- Thomas Bilis, who said he heed from Leicestershirs, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Church-street on the 4th September.—P.O. Gregory stated that on Sunday evening he received a complaint from the land'ord of the Fleetwood Arms, and on going to the house he found the prisoner and another man fighting. W,tness put the men out of the house, and got them Into the lodging-house. Shortly after- wards he found them fighting again. end he put them Into the lodging-house a second time. Prisoner however. Rama</p>
