        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 6, 188.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 6, 188.</p>
        <p>into the street again and offered to fight the hest man in the town." Witness got him into the house again, and sub- sequently, with the assistance of another officer, he brought bim to the Police Station. Prisoner said he was the champion bnxer of " Brummageat."—Prisoner I was so drunk that I did not know what I was doing.—P.O. Grepry said the prisoner's wife came down to the Police Station, and used very bad language. She would have been taaen into custody had it not be.'n that she hat children with her.— The prisoner, who begged the Bench to deal leniently with him on account of his family, was fined 55, and costs, or 14 days' hard labour. Act OLD OFFICNDER. —Mary Ann Glover, a well-known chime en, fillet to appear on a summons char4lng her with using obscene language In Gas-street. Runcorn, and the Bench issued a warrant for her apprehension. OCCASIONAL LICENSE —ROyiS Girnett.ef the nu Bridge- water Arms. was granted an occasional license to sea re- freshments. on the occasion of the Athletic Sports, to be held at Runcord on the 10th,lept.mhe..</p>
        <p>"A "TEAM COMPETITION. WARRINGTON " "v. LOSTOCK GRALAM.</p>
        <p>The Warrington "A "team which visited Lostock on Saturday was made up as follows: Jones, full back ; Heesom, Cross, Radcliffe, Pendlebury, three-qua iters ; Dunkworth, and Allen, hill tacks ; Andrews, Hatton, Swift, Williams, Jolley, Forster, Sudlow, and Wilcock, Forwards. The hornesters opposed with a strong team. Warrington hut the Lest of the play in the earlier part of the game with toe sun at their hacks, hut with the change of position at half time, the play also changed in favour of Lostock. Tee game concluded after a stiff contest. Swift, Cross, and Radcliffe, each scored a try for Warrington, Jones conveiting two hat gcals, and kicking one penalty goal. Fish scored three tries for Lostock, one being converted. Result Warrin4ton 15 points, Lostock 11 points. Sr. HELENS A 11. WIDNES A.—At St.Helens both teams were strongly represented. The Saints started before a glaring sun. Gran.' passing by the Widnes backs trans. ferred hostilities into th home territory. Boyd intr. cepting scored, Percival kicking a grand goal. The Saints worked into the vis.t.irs' 9uarzers,but the visitors defence was safe. Near the Interval Golden scored a try and Thomas placed a goal for Widnes,and Stubbins scored and unconverted try for St.Helens. Half-time Widn s, 13 p Ants; St.Heleas,3 points. Final—Widnes A, 2-6-0; Sc. Helens A, 0-2-0. ' LANCASHIRE JUNIOR WEIDE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN HORNETS T. BIRKENHEAD WANDERERS " A " Timm —The match between the Runcorn and W gan "A" teams having beets cancAlod, the Runcorn Executive v.ry kindly placed taeir ground at the diep:sal of the Itnoc)rn Hornets, who had arranged to open the reason with an engagement with the Birkenhead Wanderers " A" team, in cminection with the Lancishire Juni )r Ltague. Ths Wanderers managed, after an interestinz game, to defeat the home beam by one penalty goal and a try (five points) to one try (three points).</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. At a meeting of the committee in Manchester on Mindav evening tie f,il owing permits were granted : A Santlebury (Uardiff). with Stockport ; G Brown and J Hughes (local), with Widnes ; 8 Woodhouse, .1 Long- mire. It Jackeoa, R Graham, A Wilson, and J. Bland (local), with Morecambe ; A Boar Imr.n, J Aahurst, J Alen, G H Williams (local), with Warrinzton ; P Rep', F Peters, W Foam, R Anderson, and It Yates Co ial), with Runcorn ; and G Subbing, J Flynn, J Liver:edge and J Chapman (local), with St. Helens, J Sudlow (St. Helena) was transferred to Warrington.</p>
