      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>"I WO OLD I-Wirt/Ea It DIE !"</head>
        <p>"I WO OLD I-Wirt/Ea It DIE !"</p>
        <p>So writs a London man just before he sent a bullet crashing through his beau. Oo the third floor of an ordinary lodging-house in Loudon, the police bard the body of a lodger with a revolv rby his side and a bullet in his head. tie bail been dead over forty-eight hours; in his pocket was found a book in which was written the following "I am ti.e.l of life because I am suffering from Bright's disease. I would try to struggle again and work, but it is impossible for me to cio so, while I am sick and without the slightest hops of getthig bettor.' I would rather die ; how glad I shad bo when I am dead! Let the city bury my body." What a commentary on our boasted civilizttion ! A poor wretch, suffeiiag from whit he strop:sad to be an incurable disease, snuffs out his flickering life with a I pistol bullet. Upon whose head shall fall the blarnefor his deed—for his life? Where did he get the ides that his disease WAS Incurable? Simply from a generally accepted ohiti-ne theory,which is still regsrded by some as a fact, but which rnoiern investigation has ex. ploded. Many diseases Trlll7 be co as'clated incurab'e,but that does not mike the.n so. Whit we thought to be impossible ten years ago, is 43 shy C 30M 31141 ed today. This is not an age of impossibilities. Darin the past few years scientivs havediscovered that Bright's disease is cur tble through Warner's Safe Cure, a-id simply be- cause the poor wr.tch was not awir: of this facl, he killed hirnidtlf. Not so with others. Cures are being daily recorded,after all other in ans had failed. _</p>
        <p>t'ae deith oi that poor, misguided mu hi Loadon lodzing-boute,will not have 13E4)3 without Its lesson, if it teaches you that this terrib:e diseate is curable,and th it the remedy —Warner's Safe Cure—can be found every. where.</p>
