        <p>COOL DISFIES FOE. HOE WEATFIER. In summe• tie 'a ly of the house is syer on ne look- oat for auitabie dr sart dishes far the femily,especially vrhcr there are children. She will find that one of the canlest and most artreeable dishes, and one ever welcome to the children, and Co their elders, is a blan”-mange made with Brown ard P-ilson's Corn Flour. Ti s may te amaomonnied with preieryed fruit or stewed fruit of all kinds in semon. A dPlicious jelly can be mace by preparin.q, Corn Floor with the clear jifc, of stesrfri rhuh.trb, or anv nthar juicy fruit, inatend of saith To produce th, reVir good C r Floor is eszenti.l. Brown and Poison's" Patent " Corn Flow ham long been recegoizel as the standar larticle frr ttu,s‘. cliahFs. Though snme'im^s a triflt (law rer than ordinary cars mnoh fßridatlr than athers, and the dishrs made from it are always much finer in flaYour. Ase for Brown and Poison's " Pateat " C ,rn Flour, best quality en I be sure that you &lt;rot it,—Advt</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CONTRACTou FINED Flit CRUELTY On Tues'iny, at N,rchwich Potty Se-sions, 11r. West-in and Dr. Hewitt. John C. Lunt, 85, Howley-lene, Herrington, contractor. and John Wrench, 34, Ashton-street, Warrington, were 810=1- model. at the instance of Inspector Greatrix, for I intreating a mare by kicking and beating it on August 12th. Defendants pleaded guilty.—Clarlt4' Bees, iroefounder, Witton. stated that on the diY named at 7.30 p.m.. whsn in Station-road, he saw defendants in charze of two horses attached to a lurry containing empty barrels. Lunt lashed the mare with the reins, then kicked it on the b and rift-rwards struck it several stinging blows with a chain. Witness in'erfered, and the driver came up and asked whit he had to do with it, using foul language. Witne.s asked the owner for per- mis,ion to take charge of the horses, and on doing so Wrench rushed up and asked him to leave thorn alone, at the same time kicking it two or three times in the belly. He then said be would go and fetch sornsthing that would move the hortes. After witness had got the animals away the driver Wrench cursed him. The blows were severe, and the chain was stronger than the one produced. Wrench was under the influence of beer.—A-thur Clppark. of the " of Wales." gave corrobora- tive evidencs.—lnspector Greatrix spoke to seeing dlr. Lunt seven days after the affair, when he acknowledzed he might have given the horse a few •‘ raps with the reins owl one or two "raps" with a cheirt. lie did not remember kutk in.::: the animal. Witness c ,old not trace any marks up-m the mare.—The Bench said they were vorry to e-e Mr. Lunt the's on such a chatee.A. fine of 20,.and Icosts would have ro be paid, or 14 days' imprism- ment, and Wrench cretin have to pay 10&amp; and costs, or seven days.</p>
