      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>DRAW AT WILDERSPOOL.</head>
        <p>A DRAW AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>The football season opened under most auspicous circum4 laces on Saturday. Weather of the finest discriptioa favoured the return to the pastime of chatiog the oval—though Old Sol was mo-e in evidence th in t eto ling, boiling players desired. The visit of th famous Swinton fifteen to Warring- tou for the opening match was an event of the tit- mat importance and interest to the followers of the Rugby game in Warrington, an I there was con- sequently a crowd of most encouraging dimensions. Seven thousand would not be far from the mark at the commencemeat cf hostilicie.., and very compact the field looke 1 with its new encloQure sal en- larged stand ace unruo lation. Swinton have yet to obtain their first v;ct.rry at Warrington, for after a most exciting struggle on Saturday, the honours were even. Mr. J Brack,hsw (Stockport) was the referee, and the players under his direction were as follows</p>
        <p>_ Warrington: Boscow, full back ; Moo-es, Isher- wood, Hallam, and Holcroft, three-quarters; J. T. Bate, and R.- Bate, half hacks; Taylor, Deakin, Mason, Naoo,, Pairhurlr, Boardman, Barratt, and Eden, forward i. Swinton : Goodman. full back; Me sir, Val,n- tine, Badger, and Baran, three-quarters ; Pearson and Mo.wan. half backs ; kn,worth, Evans, H.arris. Johnson, Preston, Poflitt, Reynolds, ad Vigors,for- wards.</p>
        <p>Warrington lost the toss and commeneed operatione against the wind and sun. Tay:o • kicked off and Swinton soon had the ball in the Weerin2ton half, Moores being early called up en to save. The home forwards secured the oval from the first scrimmageeand a sin sit punt by " Smack " fotlowe I up in quick style by R. Bate evoked a hearty OW from the enthusiastic Warrington'ans. The heme- eters bustled their opponents about with a vigour which spoke well for +heir chances. The two Bates. whose tactics were skilful in the extreme, carted play still further in the enemv'a quarters by a clever passing movement. A penalty for off aide play robbed Warrington of their advantege, one of Valentine's lofty punts changing the venue of the atruzgle. Pearson punted from touch and speedy following up by the Lions prevented Isherwo)d clearing his lines. Swinton now had rattier the best of mattere, but found the defence perfect. Isherwood was cheered for a dashing ran back to ceatre, end then R. Bate got away, passed rether lets, and B:dger was seen epee ding chwn the field with the ball. Boscow and flolcroft proved too much for he tricky Welshman. Valen- tine tried one of his old-time rushes only to be grassed in short order by Moores. A •tame kick from B Vow provoked a 4-oe at goal by Valentine, but it was fruitless. Oee or two passing movements among the Swinton hacke trowel:it oit, all the re- source of the homesters, and the tackling was of the surest charecter Werrin m r etaiiatsd with ex- changes between the two Bates end $ ilcroft. The latter ran up and centred, a brilliant dash be the home forwards giving Swinton no chance and It. ' Bate notched the first try of the vason for Warring- ton. liolcroft made a fine ettempt at goal from near tnuch.and the cheers vere ro-deublel. Thing' went w-11 for Warrington, especially as Bevan and Bed- nor anniled a couple of grand chnnces by clumsy knocking-on. Badger tcok,a lot of watching, and in addition had a free kick which he made an awful mess of. Even play ensued, the two Bites attacking well on one side and Mernan,Peersolond Badger responding on the other. Little (Insist were fosght out between Wen' iee and Moereewith vantage to the latter, hut at tryst the Swinton. Dm- inx machinery not in full swing and a maenificent movement ended in Bevan breaking past Boseeve and eqaelising with a clever try. Valentine had hard luck with the placelrick. Warring'ol had a 1 warm time of it just nnw and a free-kick cause I a welcome diversion. Ralf-time arrived with the score, a try each. Swinton resterted Finer eetically. Isherwood being prominent at once. Warriegton obtained the atelier haul straight away, and II oleroft tried a kick from centre, the bell going 3 little wide. Sterling Play back and front caused the Swintonians much anxiety, and when Pearson tried a short mint out from touch, end Naylor blunle t over, n cheer was half nttere betpremettireter, for the whistle bad poande. Priem a pack on the Swinton line, " " obtain el the bill and slime it arrows to Holcroft who tried herd tenet ove,r but was stopped near the tut. Pl•ey was all in the Swintnn half. and Messer luckily Pen'. deed fro-a a we'd-judge punt by Beecow. which Boerdinen end Dsakin nearly cnnverted into a try. A moment liter Messer wee Injured. and a lengthy st,onpsge gave Swinton meich-repired breather. On resuming, the vi•dtore Rpresared to have regained all their dreih, and ley energetic forward play they entered., the horns half Bevan had a nice opening when he sent the ball forward. Moron go'. away down the Ouch line and punted past Boscow. but before his could overtake the oval, efeore.s has Fieei up and sent it dead. It was new Warrington's turn to be hemmed in, a number of unfortunate kicky into touch nearly bringing about their downfall. Vs'entine secured and ran finely for the line, the comhined efforts of Taylor and Boscow being needed to bring him down. A fee kick to Swinton in gond position was unproductive though the visitors' skipper tried hard to steer the ball through Bate and Isherwood raised the seige and a clever dribble by the home front rank carried play to eater quarters. "Smack" got sway cleverly and Warrington had ano'her ,•truggle on the Swin- ton goal-line The danger was cleared, and play operated at centre when the whistle sounded for the last time, leaving the game in an even state.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. WIGAN. TUE "LINNETS" WIN BY A POINT.</p>
        <p>The weather was rather tno fine for a vigorous ex- nettitim nf the winter pastime, but nevertheless the Wigan aniTtianeen-n reoeeaentativea, brolling beneath the rare of a powerfil sun,glVA an energetic display nn the Wigan ground, sabero a very eatisfactorY crowd aaaembled to aritneaa the initi,tniV effotts of th-se twe well-known teams. Considerable sirculation had barn rife as to the campo4lien of the home fifteen and when the men stepped into the enclosure several stranger' were to be noticed. In addition to the mixture Of W,lshmen, IT Rottomlev,of Hirldeeefield,and Harr, Ball and J Hiltan,who had previelisly done duty for luninr eluhq in the Wigan /igtrict.tumecl out, and (11;4 infu.ien Tvw talent no doubt son-elated fn, the Un'e*ty which was felt as ti hew the WiTarre wculd acquit th, m-..elveq On the other hand the Rune-rn repreaenta- tivns write rr ty well known to each other, thou7h the advertised team underwent settle slight alteration, b. Halearond taking the plane of W. Fletcher at threequarter, Taylor whilst Reece was suhstitut,4 f at. A. strong the forwards. The visitor; commenced operationa rah h the sun hehind them, and Wigan a Ere at once called upon to act on the dere lee. Unfo tun- atelY for the home club. Wa,ll(den. een're th-ee-quarter 11'5 lln'le tatistel Wthin the fir t few minutes. H" was" nen est "for vOme time, and when he returned he was of very little sarviee Wigan. haying had one ot two nar-ow escapee. to rnfit from the experi- ence, and gond half-blek play b Dixon and Highton enabted the home fourt-en to hold their owl. Then Warder, Wilkins-in, and tweed started a fine passivg movement, whicdi wag pulled rip by Kiyinarth. Stlhss- - some capital less War was witn"'"fl and fr'r a time Wigan gained the advantaga by eoleniicl f011'11157. McCarthy heing partieularlc prominent in ..Vrral bout'. Wigan at this stage per ictently attacked, but time after tune th curd internalional, Ilaughton removed the dan- ger. and Wigan found him a veritable thorn in the flesh. Ball, on one necasion, had a clear opening, but IlmthlisZ the leather the chance wq, !net. As the Inttlt'val annroached Runcern exreflenced a warm fire minutes, the home plavera peening their oppments On h gnat line, but. when the Wigan suonorters were anticirating tire downraii of the en-my the referee Foam-maned that the time had arrived when the contest ante .'-1012'0 tap a " breather " It was generally thought Wigan would forge all-Alin the second half, and for a enirevlnt lenehy priol ther had the beat of matters. Dixon had another excellent epportunity of opening an account for his side, and he eosin failed in his attunnt '0 drop a goal from an exceedingly easy plaithn. Jolley then became (extremely energetic at half-back, ard having emulate i Dlxon's exampte, with b .tter results, was mainlc resrvmsible for numertua attacks which were rra te upon the Wigan tine towards the eoneinsi .n of hostilitica. wh,m any further scoring aportared highly improbable Jolley dram-lei another goal for Runcorn, whilst within the laq two minutes the Wlgan spectator( were gladdened by 1314elthurn scampering over the Run- core line near the corner flag. The kick at the sticks moving unsuccessful, Runcorn retired vicbrs by 4 points to three. -</p>
