      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>CZAR's INVITATION TO UNIVERSAL DISARMAMENT.</head>
        <p>THE CZAR's INVITATION TO UNIVERSAL DISARMAMENT.</p>
        <p>SERMON BY THE REV. JOHN YONGE.</p>
        <p>At Wycliffe Congregational Church, Warrinnton, on Sunday evening, the Rev. John Yonge tor sachedsa special discourse on the invitation of the Czar of Russia to uni- versal disarmament. There was a large congregation. The preacher said no apolog.y cculd he reedful for intro- ducing in that Church the Czar's meFsage to the civilized world which, on Monday last, created such a universal surorise. Few events which had occurrei in their day could surpss in importance this call of the Russian Emperor te the international consideration of disarma- ment. It was commanding and absorbing the attention of the world. Assuredly it could not b' less interesting ..end less impertao t for the attention of ne.Church. He had no doubt that it, would cornet un ler nnti:e thn d in a large numb-r of Chri,t'an palpits both in this and Adler lands. And so it would challenge tie sympathy, anti evoke the prayers, of Christiana of every name. No Pronouncement of a ruler of this world could h? more fitly repeated in Christian pulpits. Nonconformists at any rate were not accustomed to he echoes either of nrincsa or of politicians. But here was n str CePtional case ;an Emperor was preaching peace; he dwelt upon the evils and imooliey 0' war:id-educed inter• national peace to he the i teal of all Christian states, end invited the powers of the world to confer as to its best pro-notion. Any Christian pulpit that should ignore 89 supremely Christian a propos ol would seem to be trier in indifferent and disloyal cm.coreting the will and nicaaure of the King of Kings, who is the Prince of Peace. After referring to the feet that the publication of the Czar's proposal was withheld ~ntil Sunday la.t so that it might appear contemporanemialy with the unveil- ing in the Krmintia at Moscow, of the monu- ment to the Czar's grandfather, Alexander 11, th. Preacher proceeded to read the document. After doing so he said it was doubtful if the woind's annals cnntainel the record of any state document that could compare with it. It had certainly net been customary with the world's rulers to define the Chri ;Van ideal as the ideal of governraents.But here in his ramming sentence.the Russian Minister speaking as his Emperor's mouthpiece die- tinctly defined the Christian ideal as that which fully corresponded with the mini of his august master, " The maintenance of universal pacers," and to that end, "a poSsib'e reduction of the excessive armcments which weigh upon all natinns,"—thece—" represent the ideal aims towards which the efforts of all Governments should be directed." That was the text of this Imperial sermon to the powers of the world. He did net think they could wonder very much at the surmise such a sermon had made. That such a sermon should be preached at all by the leader of a great state ao,d espaci- alit' that it should COMP from the ruler of a supremely military and milrtant empire,which had bens ISpe3tPd, ever) lately of the most aggressive and warrk, potio7, was indeed a great surplice. That it should be re- ceived with sentiment of widely divers character. and be interpreted with scept,cil iocredulitv on one side and hopeful expeatancv on the other was only what they should look for.But,however,eceplical and incredu- leus statesmen and st^Ckbrokers, cynics and soldiers might he as to the practicability of the proposal, it was one which Christian men of all nred s were bound to consider with willing alacrity and generous sympathy. Undoubtedly a multitude of gigantic difficulties would crowd in the path of this proposal. It might even prov,. is bs the case that it might never reach as far es the actual assembly of a conference. There were race fends, and national revenges, and military interests, and interests of Stock Exchanges, which might combine to kill the piojoct almost in its birth. All the more reason then that the religious sentiments of Christian people should be universally aroused se as to vow, if possible, a f rir and earnest onsisideratian of a project se humane and Christian in its idea. It wss fasaionahle in some quarters to depreciate the influence of the Chris- tian Churches in the affairs of the world. 'Poet was tn be explained, probably, by the indisputable fact that in such matte's the Churches had been only to silent and inactive. But he, for one, did not think it an extreme thing to say that in this present case, if Churches of all Christi,n creeds would be entirely true to the precento and interers of the Prince of Peace, 5h57 wolld be able to constrain even Cabinets and Monarchs and peoples to consider and confer as now challenged and invited by the Czar. Arneng the Many questions that had been asked this last week concerning this remarkablo proposal had been this : Is it sincere ? or had the Czar SOMP deeply conceived plot, some secret, sinister, and B.lfish motive, behind this apparent pas- sioic of philanthropy and humanity? There had been critics both on the Cont'nent and in this countrz: who had strongly asserted that this insincerity was pl linty discernable ; and they had gone so far as to say that the proposal instead of making for peace, would make, and was intended to make for war. Of course no one could say with certainty that the young Emperor was sincere. And with equal certainty no one could convict him of the reverse. But one or two things might be suggested in favour of believing him to be sincere. One was this, that this humane proposal perfectly agreed with what were kanwn to be his personal characteris- tics and sentiments. The preacher quoted several re- corded instances which bare out this view. It might be the case that in the circular greatest stress was laid up- on the financial impolicy of excessive armaments and of war itself, yet while the Czar used that argument be• Cause it made in many 919.rters the strongest of 31: appeals, underneath and behind it all there wasthe Czar's fervent humanitarianism,not ti say Christian antipa'hy</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>te the horrific carnage and massacre of battle. Another thing which went to guarantee the good faith of this proposal was the whole tone and drift of the eiveutir itself. It read and rang like an honest document. Taken as it stood it seemed difficult to doubt Re genuinely straightforward meaning. What was it that it proceeded upon ? It proceeded up el the over asd„ over again reiterated declaration of all the powers tblie;„ their governing idea for the last 20 years had hten tu-r preservation of iseace. After reminding the powers what had been don, in the name of peace he declared the indisputable fact that " all these efforts neverthelesg have not been able to bring about the berieficient resultg of the desired pacifcations." There he put in crisp, clear language the actual situatioa of the day. Europe at this morrunt was little better than a vast military camp; The air on the continent was thick and dark and distils' with surmises and sulo'cions, with anxiou3 fears, with cons'ant alarms, thit utterly killed the peace and ruined the eenfldeccs of all the nations. Id commenting up on the Czar's reference to the " ever Cr asing financ. al burdens," the preacher stated that a recent writer in a French paper was rasponsible for the following figures which Chewed that war was cesterg this year tn France. Russia, Germany, euitria, Italy and England £220,000,000. nv the year 1875, the annual military and naval expel:yes for France Itusr,ia, Ger- many. Austria and Eneland had increased nearlY £48,000.000. If the whole of Europe were taken inW account it was ealcelated that direct taxatioa of the people for naval and military norpnsea mounted each year to close upon £213,000.000. The Peace societY lo,ffied the mind's power of realisation ky asserting that tCe appalling annual cost of war incleding past indeh' tedness and present outlay came to £580,000,000 or 27e. 6d , for every man. woman, and child in Europe. W 34, not the Czar abundantly' justified in using Li exalted position, as head cf a great military people, t° cry a " halt "in this ruinous career ? How infinitely' sinful was this waste? Bow horrible and fiendish and inhuman was the policy of so-called CfuLtian States, hf whom,as the Czar put it " hundreds of millions are dC voted to acquiring terrible engines of destruction, whic7, though to-day regarded as the last word of science, ar destlre3 to-marrow to lose all value in consequence 0' some fresh discovery in the same fell." Net only Wal such outlay horrible and devilish, it we.a foolish and vain ! The Czar thought he hrid found a happy moment for its presentation to the world. He snoke doubtless, from good degree of knowledge as to how 'fat govern- !mints were open ti consider it wolfs favour. Severahat least,could not b.+ enwilling. The vophkr states would revive in hope. Strong and greater 8 ares,who yet had as much AB th y could do to manase ceinfortably tbeit own affairs, would be ready to look at any planth.t could prevent the augirentatren of anxie: • ties. The drawing together k:f Eneland : America aces forniShing a new alliarMO of power' • 1 not hi hefts much taken into European accumt. The federaton of the Arsele-Saxon peoples meant much for the future. And thus the movement might be propit oia• How ri soil churches and Cnristian P"oPla promote this scheme ? It seemed to him that Ceristi,n people could help its the formst,on and spread of a pub ic opinion ia its favour. They could refuse to join in tee cayillieg criticism which cast discredit moon the hcriesty of put- po tire, underlay the proposal. They lived in days* unhapnily, when it Was Fally cOnlmon to question the nv-i!lves or rsttituries that were conspicuously Christian Bet they had nothing to do with the motives of this pro- pos I—whether they were coestionable cr honourable. It was better to believe them true, and lb ri were r agons for so le.slieving. But te proposal i-eslf was pi-A-eminently Christian. It was also equally rational, philosophical, and consistent with sound political economy. It was aereeable. most manifestly', w h every true tradition of progrer&lt;sive politica of patriotic FentilnOnt, of esseatial humanM and tberefore hou cArry with in special degree. r. he Free Churches of this IKin7,:iorn. If th matter were put to the vote there that night, every Is in would be held us in its favour. r Then let them see to it that very voice _ purea , virhm. needful, sh)uld speak sym-• p.tthetcally, - di,„euntmein .0 the sccO cy cal and niv , cal criticism which wo"11 brasS Use proposal as insia core or Utopian and isnPrantimble The Cz:r had had the boldness to prcc ai n a Ceristian ideal ; to indicate Methods for its approximation; t invite conference cal earning it, in view o ' f tee dawning of toe New Century. The proposal was splendid ; the moveovnt, was oppor- tune; Christian duty was clear an i paramount. The Czar's csli to conference among the nylons laic Imo un- mistakably a providential call to prayer among all Christians. It might even he that thiS P ',sal to stay the progress, of the arms' merits of the nations was itself an answer to prayer. t mig Assuredly i ' tbe traced to the doetriees of JesUS Christ. 'lso Count btoi, who had by his lire and writings, done so much to p.creace even difficult Russia with the light and influence of Christian principle and doctrine, was now reaping a glad harvest ti his sedulous sowing Bat not Tolstoi alone hal helped to this dawning of hope. The labours of devoted aryl sp ritually-mind .d Engiis Quakers—who in past par: had interviewed the CMS of Russia and sought to inoculate them with peace tprin- ciples were at lost be:inning to show results. His OW Christians and conviction was stronlly this, thaif</p>
        <p>Church's wenn now be true to Chr..tian fait4 and prin- ciple, and openly faycur this propo4al,and it the? would be instant in praye concerninz it,the Cites conference would meet, and inca'eulable blessing mgt come there- from t) the nations upon earth.</p>
