      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RIGHT OF PRIVATE JUDGMENT.</head>
        <p>THE RIGHT OF PRIVATE JUDGMENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln his letter of the 27th ult. " Sursum Gorda," not satisfied with att icking the authority of the Scrip- tures, makes a somewhat feeble attempt t o assail the I Protestant principle of" The rght of private judgment lin matters of religion." Feeble, we say, because of the paucity of argument with which he seeks to maintain his position. He cites the case of four ministers in the town, each believing in the Bible, yet each by virtue of his own judgment, drawing different coaclusions from the same,and his argument is that because they come to different conclasiens—t ley should not have the r:ght to think at all on matters of religion! Was there ever an argument more absurd and preposterous ? Just think what it means when applied to other natters. There are different schools of medicine, and because medical men differ as to their application of the prin- ciple 3 the right of thinking for themselves should be taken away from them. There are different schools of science and philosophy in the world, and be- cause Scientists and Philosopher 3 do not come to exactly the same conclusions, therefore, they must surrender their right to judge foe themselves ! There are different schools of politics in the world, and because they do not all agree. They should on no a-moult be permitted to think for themselves ! How illogical and outrageous is such a conclusion ! If these men differ in their conclu- sions concerning the same thing :he logical and rational , argument is—that they should learn to think differently land think again, not that their right of thinking for themselves should be taken away from them or be sur- rendered by them, and especially is this the case in I matters of religion, for religion is a personal matter and requires the personal consent of mind and heart.persanal sacrifices and a personal life of holiness. Every one will be judged at the last great day according to his own deeds, and it inevitably follows that a man must ju ige for himself what he ought to Iselieve and do, in view of that great day. " Sursum Corda "is fond of quoting scripture, when he thinks it supports his contention, let him study the scriptures on this matter, and he will find in them no support for la's centention, that a man should surrender his right of ju igment—to priest, pope or Church ! Our Lord again and again recognised the principle of private judgment. In the sermon on the mount, He says" Beware of false prophets, which canto to you in sheeps clothing but inwardly they are ravening wolves ye shall know them by their fruits." How is it possible that men shall know these false prophets except they are to exercise their private judgement ? I In his answer to the Sadducees—Christ said "Ye do err not kno wine the scriptures." What did that imply? Not that they did wrong in knowing the scriptures, or Icoining to their own conclusion respecting it—but that they were wrong in their judgment, and wrong in their judgment because they had not studied the scriptures sufficiently. AU our Lord's appeals te the Scribes and Pharisees and to the multitude, like Matthew xi., 23-30, are based upon their right to judge for themselves. The Apostle Paul, speaks very plainly on this question. He says in Romans xiv., 5. " Let every oae be fully per- suaded in his own mind "and in 1 Tnese. v. 21.. "Prove all things, bold fast that which is good." If language is capable of proving anything, these passages—prove the right to a mathematical precssion. The apostle John also declares on this subject " Be- loved believe not every spirit, but try the spirits , whether they are of God." These are eufficient to indi- cate the teaching of scripture on the question. The abuse of private judgment is surely no argument against its exercise. For what lofty principle can be named which has not b2en abused? Strength has been transformed into tyranny and lib-rty into licen tiousneee by abuse—but who would think of coadem- ning either because they have been so treated. Mao many m'staken souls—"Sursum C orda" believes that the salvation of the church is an organic unity. Vain delu- sion! nature and grace effectually resist suah a consum- mation, and the Romish Church evidently believe that it can never be attained by legitimate and peaceful means. So she attempts to attain it by suppressing the indivi- dual judgment and using force. How st:ange that such as " Sursum Gorda " cannot ser,, that it is possible to he " Oae body aril ono spirit and yet not be of one mould, one shape, one sin sone colour. The great free churches of th's c untry arc practically one, they frequently interchange pulpits, worship, work and commune together. They have different methods of proceduriediversities of operations, but the spirit that animates them is one spirit. This is the unity of love, and is infinitely more desirable than the ghastly unity gained by suppression, uniformity and compulsion, which is the bane and the curse of the Church of Rome.</p>
        <p>In concluding allow me to call attention ti tw flagrant ineonsistenci's which the letters oE " Sursum Cords" reveal. (First) Here is a man, who acelrding to his letters is exenising his mva right of private judg- ment coneeraing his belitf, cmtrary to ttt: teaching of his church. (Secondly) Here is a man, who is en- deavouring to win Proteltants ovr to the Rmlt corn nounity by appealing to their private julgayint ; else what is the m.larling of his letters ?—I am yours dro., A CATHOLIC BAPTIST. Ssptember sth, 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>BAPTISMAL REG gNEE</head>
        <p>BAPTISMAL REG gNEE</p>
        <p>Sir,-.-:Your several correspondents would do well, in my op'ninn, if, instead of abusing the C4t lobe ChOrch, they would try tr) crack a few of the nuti co.,tainsd in my letter of the 27'h ult. lithe Protestant rule is the Iris rule oi Faith or. claimed by Christ, how is it, the'. it leads one man to adore Jesus Christ as t'le E:ersal God, and anothsr, fol- lowing, the same rule of Faith, to rejoct the Divinity of Christ; and to ragas.] Hina as a mire man. How is it that it makes one man a Ritualist. and another an Evan- Protestomt ? Ho I it th tit makei !ha Pro- testant Dean of Ripon, to deny the Bmiiteacn of a psr- Ilsonal God? Is it the Holy Spirit who is now interpreting the holy Scriptures even in the Anglican Est bl is hm.tr, in the contradictery senvs of the four main parties Broad Church, Low Church, High Church, Ritualism Who is inspiring Lord Halifax and party with the belief that Anglican ministers are Mass-Priests, and Dr. Itsls eat' party, with roe belief that Anglican ministers are not Mass-Priests ? Does the Hnly Spirit induce one class of Prot,.stants to reject Baptismal Re. generation, and an)ther class of Protestants to affirm this doetzioe ? God is not the author of falsehood ! Pro- testantism, which is diviJed into OlOr ssven hundred sects, is contradictory; and thersfore a false religion! To assert that God, who is the Eternal Wisdom and Truth itself, could possibly be the anther of Protestantism, is rank blasp%emy. —Yours &amp;c, .</p>
        <p>SIIRSUNI CORDA.</p>
        <p>P.S. MI the old, stale, and unprofitable calunanieS, wh ch fn-m the usual stook-in trads of your ordinary Proterant disputant, and which by the way, have been over and over again exploded, are of no avail, as argil. trent, in a discussion of the present nature. Protestan- t ifm is at present on the horns of a' dilenima ; and fal amount of coarse abuse of Catholics and of the Catholic Church will set the Protestant house in order. S. U.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Romanist contributor to the discussion on this sutj set has contrived to throw dust in our eyes b v introducing the much larger question of the seat of Authority in Religion,and by following the usual method of this kind in honing up to ridicule Vie divisions which exist among Protest trite. The Protestant, he maintains holds infant hapisin and many °tire thinlS On the I authority of the Catholic, Chureb, ,To ihi; afat6merit I have not the slightiSt dbjectida, ptavidei that we have a clear idea of the exact meaning of the words We use. Most religious differences, as I baton pointed out, de. pend on the oppnsing sides using the same formulae in differrnt re .SM. Th 3 Catholic or IJaiversal Church is the Divine Srciety fouteel by our Lo:d and Fis Apostles to which the only method of admission is, and ever has been by Baptism, and to day as my friead the Romanist knows very well, all who blve been baptise t in the name of the Holy Trinity are members of the Chumb. Biptiern not performed by ministers Oi ths churn but by laymen, and even by Women, nag tils Same value, though it has always been held desirable that ii: should be performed by a minister of the Church, hs Orilei to secure the registration and pplic ri:cagnition of the act. In other words Baptisms only o be adminirend by a layman (or as the word means 'one of the peop!e') in case of necessity.</p>
        <p>Infant Baptism was practised in the Catholic Church from the very earliest tinges, 'and with the approval of that body. That tits Clurch acted rightly In holding that the children of its converts atmld 13) ad• mitted into its fl owahip and trained up in its fa'th from theit earliest years seams to LW, nothing outr tgeous. Nor will it I think be to anyone, who has once got rid of the mistaken idea that "Regenemtion" or "being born again "is the Fame thine. as "being converted" (or as the Revised version wistly translates the word 'turning' I.e. from evil eoursPe).Regeneration is the entering into a new re'a,ionship v:z that of a son of God (of course in a specialized senfe since all men owe their exiA?uce to God): it is the first step in sacramental grace. The following words of the lite Bialvp of Wakefield seem to give a good illustration of what I mean. "But it will be said, surely there are some, who have not lived a life of enmity with God. Yes, thank Gad there are some. We may know or we have real of, some child. perhaps</p>
        <p>EVERY CHING FOR rum SKIN SCALP and hair of infants and children that is cleansing, purifying. and beautify ing, CIITICIJRA will do. A viftral bath with CUTICIIRA. Some, and a gentle anointog With Covenae, purest of emollients and vastest skin cures, will afford instant relief, pc.rnait rest and sleep to both parent and child, and pit to a speldy,permanent, and economical cure in the most distressing forms of itohing, burning, scaly, and crust kin and scalp btrnourst ,with loss of hair, when all else fails.</p>
