      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EXAMINER. FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 9, 1898.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 9, 1898.</p>
        <p>early called away from earth to Paradise wile seemed from the very first, so full of the grace and love of God, so heavenly-minded, so unworldly, that there was no need of the great change there. The truth is the great change was there. The new life, pledged and entered upon in Baptism war simply being carried out. The Holy Spirit given, then, was simply doing his H work. And this is jest what should be. Some speak as if everyone must, f sr a time at least, live in a s:ate of sin and as a stranger to God, and if converted at all, I be converted from such a state. Sarely this is not true. The true pat:ern of a renewed and Christian life is that of him,who from His earliest years: " increased in wisdom and stature and in favour with Go i and man "—the highest blessing that can br Riven was that which was promised to John the Baptist " he shall be flee] with the Holy Ghost even from his mother's wemb." It is a most blesse I thing to be converceq, and to return to our heavenly Father's home. It is a more blessed thing Co have grown in grace from the first.and never to have gone away Irmo that heavenly Fether'e home. Bet oh! of how few dare we hope this better portion !" Surely the above considerations are not unworthy of the attention of a seeker after troth like " Iona:" surely they teach a religion that appcals both to the heart and to the intellect with greater force than the narro v theology of such a man as your correspondent " Bible Christian " who seems from his lettsr to be neither logi- cal in his own nor tolerant of other people's opiniens. But I have something more to say. Toe early Catholic Church, to which we owe both the distinctive teeets of our religion and the Bible which narrates how they were first declared (for be it remembered that the Church ex• 'steel years before any of the hooks of the Now Testa- ment were written, many years before they were col- lected into one volume) keew nothing of Rornanism. By Romanism, of course. I mean the corruptions and inno- vations of the mid Ile ages, the preposterous claims of the Pope to Uaiver,al Bishopric, and th e mata• physical definitions like 'Trensubstantiation which turned a spiritual religion into materialism. It is neither fair nor werthy of an educated mini to call such things as a crucifix, altar lights or the voluntary practice of con- fession to a minister of the Church, Romaaism. Such things existed before Romanism was invented, acd they exist still quite independently of Romanism, not only in the Church of England nay, these very thr3e things are to-lay used by the Evangelical Lutheran Church in Germany, the very centre of Protestantism. S 'rely to know 'his will cause a ray of light to burst in upon the minds of some of your readers. Now the Catholic Church extended into this country during the early period when the Bishop of Rome was nothing more than the ruler of the Church in the chief city of the Empire. As such of course be occupied a distinguished position. To-day the Pope is not Anti-Christ, but a Chr.stian Bishop, and as such worthy of our respect as well as for his high personal character. But this was an] is a vers different thing from admitting the extraordinary claims b 'sad upon his supposed succession to St. Peter. The Ancient British Church is believed to have been origin- ally founded chiefly by missionariee from the East, whicn might well be a reason why the Bishop of Jerusalem, or Ephesus or some place in that melon should claim our allegiance. When the retirement of ths Roman legions left the British at the mercy of the Anglo-Saxon invaders it is well known that they retired into Wales,where the four most ancient diocese e in this country St. David's, Llandaff, St. Asaph and Bangor remain t attest the ant'qility of the Church. Augustine of Canterbury sent from Runes as a missionare male' years later was iaiernmental in converting only the south-eastern part of Saxon England, the rest of the country owing its Christianity to Celtic missioleries [corn Waleseecotland, and Ireland. These latter were no willing listeners to the claims of the Roman See to their obedience, an 1 when eventually the two portions of the Catholic Church the Italian and the Celtic were welded into one institution conterminous with the na- tion,the as yet mild claims of the Papacy were often treated with scant respect. But when together with growing corruption and superstition the Roman obedi- ence that had by slow degrees been forced on this country, became intolerable the English Church at length, at the Reformation,aseerted its independence. It was the same Church as before, but purifiel, differing , jest as a garde' weeded does from what it was in its unweeded state, ; it is still the same garden. It is quite true that during the intellectual upheaval of the tong period of the R-formatioa (which lasted about 130 years) members of this Catholic Church of England founded vari- ous separatist sects,b3sed generally upon interpretations of the Bible, male without sufficient learning, and oftsn by build.ng ue a system on one single text without the I least r ege id to other passages that directly contradicted I their conclusions. Such separate bodies still exist, notably the Congregationalists and the Baptists, both oi whom, have, by God's providence, played no mean part in the history of English prouress. It is quite true also that there was another great sveration, that of the Methodist, which began about a hundred yea-s ago after the death of Wesley. Now the Methodists I am in- timately acquainted with, and I believe that they more than any other body helped during the apathetic period of the Church te keep England a Christian nation. But I cannot help believing that they have Moored from the original standpoint of John Wesley. The Sacrament ii doctrines f eund in their wonderful hymn-book S?,8111 to attest this. Certainly these doctrines receive little or no encouragement from their preachers. And now per- haps your readers will see what is the real position. There is, in truth, ouly one Church of Christ, of weiela all the taptisei are memb-rs. Ills at present split into three main divisions the Roman, the Greek, arid the Anglican of which the two latter are equally resistent to the Papal claims. The Anglican division wnich, one Bishop lately, remarked h is always been in a crisis, is Sr present assailed by two parties : (1) Her own separate] members the Nonconformists of whom some preach strange doctrines and others scoff at any d octrinei, but among whom despit • all this, there are n cvertheleee I believe, sone of tee truest christians in the country. (2) The Italian missioearirs and their fo'lowing ordin- arily known as Roman C itholice, who desire te bring us again into subj en to the Roman see. Ire conelusem let one quote some lately published words of M Gladstone, Writing to a ROllllll priest in India. he said " It will surprise you to learn my belief that I was beim and have always live] in the Catholic Church of this country founded long before St. Aueustine ex- tended it, and that be leaving it I should commit an act of rashe, ss and great sin." I believe there is yet a great future before the Anglican Communion, and that it is destieed tre play the part ot mediator 0 et weea the different parts into vrei3h the Holy Catholic Church has become divided, an] it is because I have c ome to realise the true Catholic teichiog as to the Christian life that I have ceased to be a Nencenfornaiag member of the Church f Eigtand, and can now sign mese f, QUONDAM SCHISeIeTICIJS,</p>
        <p>NEWTON URB&amp;N DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hal', E irlestown on M )r.d iy afternoon. Mr. C. B. F. Borron, J.P., C.C., occupied tile chair, and there were al,o pre- sent : Messrs. 1% Ormand, P. McManon, F. J. Wood,W Matte., W. Gilmoar, R. Stone, W. Collingwood, J. Simp- son, Dr. Patterson, J. Forster, with Mr. W. W. Shirley (clerk), Mr. Brierley (surveyor), and Mr. Bowes (assis- tant surveyor). TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.—THE COUNTY GRANT.</p>
        <p>Th, minutes of the Technic tl Instruction Committee st ited that a letter,dated August 4th,was read from the Lanc,shire County Council, offering a surn not exceed- ing £l6l in aid of the technical instruction within the distiict of Newton-io-Mae.fleld for the session 1893-9. —lt was resolved that too offer of the Lancashire Coun'y Council b accepted.—The minutes were ap- proved on the motion of Dr. Patterson, seconded by Mr. Simpson.</p>
        <p>division.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmnar : I apologise, I simply wished to point out that you were not always in Earlestown and New- ton. The difficulty experieoced by the police was to get summonses signei in Earlestown and Newton.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said 1-1-e--W;;-Cin'it7-in—t.o.u—c',-; with the police work in triose districts, and he had no', beard the police complain of any difficllty in getting summonses signed. A magistrate was not suppmed to be up night and day attending to the business .sf the district. The subject then dropped. and the minutes were passed. THE PAVING OF STREETS.</p>
        <p>In accordance with notice of motion given Mr. F. J. Wood msvPd the rescinding of a resolution referring to street making which was passed by tho Council at a meeting held on the Isit of July, 1895. Mr. iiilmour ssicoudel the proimition. The Chairman said if tho resllution was passed it vr:-.uld give the Council a free hand to say whether the streets should be paved with macadam or setts.</p>
        <p>The resolution was than passld. MR. GILMOUR AGAIN ON THE WARPATH.</p>
        <p>The Chairman intimated to the Council that he had received a letter from Me. R. Eaton with reference to a matter the Council had placed in the hands of the Chairman of the Elighways and Sanitary Committee. Mr. Wood moved that it be referre I to the Chairman of the Committee. Mr. 0 linour said he ulderstood that the officials; of the C iuncil had written Co say that the Council had no jurisdiction in the matter in questio r. The ro id fore- man had been up thire,and he (Mr. Gilmour) was afraid they would be involved in legal proceedings if they interfered. and would come off second bes. _ .</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he thought the Council was of opinion that the matt3r was in gaol hands. Ms. Gilmour: Has an official written to say that We have no jurisdiction. lam afraid the matter is not in our hands now.</p>
        <p>Mr. Collingwood . The remarks that hwe just been made lam afraid have taken the matter owl of my han is. The remarks will go forth to the pub i e and will prejudice the case. Mr. Gilmour : My contention is that there is a revo- lution on the minutes which will go forth to the puslic that the matter has been placed in the hands of the Chairman of the Committee and the Clerk, and still at the same time a letter has been sent saying we have no jurisdiction in the rit atter. The Chairman said the matter was left in the hands of the Chairman of the Committee and tee Clerk, and it was disadvantageous to discuss it when it was still in their hinds. By Mr. Gilmour' interference at the pre- sent moment he had prfju Heed the case in the opinion of the Chairman of the Committee, and he (Mr. Borron) thought the Council would be of the same oplinon. Mr. Gilmour : I will as the Cierk whether a letter has been written stating that we have no jurisdiction. Mr. Stone hoped the Chairm n would not allow the CLerk to answer. When these matters were left in the hands of the Chairman of the Committee it was very ir- regular and prejudicial to any case to have it brought f er ward in the manner this had been done.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour was attempting to address the Council when Mr. Simpson rose to a point of order. He remarked that Mr. Gilmour had been on his feet half a doze:. time 3. Mr. Gilmour : I don't see why Mr. Simpson should interfere. Til9 Chairman : Mr. Siinpson has a perfect right to applal to me on a point of order. Mr. Gilmour: But-- The Chairman : I must ask you to sit down. Mr. Gilmour : Do you refuse to give me an answer to my question P. Toe Chtirman : I ask you to sit down. Mr. Gilmour : If you express an opinion I shall follow you. The Chairman : I think it iq the wMo cf the Council that iho matter be left in the hands of the Chairman of th way Committee. Mr. Gilmour : I knovr as a matter of fact that it has been tak3n out of oar hands. The letter was then handed to the Chairman of the Highways Committee.</p>
        <p>This Was all the business of nubile interest. ST.. HELENS.</p>
        <p>FORTHIOMING LIBIRAL DEMONSTRATION AT Sr. HELENS, The S. Helens Liberal Association are completing, arraobements for a greit demonstration in the town towards the middle of Oorober. Toe gathering will be addrpssed by Lord Tweedmouth (the preUdent of the St. Helens Liberal Assaciation), Sir Herbert Naylor Ley- land (the new member of Parliament for Southport), and other gentlemen. TECHNICAL EDUCATION AT Sr. HELENS. • •</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the St. Helens Corporation technical education committee, the director of technical education reported that, with the four exceptions named, the same teachers for the technical education classes as last session had been re-engaged. Dr. Uneworth had been appointed teacher of the classes in physiology h.nd hy- giene, Mr. E. Quirk teacher in theoretical and practicsl plumbinz, Mr. J. Scnit'l and Mr. A. Clarke respectively in typewriting and commercial law. PERSISTENT CRUELTY TO A. W IFE AT HAYDOCIC.</p>
        <p>William Abbott, of Miners' C.ittige3, Haydoek, was summoned at the St. Helens Police Court on Monday by his wife, Margaret Ann, for persistent crdelty.—Mr. H. L.Riley appeared for the complainant, and Mr. E. W Swift for the defendant.—The parties had been married for about two years, and f r so ne time lived at the house of def-ndant's motnee. Complainant alleged that her husband had latterly ab ased her, and she wanted to live in a hone of her own. She had taken a separate house, but her husband would not live with her there. —The def-mce was a denial that the husband had been persistently eruel.—AfGer hearing the evidence, the beach grants I a separation order, and said the defen- dant must pay his wife 153. per week.</p>
