      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>GAS MANAGER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>GAS MANAGER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. S mann. in his monthly report, stated th it &amp;Ling the last month all at the Gas works had been going on in a satisfactory mantrir. At the last onnmittee meet• ing he WAS initructed to obtain thr3e of the new Weis- bach burners, and to report relative to their use for street-lighting purposes, but he hal not been able to do so as these new burners were not yet in the market, and as many of their present iocandeec sot burners re- qu rid new mantles pait n; on, h 3 WAS now waiting for perm,ssion to get a trestt supply of mantles, so that he might get the incandescent s^,reet lamps into proper working ort,r. The consumption of water durng, ho month was 6,430,000 gallons, against 6,882,000 gallms last year. THE GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The m flutes of this commit:ee stated that a letter, dated August 16 h. as read fr mu Mr. C. H. Marsh, of Newton Hall Farm, stating taat owing to a leakage in his water pipe welch he had xot discovered until the account was rendered, it amounted to the sum of £3 3s. as against an average charge per quarter of about 13s. Us nquestsd the Coujoil td make a rebate in th amount.—lt was resolv3d that a reduction of one third be a'l3wed from his ac:ount.—The Comm: t-ce alm re• solved that the tender of Messrs. The Clay Cross Com- pany, near Criesterfield, for abut 15 tons eacs of 3 incles and 4 inlhes diameter cast hot m tin pipes at £5 81. 41. and £5 74 61. per tm revaactively, be ac- cepte I; that the tender of Messrs. R. S. Clara and Co.. of St anhope-str,e-, Liverp-iol, of £1 is, 31. per ton of tar, and 6. 61. per ton of ammoniaca I liquor wnich may be unavoidably sent with the tar, during the year com- mencing September let, 1898, and August 31, 1899, be accepted. On the proposition of the Chairman the miaute4 were approved. SANITARY INSPECTOR'S AND SURVRYOR'S REPOTS. _</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Brierley reported to the Sanitary Committee that during the five weeks ended Wednesday, the 17ch inst. , the nightsoil s'alf removed 193 loads of pails, 104 hale from ashpits arid 245 loath if dry rubbish, total 542 loads, at a cost of £74 19so He was pleaslci to b 3 able to report toat thev were frill cl-ar of all infectious diseases. Since his lat report 69 notices had been sarved on owners to abate nuisances. Toe premises used as a slaughter- how°, etc., at the rear of the " Eland and Banner,' otT Hign-street, Newton, did nit Come up to the present requirements—it was not kept in the omditioo it ought to os and it was too near dwellingrh nose% HI had gest Id to the Brewery Company t abandon it, use as a slauthter-house entirely. In his report to the Highways Committee Mr. Bderley stated that during the past month the workmen bad painted the woodwork and spouts at the central se vage works, and Busied the sewers a': the higher levels. Toe thurvierstorm caused flooding below Emmett's Brow, Wargrave Railway Bridge, arid Willow 1.3a/di at S3uthivorch-road, Newton, and thee runt of the water load gutted about 400 lin.at yards of the channel down the Common-road Hr w, fir which 50 tons of slag would be necessary to restore the surface. Th 3 roadways in toe other cases had been restored, and plans ware in preparation for living down a storm overfl w 'ewer from VVargrave-road Bridge to the wt qcourse through the Mesnes. The sett pavement in Earle-stree4laoging in Cross-lane and Crow-lane had been repaired. Barrow-lane had been repaired with broken stone, and the general scavenging of the streets and roads had receicel fine attention.</p>
        <p>....- ........,... ....,..,, .„....... ___ .... THE HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE. Th's commit es rec anmende i that the tender of Mr. Jamet Sankey, of High-street, Newton-le-Willows for ' one ne , tank cart for the Sanitary Depart- ment for the sum of £27 103 be accepted.— An application for an advance of wages from the woi k- men in the Sanitary Department was referred to the sub-committee appointed to deal with a similar question at the last meeting. A lett T. dated August 10m, was read from Mr, T. HinTey,of Leigh, stating that owing to an error in his calculatons, he desired to amend his tender for the making of Alpine,Peel, and Nelson-streets from £962 11s. 101. to £1.033 18s. 61, whereupon it was resolved that the Clerk inform Mr. Flindley that the committee cannot se their way to allow him to amend his tender, and failing to his agreeing t 3 proceed with the work according to his original tender, that the offer 05 Mr. F. T. Bennie, of Warrington, for the making of A'pine Peal, and Nelson-streets, for the sum of £989 164 ld., be accep'.ei. Applications for a holiday for 14 days and 9 days were acceded to—to the toll collector and the road foreman respectively. A letter, dend August 22nd, was road from Mr. G. W. Coombs, of 42, Bridge-street, E trlestown, complaining of a smoke nuis- ance from Mr. G. Ronayne's bunaglow. The Clerk was directed to reply stating that the Council had no juris- diction in the matter. A latter, dated August 22 id,was read from Mr. R. Eaton, of St. Helens-road, Newton-Is. Willows,complaining that Mr.H. St trkie was connectmg the drains of thrre new houses, being built by him in Lyme-street, witnout his (Mr. Eat m's) consent. The ma.'ter was left in the Is tnds of the Chaitman and the Clerk. A letter,dated August 22nd, was also r3ad from Mr. A. J.L tne,of W trgrave-road, complaining of the in- efficiency of the drainage aid a consequent flooding of his houses in Crow-lane. The matter was referred to the Surveyor,with instructions to let the matter have his at- tention. It was ordered that the Clerk write to Mr. W: Gilb,rt, farmer, of Ashton-nal, calling his attention to complaints having been made as to the smell arising from the nightsoil depot off Vista-road, and re- questing him to adhere to the terms of his agreement. It was J.. solved that the Clerk be instructed to write to the Urban District Council of Haydock. and to ;tate that owing to their permittiqg tc.i ill or flop secira,e in an unpurified state into Dean Dam within their dierie, there was a C trieciaeut pollution of R tkedane Brook, t nll3O 'Which stream Data Dam flows, and from thene3 linto Newton Lake—both with% this diStric)—arld that, unless steps were taken within two Calendar months to abate the pollution complained of, legal proceedings Iwould be taken to enforce the provisions of the Rivers Pollution Prevention Acts. Letters d tted August 11th and 18th were read from Mr. Pard sy, and one dated August 19h from the Golborne D strict Canned, all , hiving reference to the pollution of Newton Lake. ; GENERAL PURPO3E3 COMMIT TEE =THE SUG.</p>
