      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>GES fEri INCREAzi OF MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>GES fEri INCREAzi OF MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee stated that a letter, dated August 11th was read from Mr. H. Greenall, clerk to the magistrates, stating that the appointment of ad- ditional magistra`es had always had the careful and prom, t consideration of the acting magi.trates 'rhea necebsity arose. A letter dated 27th July. WAS read from the Local Government Board, stating that, subject -to the production of proper voucherj to the District Auditor, they sanction the expenditare incurre I by the Urbsn District Council of Newtoa-in-Makerfisld on the occasion of the visit of His Royal Highness the Prince of , Wales to this distric: in May last.—lt was resolved that ' the application of Mr. Gorse for a fortnight's holiday from August 27th be accede] to. A letter, dated 22nd August, was read from Mr. J. Sutcliffr, of Bridge-street, Earlestowo, r ,questing to be allowed Co take down a wall in the room rental by him over the shops belonging to the Council on the market ground, and he would bear *the expense incurred thereby. It was resolved that Mr. Sutcliffe's application be granted subject to the removal of the wall not interfering with the s'ractural stability 02 the rremise 4 and to his depositing a sum of mosey to rebuild same should it be necessary. The Chairman moved and'Air. Simpson second sd the confirmation of the minutes. Mr. gihlrlir nifil 01 wished to refer to the letter which Iha i 13• Jen received foul the Clerk to the magistrates. Re contendel that it wail no ati answer to the letter atat by the council. The council's C6iiiildlilloiiVoul tb4 mernodr9 would reutember,was distinctly sent to Mr. T. J. Gillespie, tile thairmati e the bench; with a view to the opinions of the Council being placed before the Ch 'ln' cellor of the Duchy of the CloUnty. On bikini' Into the Mat! er he found there was a IledeSsity for additional dagistratrs notwithstanding what the Clerk to the Bench might ear. Three magistrates had needy died, wiz.. Colonel McCorquodsle, Mr. Pear Sod, and Mr. Mitchell, of Golturne. He also found that others had left th 3 di.ltrict, including Messrs Alec McOorquo- dale, Norman MoCsrquochle, Grimk6 Drayton, W. F. Gooch. adff S. Br own. There were really Mae Vacancies oh the Bench, and he contended there was A necessity for addition it magistratf a. The Chairman of their Coatiacil (Mr. Barron) lived at Gol- borne and was away at the week-end and ths conse- quence of the deuth of magistrates was that the police- inn in Esrlestown and nwtoa were running from Pillar t) post and journeying to Vitarriagton in order to get summonses signed. He ' Wasted that the m Itter ' would be brought before the Chancellor of the Dochy of the County. He thought M. Gillespie would have taken some further action. He did not think the letter received mai a Droper answer to- HA cbmrouninstion of the C ,unln. Ta, r s w.re plenty ot men in the neigh- bourhood who would do ample patio() to the Bench if appointed.</p>
        <p>Tbe Chairman said Mr. Gilmour tuivd male one or two speeches on the subj.ct on previous ocbasiors: They had mede certsin rspreaentations to' those who were the recognised parties in the matters arid they hal therefcrs dole thir duty. It rested wlth others to act if there was any tendency to remedy thd present condi.' tions. With reference to the remark of,Mr.,, Gilmour that he (Mr. Bon) resided at Golbornahe nitht'state' Ora Golhorne was part of the Newton Pal* SesiOnAl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>SMART ARREST RE Sr. HELENS DETECTIVES.</head>
        <p>SMART ARREST RE Sr. HELENS DETECTIVES.</p>
        <p>The police authorities at St. Helens have effected the arrest of a,n individual aliened to have been concerned in ole of a series of burglaries in the Leigh district. Last week the Polio received from the Leigh author'. ties the description of a man" wanted " for pawning a portion of the proceeds of a burglary at Leigh Vicarage, and Detective-sergeant Strong and Detective nolding, suspecting that the description agreed with the appear- ance of an individual within their knowledge, 'visited Scholes-lane, Thstt4 NW:hes silhilth of the borough, and I arrested %WWI WWait,a siOnemaion, on suspicion of Len t p.rion mantel, Williams was subsequently identified by the pawnbroker as the man who had offered the artiees in pledge, and he was handed over to the custody of the Leigh police. A. CONSCIENTIOLTS OBJECTOR AT Sr. HELENS. '</p>
        <p>Oa M naday afternoon, at St. Helens- TOW-Eia.ll, Mr Alfred Henry Thomas Clarke. of 137 Speakman-road, electrician to the St. Helens Corporateon.appliel to Mr. J. Forster and other nalgistrates to be exempted under the new Vacrination Act from the vancinat'on of his child.—The Chairman (Mr.Forstsr) asked if he had any experience of vaccination, as to its virtues or otherwise. —Mr. Clarke replied that he had not, but he had very strong views upon the qfsestion.—Mr. Droaa,goole, J.P., said he did not think daft it was necessary for Mr. Cfarke to answer those questions.—The clsrk (Mr. B. Brewis) said that if the bench were satisfied that the man had conscientious views upon the question they were obliged to grant him exeMptio Clarke then form- ally declared that he had conscientious objections to vaccination,and the exemption cerlificate was granted</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>DANGERgOS PLAY BY COLLIERS.</head>
        <p>DANGERgOS PLAY BY COLLIERS.</p>
        <p>_ ------- At St. Helens Pollee Court on Monday Dominic Doyle, of Owen's-row, Rainhill, was summoned by :he Sutton Heath and Loa Green Coilieries Company for a brealh of the special rules under the Coal Mines Regulation Act, by jumping on an endless haulage rope in the mine, el as to enlaager his life.—Mr. J. 0. Swif c, who prose. euted, stated that 03 the 6th ult. the defendant, appar- ently in playfulness, tAr hold of an endless haulage cope and allowad himself to be drawn alnalst to the bot- tom of the shaft. Oa the way one of his hands was crushed by a pulley, which had necessitate 4 his absten- tlon from work. The firm had no desire to press the case against him, but it had been br3ught as a warning elf id ire r7s et r atiat Out eet, was slugitnliniiindriO;Sts.—John Burehall, It appased that defend tot had taken CCI: off a coal waggoa. i for a breach of the special rules by taking the brake brake off a cal 'waggon, and allowed it t 3 rua down a brow' In the min s while he had a ride upon it. In attempting. apply the brake afterwards his head was ereshcd.—De- feculent was fined 5s and costa.</p>
        <p>IPing AT COLLINS Gam. A seangorbat alernting fire occurred on Monday night at the premises in the rear of the Pear Tree Hotel, Col- lin's-green, St. Helens,b which much damage was cl.,nr. Th 3 farm and house ad. °in the Collin's-green Station on al the mn London and Ni orth-Western line btween Liver- pool and Man:theste- About a fortnight ago Mr.Nayior harvested his crap of oat, the produce of six Cheshire fictie, and place i the oatl in a large barn over the cart shod. In this been was alio placed the hay gathered lliOgrnht lsra tmkteeard C% C b r,12, out hall-past twelve on Weineea7 a the signalmen in the bax at Col. iin's-green Station,noticed that the barn was on fire, the filmes buretitits oft', vigorously from under the cart shed, He at owe alafnied M. Naylor. A strong wind *as blowing at the fie which carried Vie sparks and flames across the yard in ehe directift of the hotel,whiCh is only about ten yards avir'ay from the seas of the outbreak. Mr. Naylor at once, with lid help or a nuinter of men who had been previously staoding in frpf of the house.set Co work to prevent the taines spreading. They were un- abl ',however' t a prevent the roof. of a firgra foui-stallel stable takingilte,and the( riPrieeportion of tits stable, as well as the whole of the tiara and its contents, were de- stroyed. The damage is covered by insurave, and will annunt to aoout £2OO. Mi. Naylor states that he was round the yard abiut a quarter past twelve, and he is Strongly of opinion that the fliS has bein deliberately caused by some one. Consiyiering the position Warn- which the wind was blowing M. Niylor was decidedly (intonate in preventing the fire 'spreadifig to is house.'</p>
        <p>LICENSING SESSIONS. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE BENCH AND Tim SERVING OF CHILDREN. The usual licenAing s'ssions for the borough of St. Helens wora held at St. Helens on Tuesday, when the magistrates on the bench were the Mayor (Alderman R. Pilkington), the Deputy Mayor (Councillor H. M artin), Colonel W. W. Pilktogt m, Major J. W. M. Tear, Dr. Gaskell, Ald vman A. Sinclair, Alderman T. Cook, and Messrs.J. Burchall,J. B. Leach, B. A. Dromgoole,and C. Sharpies.</p>
        <p>Cnlel Constable Wood reported thtt shr.:e the last annual licensing sessions n 9 licensed victuallers had been procebied against. One beerseller—inn Live=e7, of the Bay Horse Inn. 39, Parr-street—was an the 20th May last, fined 103. and elsts for selling be,,r during prohibited hours. The number of persons surnmonel for drunkenness for the year ending 31st July lait 312, against 394 in 1897, 333 in 1896. and 330 in 1895. Toe persons apprehended for drunk tiness numbered 463; 1897, 485; 1896. 475; 1895. 510 ; making a total for toe pts ye3r nf 775,against 879 in 1897, 808 in 1896, and 840 in 1895. There are at prwent 146 public houses,l33 blerhouss, and six grocers, Sze., dealing in wines and spirits. Thee was only one new Lpplication, and that was by John Glover, of 49, Higher Parr-stre3t, chemist and druggiit, for an off wines and sweets license for premises at 14, Ormskiik-street..</p>
        <p>Chief Constable Woodeaddressino the magistrates,said —Last year a resolution was passed' by tin magistrates with reference to children under 13 years of age b sing supplied with liquor for consumption off the prem ;e3. Notice of that resolution was served upon every publican and beerseller in the town in September last, and during the twelve months I have only had three reports show- ing that children under 13 have been served,and in each of the three cases the licensee expressed his regret that it had occurred. Altogether, as far as I can ascertain, the resolution has been loyally complied with. The Mayor said that last year they had reports as to one or two licenses which were practically ohm. lete, and asked if there was anything of the I kind this year.—Chie"-constable Wood said that there was one mentioned last year—an off-license in B ildwin- street—and he believed nothing had been done in the matter since.—The Mayor : Are there any other lice Me that are practiacally not used 2--Chief-constable Wood said there was the Traveliers' Rest Inn, in Liverpool. street which was very little used.—Mr. E. W. Swift, solicitor, said he appeared for the owners and tenants in that case. He explained that shortly after the death of the late licensee the widow was ill, and she was not able to take out the necesvary transfer papers. For some little time she sold no beer at all, and that was-55 far as he knew—the only ground for the suggestion that it was not used.lt was a very old license—it was one of the 1869 licensed houses—Chief-constable Wood said the off- license in Baldwin-street to which he had referred was that in the name of Alfred Wilcock, 12, Baldwin-street, Last year he objected to its renewal on the ground that it had not been used for 18 months p-eviously, but the license was granted.—Mr. w Icock, stepped forward and said he was not aware any objection was to be made against its renewal this year. ED had not received any intimation at all. Mr. Wood was under a misapprehension with rega-d to the matter. His (ffr- Wilcock's) mother had the license at Sutton Brewery, and he (Mr. Wilcock) had it for tho bottling stores in Baldwin-street. It was only in April this year that they finally cleared up and gave up possession of the brewery at Sutton. Since that time he had regularly used the, !leers 3 in Baldwin-street for bottling and selling. The Mayor intimated that alt the cases which had hen mentioned would be considered at the adj mrned licensing sessions on the 29th September. The holders of temporary au'horities would have their temporary authority renewed that day, when their cases would also agasn come forward. The whole of the other licenses would be renewed. The magistrates, the Mayor added, were very glad to hear that the matter of the servirg of children had been so properly taken up by the holders of licenses, and as the bench of magistrates they desired to acknowledge and recognise what had been done. The annual licensing sessions for the bon ugh of St. He'ens were resumed on Wednesday when the magis- trates on the bench were the Mayor (Alderman R. Pilkington), Sir David Gamble, Aldermen A. Sinclair arid T. Cook, Dr. Gaskell, and Messrs. H. Martin and C. Sharpies. The only application before the court was one by John Glover, of 49, Higher Parr-street, chemist and druggist, for an off-license to sell wines and sweets at 14, 0-mskirk-treet. Mr. R. W. H. Thomas appeared for Mr. Glover, and explained that the license was re- quired in order that Mr. Glover might sell certain medica- ted foreign wines. At present there were only two shops in the town which bad such a license, and those were both grocers—Messrs. Lewis and Stringfellow.—ln reply to the bench, it was pointed out that the wines were not prepared by Mr. Glover, but were sealed preparations. The bench ultimately refused the application, on the ground that the applicant had failed to produce satis- factsry evidence of good character, and that the applicant, or the house in respect of which he applied, was not duly qualified as by law required. ST. HELENS (COUNTY). The annual licensing sessions for the districts of Bold, Eccleston (rural),Rainford and Windle (rural), compris- ing the St. Helens County Petty Sessional Division, were held at Sc. Helens Town Hall on Wednesday, before Colonel W. W. Pilkington (in the chair), Colonel R. Pilkington' Alderman Cook, Alderman Sinclair, and Messrs. J. Beecham and H. Martin.—Stmerintendent Barcendale reported that there were in the district 27 licensed victuallers, twelve beersellers. and two off-beer- sellers, making a total of 41. The population of the dis'rict was 8,143. being a license to every 198 persons. There had been an increase of 15 in the number of per- sons proceeded against for drunkenness compared with the previous year. One licensed victuailer had been fined for selling drink to a drunken man, and the license was endorsed, but the conviction was quashed on an appeal Ito the quarter sessions.—The Chairman asked if there were any licenses which were not being used.—Superin- teneent Baxendale replied that there were not in his district.—This concluded the business,vvhich was for- mal. All the old licenses were renewed. - FRODSHAM. •</p>
        <p>The annual licensing sessions for the Frodaham portion S. eight ipgehr ti nbte eenr nweesrdeaiyn, thehl r. divisionC harl es Reynoldsl7licensed tdui of the Eddisbury division of Cheshire was held on Wed- dent Johnson, in his annual report, stated that there an:e.—rs sellers, and two others who sold off the premises, giving a total of 27 licensed houses, or an average population of 245 to e toh license. During the year no license holder hal been proceeded against,this being the Case last vetr; while 50 persons had been procee led against and 49 con- victed, for drunkenness. Last year 33 persons were proceeded against, and all convicted, for drunkenness. The Superintendent also handed in the name of a licensee Who had been reported for serving children appar- ently under the age of thirteen years.—Mr. E. S. 1 Giles, Chester, applied on behalf of Samuel Davies tenant of the Railway Inn, ileleby, for a seven days' license instead Pf the Sin days licetille now attached to the inn.—Mr. W. Chtirton,-Chester, appeared on behalf of the Chief Constable and local religious holies to oppose the granting of the licence.--Mr. Gape said the house was once a beerhouse. Then a spirit licence was added to it, and afterwards a seven days Renee was granted. This license was only held for a short wriocit and then the owner and licensee had it altered to a FIX day,' license. Now they applied for an evtension of the premises and the old 7 days' licence. It was proposel to take down the place altogether, as they contended that the accomo- dation was inadeqqate, and build a modern house. The owners were Messrs. Greene'', Whitl-v, and Co.—Evi- dence was given by witnesses who said a seven days' license was needed, Th. cost of the propesad alterations would he £1,970 to £2,000.—0n behalf of the opposition Mr. Murton said the alterations were a bribe to induce the magistrates to grant the seven days' license. If the licence were not granted the alterations would not be carried out. Au application of this kind in regard to the same house was refused by the marristrates two years ago, and the people of the loeality did not see why they should suffer because the owners of the house wanted to improve their property.—Several retitions were handed in and evidence given by ministers of the local church and chapels, while the police said there was no need for further facilities for Sunday drinking in the place.—Ultimately the application was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WA B, IN O. TON!</head>
        <p>WA B, IN O. TON!</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT ON TUE WARRINGTON FOOTBALL FIELII. On Saturday afternoon a young man named 11s-,7litts residing in Ghs.street,tnet with an accident whilst he was witnessing the match between War- rington And Swinton from one of the stands and durinCf a crash he tell and fractured his right leg. Ile was removed to the Infirmary for attention. The Pootball dommittee have genermsly decided to compensate bira during Hs temporary disable- ment frotn following hie employment.</p>
        <p>FIRE At ME6SII€I. BISROg VIE Co.'s WORKS. On Monday night at ten minutes to eleven in- fornpation Ne-40 Overt at Heath-aids Fire &amp;lon that a lire had broken out at Messrs. iliehop and Co.'s vrorlrs; DitiNsteeet. Under thp directidd of Superintendent Turrittr, a number of men turned out with the steato fire &amp;gins Major, a reel care °and escape ladder. When fluty reached the works, the fire, which had Wen caused by the overheating of some bearings in the machirw rOom,had been put out, and their services were.therefore, not required. The damage was only slight.</p>
        <p>Henry' Olitoi, alias John Henry o'CW:in&amp; and Patrick Flannigan, who in Liverpool in 180 alma sm• tenced to penal servitude for life for treason felony, have been released from Portland Prison by ordet from the Home Office. Detective-Thspeetor John H. Laybourne, of Liverllobl, has been appointed Chief Constable of Chester, in succes- Ilion co Mr. G. LI Fenwick, who re3igned after thirty- five years' service. ' There were twenty-nine applicants for the post.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>HON. T. W. LEGH, ALP.</head>
        <p>THE HON. T. W. LEGH, ALP.</p>
        <p>A CHARACTER SKETCH. " M.A.P. "for the current week contains a slld6ds r: character sketch of Mr. T. W. Legh, .M.P., ua the title of "The young men of the House" It Ift, as follows :—" If you want an opposite type to of Flower, you will find it in the next young inaacor whom lam going to speak. Mr. Legh, memba.i one of the divisions of Lancashire, is a tYP* Englishman in appearance and in manner; the 0 is is fair, almost red, so is the complexion ; the fie:ni freckled, the manner dry and somewnat tame. as is the manner, to a certain extent so also is,!• man. Cool, resolute, firm of purpose, Mr. Le.gu not a man to be easily moved from any of his . victions. When he was standing for nis exist ency, the Compulsory Eight Hours went was strong among the miners, formed a large portion of the elect But while candidates, Tory as well as Liber other parts of the country, and especially of particular county, were giving in t 3 the der of the miners—or what were supp)sed to be demands—Mr. Legh stood firm, and would . nothing to do with an eight hours' law. in the House of Commons, he has been one of first revolters against the faddish policy of Government, and although he is not an orator, speaks in a cold, clear, ahnost hard voice. Oat language rough, dry, he yet manages to conveT,, ideas with clearness and cogency. He is clearlY young man who knows his own mind, and Wil° not easily to ba moved from his purpose, He drei as the typical Englishman, in rather sulyll colours. He is, unadorne 1, in short, one of th quiet, unobtrusive, yet determined fellows have distinguished themselves in almost all struggles of the Empire. He has the advantage some diplomatic experience. He knows mos the personalities of the Continent, and it is not prising that when people were making fancy : of the changes which wpald be reqaired by the r tirement of Mr. Curzon, his name was among 01°' mentioned for the vacant Under-SecrstarygliP Foreign Affairs."</p>
