        <p>rugurres naa yet guinea tne notate, tnougn in one corner of the Duke's room her quick eyes had seen a rope-ladder. She opened the eating-room door, and walked boldly in. Gaspard sat at the table, eyeing Madame Tarbes with scowling defiance.. He started as Jeanne came in; his jaw fell, he rose and made a respectful salute. She went up to him, smiling as she seated her- self on another bench, not far from the one the ruffian occupied. "Good evening, "-she said; "you have, I trust, brought me news ; have you not a message fro's yourleader ? Stay, Madame Tarbes; where go you V so fast ?" Then, as the terrified woman, heedless of her question, passed out at the door, and Gaspard rose to follow her, Jeanne nodded pleasantly at him. "Sit you down, honest Gaspard; I will follow the hostess, and bid her fetch my women. She shall then bring you a flask of wine, and you shall deliver your master's message." Gaspard had been drinking in the village, but Jeanne spoke so simply, and in so childlike a manner, that it might have deceived him even if he had not dulled his wits with liquor. He grinned, and told himself as she hurried from the room that the lady had gone to order wine.. "Pardieu I she is a beauty, and she knows how * man should be treated; I trust she will order good liquor. Her women forsooth! Anne is willing ta come at my whistle, she needs no persuasion. The wench is ripe and ready; she will give me the chance I want, and then a few kisses will purchase her tidings," he added with a fatuous smile. "Mille diables ! the mistress is almost as tempting as the maid. My master. has lucky chances with the sex." Jeanne hurried after Madame Tarbes, who moved more slowly than did the young feet winged with determination to save the Duke from capture. "Histl" The girl spoke between her teeth; then, as the hostess paused an instant to listen: "Warn them to hasten; then return to me in the eating room, and bring good wine with you. I will keep the fellow quiet as long as I can. This said, she went back to Gaspard. She shivered when he greeted her with a leer. 1 "Madame asked for my leader's message, but I fancy he will deliver it himself." "When do you expect your master to be here ? " Jean'st said calmly, "Pardieu as quickly as madame wishes. 110 will be here anon; maybe his words would lose flavour were Ito try and speak them. Caramba t as the Spaniards say, we have run the fox to earth this time!" Madame Tarbes came in with a flask .of Mal- voisie ; she looked solemn as she bent forward to place it before Gaspard, hart Jeanne was wrought up and desperate. "Let me have the wine, madame, since I shall pay for it. I mean to taste your Ilalvoisie, and E 0 shall you; we will all three drink a happy endia to my journey. I will drink to your health. Gaspard. I know, you are a judge of wine ; yeti shall tell me what you think of this." She bade the hostess fill the glasses, then, just touching her own glass with her lips, Jeanne said gaily: 'A happy ending to my journey ;" she bade the woman place the flask before the astonished trooper. He filled his glass to overflowing,' then, rising., he shouted: "Au bonheur de Madame la Comtesse,' and tossed off the liquor at a draught. He was going to add some coarse compliment, but looking at her he met her strong, fearless glance, and he sat down again without a word. "Drink freely, friend," Jeanne said to him; "the wine is good, and the long ride must have made you thirsty." Then to Madame Tarbes, turning her back so completely on Gaspard that he could not see her significant glances: "You did my' errand upstairs, madame, with my women?" She spoke louder than usual, that the man at the table might hear. The hostess answered the girl's eyes, her own shewed a deeper meaning than her words did. "I went to their chamber, znadame, but they had left it. I did not linger, because madame had com- manded the wine, but I can, if she now pleases, seek her women in madame's chamber." Gaspard had already drained the contents of the bottle. "Yes, yes; send the wenches here, or one of them. Aferbleiel I have emptied the flask befor,e they came to help me; saving your noble presence, —he looked drunkenly at Jeanne—"'tis but dr, work to talk with a thirsty throat." Jeanne shuddered as she met the man's bOll, glance axed on her, but the thosrght of Bouern danger nerved her to go on. ulfe says sooth, mistress," she said rebulungll to the hostess, who, shocked by the freedman the lady allowed to this drunken soldier, looked at her with alarm and a certain distrust. "You must bring another flask—stay, wine is thin drink for fighting men; bring him a flagon of ale, the strongest you have.' Then, as the woman quitted the chamberSeanno followed her to-the door. "Hasten," she whispered, "do not let the grass grow under your feet." It seemed to Gaspard that the Countess scolded, for he said quite audibly what he doubtless meant for an aside: "Who would have thought the beauty had such a spirit? My lord may need to slit her tongue before he has done with her. Mort de diabld Sit is too slender for my taste. Sit ye down, lady,- he called out; "I mean kindly by you and your wenches—that is, the young ones! May the foul fiend fly away with all ill-favoured and old women!" - While Jeanne hesitated, unwilling to put herself within his reach, the hostess had returned, and she set the flagon of ale and a tall mug beside the trooper. She had by this time guessed the lady's meaning, and she poured out a brimming mug of the liquor with hopeful expectation. "Ho, ho!" Gaspard cried, "hero is Death tO all traitors! ' " . Jeanne stayed till he had drained the mug. Silo felt sure from the change she saw in the hostess that , the fugitives had escaped; and saying she would go to find her women, she hastily rose and left the chamber. Her courage fled when she gained the ' stairs; she hardly knew how she reached the upper floor. She barred her chamber door against in- truders, and fell exhausted on the bed. She listened; only now and then a dull sound reached her. All at once she heard a voice raised in anger; then a firm armed heel on the stairs. Jeanne's heart beat faster; she knew that this must be Laval, and that he had discovered the drunkea condition of his follower. (To be continued.)</p>
